Guide.content._register("w10", {"id":"w10","order":10,"region":"agents","title":"Agent and Multi-Agent Patterns","short":"Agent patterns","tagline":"Choose the loop, the crew, the context and the memory on purpose.","story":"One model call is no longer enough: the question needs several steps, some tools and maybe several specialists. Every step you add costs tokens and latency and adds a new way to fail. Here you learn to add autonomy only when it earns its cost, and to keep the context, the memory and the run alive long enough to finish.","oneLiner":"Start at the lowest rung of autonomy that works, then climb only when a dial you can name forces you to.","youWillLearn":["Place a task on the autonomy dial: single call, workflow, agent or multi-agent","Pick among chaining, routing, parallelization, orchestrator-workers and evaluator-optimizer by task structure and risk","Run ReAct, plan-and-execute and reflection loops with hard brakes on steps, tokens and repeated calls","Compare supervisor, handoff, group chat and Magentic topologies, and weigh the single versus multi-agent evidence","Budget a context window with clearing, compaction, offloading and sub-agent isolation, and explain context rot","Design memory with explicit write and read policies and defend it against poisoning and privacy failures","Keep long-running agents alive with checkpoints, idempotency keys, human approval waits and budgets"],"minutes":125,"prereqs":["w08","w09"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w10-fc-001","front":"What separates a **workflow** from an **agent** in Anthropic's definition?","back":"Workflows orchestrate LLMs and tools through predefined code paths. In an agent the LLM dynamically directs its own process and tool use. Both are agentic systems.","tags":["patterns"]},{"id":"w10-fc-002","front":"State Microsoft's rule for choosing a rung of the complexity ladder.","back":"Use the lowest level of complexity that reliably meets your requirements: direct model call, then single agent with tools, then multiagent orchestration.","tags":["patterns"]},{"id":"w10-fc-003","front":"Name the five workflow patterns from Anthropic's guide.","back":"Prompt chaining, routing, parallelization (sectioning and voting), orchestrator-workers and evaluator-optimizer, all built on an augmented LLM.","tags":["patterns"]},{"id":"w10-fc-004","front":"When does evaluator-optimizer pay off?","back":"When clear evaluation criteria exist and revision measurably helps. Without criteria or an external signal the critic drifts and the loop burns tokens.","tags":["patterns","loops"]},{"id":"w10-fc-005","front":"ReAct in one line, and its reported gain?","back":"Interleave reasoning traces with actions and observations. On ALFWorld and WebShop it beat imitation and RL baselines by an absolute 34% and 10% success rate.","tags":["loops"]},{"id":"w10-fc-006","front":"What does **ReWOO** change compared with ReAct?","back":"It plans the tool calls first and runs them without interleaved reasoning. The paper reports 5x token efficiency and 4% higher accuracy on HotpotQA.","tags":["loops"]},{"id":"w10-fc-007","front":"Why does a naive agent loop grow quadratically in steps?","back":"Each step re-reads the prefix plus all earlier observations. Total input is n times the prefix plus observation size times n(n-1)/2, unless you cache, clear or compact.","tags":["loops","context"]},{"id":"w10-fc-008","front":"Which loop brakes must live outside the model?","back":"Step cap, token or cost budget, wall-clock limit and repeated-call detection, each enforced by the harness and each ending the run with an explicit stop reason.","tags":["loops"]},{"id":"w10-fc-009","front":"Handoff versus agent-as-tool: who owns the conversation afterwards?","back":"Handoff: the receiving agent takes ownership and the full context. Agent-as-tool: the caller keeps ownership and regains control when the tool returns.","tags":["topology"]},{"id":"w10-fc-010","front":"What token multipliers did Anthropic report for agents and multi-agent systems?","back":"About 4x the tokens of chat for agents and about 15x for multi-agent systems, vendor-reported on its own research traffic. Value per task must justify that cost.","tags":["topology","cost"]},{"id":"w10-fc-011","front":"How did Cognition's view move between 2025-06-12 and 2026-04-22?","back":"2025: share full traces, actions carry implicit decisions, prefer one single-threaded agent. 2026: parallel writers still fail, but several agents can contribute while writes stay single-threaded.","tags":["topology"]},{"id":"w10-fc-012","front":"What is **MAST**?","back":"A taxonomy of 14 multi-agent failure modes in 3 categories: system design, inter-agent misalignment and task verification. It rests on 1600+ annotated traces from 7 frameworks.","tags":["topology","failure"]},{"id":"w10-fc-013","front":"What is **context rot**?","back":"Quality falls as input grows, unevenly and well below advertised windows. In NoLiMa, 11 of 13 models claiming 128K or more fell under 50% of their short-context baseline at 32K.","tags":["context"]},{"id":"w10-fc-014","front":"Which compaction step does Anthropic call one of the safest and lightest?","back":"Tool result clearing: drop stale tool outputs deep in history while keeping the call record and a restorable pointer such as a path or URL.","tags":["context"]},{"id":"w10-fc-015","front":"Why does a timestamp at the start of a system prompt hurt an agent?","back":"Prompt caches match an identical prefix. A changing first token invalidates everything after it. Keep prefixes stable, context append-only and serialisation deterministic.","tags":["context","caching"]},{"id":"w10-fc-016","front":"Which memory types does the CoALA framework distinguish?","back":"Working memory, plus long-term procedural, semantic and episodic memory.","tags":["memory"]},{"id":"w10-fc-017","front":"Foundry Agent Service memory: types, status and key limits as of 2026-10-13?","back":"Preview. User profile, chat summary and procedural memory via extraction, consolidation and retrieval. 100 scopes per store, 10,000 memories per scope, no VNet integration.","tags":["memory"]},{"id":"w10-fc-018","front":"How can an attacker poison agent memory without write access?","back":"MINJA plants malicious records through ordinary queries and outputs. AgentPoison backdoors long-term memory or RAG stores at a poison rate under 0.1%. Treat every write as untrusted.","tags":["memory","security"]},{"id":"w10-fc-019","front":"Checkpoint versus durable execution?","back":"A checkpoint lets a run resume. Side effects still need idempotency keys or compensating steps, and replay needs deterministic orchestration code.","tags":["durable"]},{"id":"w10-fc-020","front":"What does a Microsoft Agent Framework workflow checkpoint capture?","back":"At the end of each superstep: executor state, pending messages, pending requests and responses, and shared state.","tags":["durable"]},{"id":"w10-fc-021","front":"Which rules govern an idempotency key for a retried tool call?","back":"Generate it once per logical operation, client side, and reuse it on every retry. Stripe allows up to 255 characters and may prune v1 keys after 24 hours.","tags":["durable"]},{"id":"w10-t01-fc1","front":"Workflow vs agent in one line each?","back":"Workflow: LLMs and tools run through predefined code paths. Agent: the LLM dynamically directs its own process and tool use [[r1]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t01"},{"id":"w10-t01-fc2","front":"What does Microsoft's rule for complexity say?","back":"Use the lowest level of complexity that reliably meets your requirements. A single agent with tools is often the right enterprise default [[r9]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t01"},{"id":"w10-t01-fc3","front":"What do agents and multi-agent systems cost in tokens, per Anthropic?","back":"About 4x chat for agents and about 15x for multi-agent systems, vendor-reported in Anthropic's own traffic [[r2]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t01"},{"id":"w10-t02-fc1","front":"What does prompt chaining trade for accuracy?","back":"Latency. Each call becomes an easier task, but total time is the sum of the steps plus gates [[r1]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t02"},{"id":"w10-t02-fc2","front":"What is another name for prompt chaining in Azure's pattern guide?","back":"Sequential orchestration, also called a pipeline or linear delegation. The workflow, not an agent, picks the next step [[r9]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t02"},{"id":"w10-t02-fc3","front":"Why can a five-step chain at 95 percent per step still be unreliable?","back":"0.95^5 is about 77 percent under independence. Gates between steps stop errors from compounding.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t02"},{"id":"w10-t03-fc1","front":"What two examples does Anthropic give for routing?","back":"Different customer-service query types down different paths, and easy questions to a smaller model with hard ones to a larger model [[r1]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t03"},{"id":"w10-t03-fc2","front":"What three things should every router have?","back":"A confidence threshold, a safe fallback path and a route log to measure per-route accuracy.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t03"},{"id":"w10-t03-fc3","front":"Router versus handoff?","back":"A router classifies once and steps aside. Handoff lets agents transfer control during the task and can loop [[r9]][[r11]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t03"},{"id":"w10-t04-fc1","front":"Name the two variations of parallelization.","back":"Sectioning (independent subtasks at once) and voting (the same task run several times for diverse outputs) [[r1]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t04"},{"id":"w10-t04-fc2","front":"Formula for fan-out latency?","back":"max(branch latency) plus the aggregation step. Cost is the sum of all branches plus aggregation.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t04"},{"id":"w10-t04-fc3","front":"Why might voting add cost but no accuracy?","back":"Votes from the same model and prompt share blind spots, so errors correlate. Diversify prompts, models or evidence and check that votes ever disagree.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t04"},{"id":"w10-t05-fc1","front":"What does the orchestrator do that a parallel workflow does not?","back":"It decides the subtasks at run time from the input, delegates them and synthesises the results [[r1]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t05"},{"id":"w10-t05-fc2","front":"What should a worker brief contain?","back":"The objective, output format, tools and sources to use, and the boundary of the task, so workers do not duplicate or leave gaps.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t05"},{"id":"w10-t05-fc3","front":"Anthropic's effort rules for subagent counts?","back":"Simple fact-finding: 1 agent with 3 to 10 tool calls. Direct comparison: 2 to 4 subagents with 10 to 15 calls. Complex research: more than 10 subagents [[r2]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t05"},{"id":"w10-t06-fc1","front":"Other names for evaluator-optimizer in Microsoft's guide?","back":"Maker-checker, generator-verifier, critic loops and reflection loops. It needs clear acceptance criteria and an iteration cap with a fallback [[r9]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t06"},{"id":"w10-t06-fc2","front":"When does Anthropic say this pattern works well?","back":"When evaluation criteria are clear and iterative refinement gives measurable value [[r1]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t06"},{"id":"w10-t06-fc3","front":"What helped Cognition's review loop most?","back":"A reviewer that shares no context with the coder. The reported average was 2 bugs per PR, about 58 percent severe, vendor-reported [[r4]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t06"},{"id":"w10-t07-fc1","front":"What does ReAct interleave?","back":"Reasoning traces and actions. The model thinks, calls a tool, reads the observation and repeats [[r16]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t07"},{"id":"w10-t07-fc2","front":"Why do ReAct input tokens grow so fast?","back":"Each step resends the history, so total input grows roughly quadratically with steps unless results are trimmed or cached.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t07"},{"id":"w10-t07-fc3","front":"Who owns the brakes in a ReAct agent?","back":"The harness: step cap, token budget, deadline and repeat detection, returning an explicit stop reason. A prompt asking the model to stop is not a brake.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t07"},{"id":"w10-t08-fc1","front":"What is the main weakness of plan-and-execute?","back":"Brittleness. A plan written before the first observation can rest on wrong assumptions, so you need replanning and short plans for uncertain tasks.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t08"},{"id":"w10-t08-fc2","front":"What did ReWOO report?","back":"5x token efficiency and 4 percent higher accuracy on HotpotQA versus interleaved reasoning, by decoupling reasoning from tool observations [[r18]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t08"},{"id":"w10-t08-fc3","front":"Why is a plan useful beyond cost?","back":"It is an artifact you can show, store, diff and approve before side effects run.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t08"},{"id":"w10-t09-fc1","front":"What does Reflexion store, and what does it not do?","back":"It stores verbal reflections in an episodic memory buffer. It does no weight updates. Paper: 91 percent pass@1 on HumanEval vs 80 percent for the GPT-4 baseline [[r17]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t09"},{"id":"w10-t09-fc2","front":"When does self-critique fail?","back":"When the model must find its own reasoning errors with no external feedback. Performance can even degrade [[tr7]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t09"},{"id":"w10-t09-fc3","front":"What makes a reviewer more useful?","back":"Independence: a different prompt or model, and fresh context that does not share the generator's assumptions [[r4]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t09"},{"id":"w10-t10-fc1","front":"What does LangGraph's recursion limit count, and what is the default?","back":"Maximum super-steps per run, raising GraphRecursionError at the limit. The default is 1000 steps from version 1.0.6 [[tr4]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t10"},{"id":"w10-t10-fc2","front":"What does the OpenAI Agents SDK do at max_turns?","back":"It raises MaxTurnsExceeded. Passing max_turns=None disables the limit [[tr3]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t10"},{"id":"w10-t10-fc3","front":"What should a brake return besides stopping?","back":"A typed stop reason and the best partial result, so policy can retry, escalate or warn.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t10"},{"id":"w10-t10-fc4","front":"Why count input tokens in a budget?","back":"A naive loop resends the whole history each step, so input tokens usually dominate and grow roughly quadratically.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t10"},{"id":"w10-t11-fc1","front":"OpenAI's two signals to split a single agent?","back":"Prompts with many if-then-else branches, and tool selection that still fails after clearer tool descriptions. Tool overload depends on overlap [[r19]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t11"},{"id":"w10-t11-fc2","front":"Cognition's updated view in 2026-04-22?","back":"Parallel writers still fail. Multiple agents contributing intelligence works when writes stay single-threaded and a clean-context reviewer checks [[r4]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t11"},{"id":"w10-t11-fc3","front":"What is the token cost of each rung, per Anthropic?","back":"About 4x chat for an agent and about 15x for a multi-agent system, vendor-reported [[r2]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t11"},{"id":"w10-t12-fc1","front":"Supervisor versus handoff in one line?","back":"Supervisor: specialists are tools and control returns to the primary agent. Handoff: the receiving agent takes full ownership [[r11]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t12"},{"id":"w10-t12-fc2","front":"What should a specialist return to a supervisor?","back":"A status, a short structured summary and artifact references, so the hub keeps its context small and can open details on demand.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t12"},{"id":"w10-t12-fc3","front":"What is the supervisor's main bottleneck?","back":"All routing and results pass through one context and one model hop per delegation, so overload and latency grow with the number of specialists [[r32]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t12"},{"id":"w10-t13-fc1","front":"What does the receiving agent get in an Agent Framework handoff?","back":"Full ownership of the task and the full context. It is a mesh topology with no central orchestrator [[r11]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t13"},{"id":"w10-t13-fc2","front":"Name four handoff options in the OpenAI Agents SDK.","back":"tool_name_override, on_handoff, input_type and input_filter, plus is_enabled, tool_description_override and nest_handoff_history [[r20]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t13"},{"id":"w10-t13-fc3","front":"What is OpenAI Swarm's status?","back":"Experimental and educational, replaced by the OpenAI Agents SDK, per its README checked 2026-10-13 [[tr17]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t13"},{"id":"w10-t14-fc1","front":"How does Agent Framework group chat choose the next speaker?","back":"An orchestrator in a star topology uses round-robin, prompt-based or custom selection. All agents see the full conversation history [[tr1]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t14"},{"id":"w10-t14-fc2","front":"Name the stop controls in Agent Framework group chat.","back":"MaximumIterationCount on the C# round-robin manager, and termination_condition and max_rounds in the Python builder [[tr1]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t14"},{"id":"w10-t14-fc3","front":"Why does group chat cost grow faster than its message count?","back":"Every agent rereads the whole transcript before each reply, so input tokens grow roughly quadratically with turns.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t14"},{"id":"w10-t15-fc1","front":"What does the Magentic manager keep, and what does it do on a stall?","back":"A task ledger and a progress record. After consecutive stalls it replans, and too many plan resets end the run [[r10]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t15"},{"id":"w10-t15-fc2","front":"Which sample limits does the Agent Framework Magentic doc show?","back":"max_round_count=10, max_stall_count=3 and max_reset_count=2 in Python, with matching WithMaxRounds, WithMaxStalls and WithMaxResets in C# [[r10]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t15"},{"id":"w10-t15-fc3","front":"What is Magentic's status caveat, checked 2026-10-13?","back":"Untested outside the original Magentic-One design. C# types are experimental, with diagnostic MAAIW001 suppressed in samples [[r10]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t15"},{"id":"w10-t16-fc1","front":"What are MAST's three categories?","back":"System design issues, inter-agent misalignment and task verification, covering 14 failure modes [[r5]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t16"},{"id":"w10-t16-fc2","front":"What data underlies MAST?","back":"A taxonomy built from 150 traces (kappa 0.88), and MAST-Data with 1,600+ annotated traces across 7 frameworks, plus an LLM-as-a-judge annotator [[r5]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t16"},{"id":"w10-t16-fc3","front":"Which failure is most actionable to fix first in many crews?","back":"Verification. Use an independent verifier with a clean context and external checks, since a same-context reviewer tends to approve [[r5]][[r4]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t16"},{"id":"w10-t17-fc1","front":"What is the attention budget idea?","back":"Context is finite with diminishing returns. Aim for the smallest set of high-signal tokens for the next step [[r6]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t17"},{"id":"w10-t17-fc2","front":"Manus's cache rules?","back":"Stable prefix (no leading timestamp), append-only context, deterministic serialisation, and mask tools instead of removing them [[r7]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t17"},{"id":"w10-t17-fc3","front":"Where does the model recall best in long input?","back":"At the start and end of the input, with the lowest recall in the middle (Lost in the Middle) [[r39]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t17"},{"id":"w10-t18-fc1","front":"What did RULER find about 32K context?","back":"Of 17 models tested, only about half kept satisfactory performance at 32K despite claiming 32K or more [[r33]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t18"},{"id":"w10-t18-fc2","front":"Which Claude API settings control tool result clearing?","back":"Context editing (beta, header context-management-2025-06-27) with strategy clear_tool_uses_20250919, and clear_thinking_20251015 for thinking blocks [[r30]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t18"},{"id":"w10-t18-fc3","front":"Why keep tool call and result together when trimming?","back":"Dropping one half causes API errors. Agent Framework treats them as one atomic message group [[tr14]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t18"},{"id":"w10-t19-fc1","front":"CoALA's long-term memory types?","back":"Procedural, semantic and episodic, alongside working memory [[r28]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t19"},{"id":"w10-t19-fc2","front":"Foundry memory types, status and one limit?","back":"Preview as of 2026-10-13: user profile, chat summary and procedural. Limits include 100 scopes per store and no VNet support [[r13]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t19"},{"id":"w10-t19-fc3","front":"LangGraph checkpointer versus store?","back":"A checkpointer saves thread-scoped state. A store holds cross-thread memory [[r43]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t19"},{"id":"w10-t20-fc1","front":"What do AgentPoison's numbers show?","back":"Average attack success above 80 percent at a poison rate below 0.1 percent, with under 1 percent impact on benign use [[r24]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t20"},{"id":"w10-t20-fc2","front":"Four parts of a memory policy?","back":"Write policy, read policy (scope, relevance, provenance), expiry and deletion that reaches derived summaries and embeddings.","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t20"},{"id":"w10-t20-fc3","front":"Foundry memory limits, preview as of 2026-10-13?","back":"100 scopes per store, 10,000 memories per scope, 1,000 search and 1,000 update requests per minute, no VNet support [[r13]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t20"},{"id":"w10-t21-fc1","front":"Checkpoint versus durable execution?","back":"A checkpoint lets a run resume. Durable execution adds retries, timers and deterministic replay, with side effects in activities [[r42]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t21"},{"id":"w10-t21-fc2","front":"What does LangGraph's interrupt() require?","back":"A checkpointer and a thread_id. It waits indefinitely until resumed with Command. InMemorySaver loses state on restart [[r44]][[r43]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t21"},{"id":"w10-t21-fc3","front":"Why do durable human-in-the-loop waits cost little?","back":"They consume no compute and no model tokens while waiting. A 24-hour approval costs seconds of execution on Flex Consumption [[r12]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t21"},{"id":"w10-t22-fc1","front":"How does an Agent Framework tool approval work?","back":"Wrap the function in ApprovalRequiredAIFunction. The run completes with a response saying what input is needed, and the caller returns the decision in a new run [[tr13]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t22"},{"id":"w10-t22-fc2","front":"Stripe idempotency key rules?","back":"Client-generated, up to 255 characters, a retry returns the saved response, and v1 keys can be pruned after at least 24 hours [[r26]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t22"},{"id":"w10-t22-fc3","front":"What does the Compensating Transaction pattern do?","back":"When a later step of an eventually consistent operation fails, it undoes the steps already completed [[r27]].","tags":[],"topic":"w10-t22"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"Building effective agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Shared vocabulary: workflows versus agents and the five workflow patterns. Dated 2024-12-19; a later banner says the tooling landscape has changed.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r2","title":"How we built our multi-agent research system","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Best vendor-reported evidence on multi-agent gains, token cost and production lessons. Dated 2025-06-13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r3","title":"Don't Build Multi-Agents","url":"https://cognition.com/blog/dont-build-multi-agents","type":"blog","publisher":"Cognition","note":"The strongest counterargument, with two context principles. Dated 2025-06-12.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r4","title":"Multi-Agents: What's Actually Working","url":"https://cognition.com/blog/multi-agents-working","type":"blog","publisher":"Cognition","note":"Refined view: single-threaded writes and clean-context review. Dated 2026-04-22.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r5","title":"Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail?","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13657","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"The MAST taxonomy of 14 failure modes. Submitted 2025-03-17, revised 2025-10-26.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r6","title":"Effective context engineering for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Attention budget, compaction, notes, sub-agents and on-demand retrieval. Dated 2025-09-29.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r7","title":"Context Engineering for AI Agents: Lessons from Building Manus","url":"https://manus.im/blog/Context-Engineering-for-AI-Agents-Lessons-from-Building-Manus","type":"blog","publisher":"Manus","note":"KV-cache discipline, file system as memory and todo recitation. Dated 2025-07-18, vendor-reported.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r8","title":"Context Rot: How Increasing Input Tokens Impacts LLM Performance","url":"https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot","type":"blog","publisher":"Chroma","note":"Study of 18 models showing non-uniform degradation as input grows. July 2025.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r9","title":"AI agent orchestration patterns","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/ai-agent-design-patterns","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The complexity ladder and when to use or avoid each multiagent pattern.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r10","title":"Magentic orchestration (Microsoft Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/workflows/orchestrations/magentic","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"How the planning manager and task ledger work, and the experimental caveats for C#.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r11","title":"Handoff orchestration (Microsoft Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/workflows/orchestrations/handoff","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Mesh topology, ownership after handoff, and autonomous mode with a turn limit. The handoff-loop risk is in the Azure guide (r9).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r12","title":"Durable Extension for Microsoft Agent Framework","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/integrations/azure-functions","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Durable agents and human-in-the-loop waits that use no compute. The page states no GA status as of 2026-10-13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r13","title":"Memory in Microsoft Foundry Agent Service (preview)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/what-is-memory","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Managed memory types, phases, limits and security guidance. Preview as of 2026-10-13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r14","title":"Effective harnesses for long-running agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-harnesses-for-long-running-agents","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Progress files and incremental sessions across context windows. Dated 2025-11-26.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r15","title":"Scaling Managed Agents: decoupling the brain from the hands","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/managed-agents","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Session, harness and sandbox separation and recovery design. Dated 2026-04-08. The beta status and header come from the Managed Agents docs overview, not this post.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r16","title":"ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Origin of the interleaved reason-act loop.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r17","title":"Reflexion: Language Agents with Verbal Reinforcement Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11366","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Verbal reflections stored in an episodic buffer, no weight updates.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r18","title":"ReWOO: Decoupling Reasoning from Observations for Efficient Augmented Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18323","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Plan first, run tools without interleaved reasoning, with a reported 5x token saving.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r19","title":"A practical guide to building agents","url":"https://cdn.openai.com/business-guides-and-resources/a-practical-guide-to-building-agents.pdf","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"When to split agents, tool overload and the manager versus decentralized shapes. The PDF carries no date in extracted text.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r20","title":"OpenAI Agents SDK: handoffs","url":"https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/handoffs/","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Handoffs exposed as tools with a default name and filtering options.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r21","title":"Checkpoints (Microsoft Agent Framework workflows)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/workflows/checkpoints","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What a checkpoint captures and when it is written.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"NoLiMa: Long-Context Evaluation Beyond Literal Matching","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.05167","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"13 models claiming 128K or more degrade sharply by 32K.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r23","title":"Memory Injection Attacks on LLM Agents via Query-Only Interaction (MINJA)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03704","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Memory poisoning through ordinary queries, 2025-03.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r24","title":"AgentPoison: Red-teaming LLM Agents via Poisoning Memory or Knowledge Bases","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12784","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Backdoor attack on long-term memory or RAG stores, 2024-07.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r25","title":"Background mode (OpenAI API)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/background","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Asynchronous runs, polling, cancel and retention behaviour.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r26","title":"Idempotent requests (Stripe API)","url":"https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests","type":"doc","publisher":"Stripe","note":"Reference behaviour of idempotency keys: length, retry semantics and pruning.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r27","title":"Compensating Transaction pattern","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/patterns/compensating-transaction","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Undo completed steps when a later step of an eventually consistent operation fails. Page metadata updated_at is 2026-09-29; the visible 'Last updated on' line reads 2026-04-20.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r28","title":"Cognitive Architectures for Language Agents (CoALA)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.02427","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Working memory plus procedural, semantic and episodic long-term memory.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r29","title":"MemGPT: Towards LLMs as Operating Systems","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08560","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Virtual context management across memory tiers.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r30","title":"Context editing (Claude API)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-editing","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Tool result clearing and thinking clearing strategies. Beta as of 2026-10-13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r31","title":"Magentic-One: A Generalist Multi-Agent System for Solving Complex Tasks","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04468","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Orchestrator that plans, tracks progress and re-plans over specialised agents. Submitted 2024-11-07.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r32","title":"Multi-agent patterns (LangChain)","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/multi-agent","type":"doc","publisher":"LangChain","note":"Subagents, handoffs, skills, router and custom workflow compared.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r33","title":"RULER: What's the Real Context Size of Your Long-Context Language Models?","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.06654","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"17 models; only about half keep satisfactory performance at 32K.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r34","title":"Reasoning models (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reasoning","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Effort values, budgets and how to prompt reasoning models.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r35","title":"Develop AI agents on Azure (learning path)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/training/paths/develop-ai-agents-on-azure/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Guided path on Foundry Agent Service and multi-agent solutions.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r36","title":"AI Agents in LangGraph","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/short-courses/ai-agents-in-langgraph/","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Hands-on agent loops, persistence and human in the loop.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r37","title":"Long-Term Agentic Memory With LangGraph","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/short-courses/long-term-agentic-memory-with-langgraph/","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Memory types implemented in code.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r38","title":"Plan-and-Solve Prompting: Improving Zero-Shot Chain-of-Thought Reasoning by Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.04091","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Plan first, then execute subtasks, to reduce missing-step errors.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r39","title":"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Accuracy is highest when relevant text is at the start or end of the input and lowest in the middle. 2023.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r40","title":"Mem0: Building Production-Ready AI Agents with Scalable Long-Term Memory","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.19413","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Vendor-authored LOCOMO results for latency and token savings against full context.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r41","title":"Memory tool (Claude API)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/memory-tool","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Client-side memory: the model requests file operations under /memories and your app executes them. GA status not stated as of 2026-10-13.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r42","title":"Understanding Temporal","url":"https://docs.temporal.io/evaluate/understanding-temporal","type":"doc","publisher":"Temporal","note":"Durable Execution, deterministic workflow code, and Activities with retries, timeouts and heartbeats.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r43","title":"Persistence (LangGraph)","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/persistence","type":"doc","publisher":"LangChain","note":"Checkpointers store thread-scoped state. The in-memory saver loses state on restart.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r44","title":"Interrupts (LangGraph)","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/interrupts","type":"doc","publisher":"LangChain","note":"interrupt() pauses for human input and needs a checkpointer and a thread_id.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr1","title":"Microsoft Agent Framework Workflows Orchestrations - Group Chat","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/workflows/orchestrations/group-chat","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Star topology, speaker selection strategies, shared history and the iteration and termination controls.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr2","title":"Microsoft Agent Framework Workflows Orchestrations - Concurrent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/workflows/orchestrations/concurrent","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Parallel agents on one task with aggregated results, for ensembles and voting.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr3","title":"Running agents (OpenAI Agents SDK)","url":"https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/running_agents/","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"The runner loop, max_turns and MaxTurnsExceeded.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr4","title":"Graph API overview, recursion limit (LangGraph)","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/graph-api","type":"doc","publisher":"LangChain","note":"Recursion limit as a cap on super-steps, with the default and the error it raises.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr5","title":"Self-Consistency Improves Chain of Thought Reasoning in Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.11171","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"The sampling and majority-vote idea behind voting, with benchmark gains.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr6","title":"Improving Factuality and Reasoning in Language Models through Multiagent Debate","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14325","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Multiple model instances debating over rounds to reach a common answer.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr7","title":"Large Language Models Cannot Self-Correct Reasoning Yet","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01798","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Evidence that intrinsic self-correction without external feedback is unreliable.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr8","title":"Self-Refine: Iterative Refinement with Self-Feedback","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.17651","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"One model generates, critiques and refines, with reported gains across seven tasks.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr9","title":"RouteLLM: Learning to Route LLMs with Preference Data","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18665","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Trained routers that choose between a strong and a weak model to cut cost.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr10","title":"LLM06:2025 Excessive Agency (OWASP GenAI Security Project)","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm062025-excessive-agency/","type":"spec","publisher":"OWASP","note":"Root causes of excessive agency and the case for approval of high-impact actions.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr11","title":"Art. 17 GDPR, Right to erasure (right to be forgotten)","url":"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-17-gdpr/","type":"spec","publisher":"gdpr-info.eu","note":"Annotated copy of the regulation text, for erasure grounds and exceptions. The official text is on EUR-Lex.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr12","title":"Microsoft Agent Framework Workflows - Human-in-the-loop (HITL)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/workflows/human-in-the-loop","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"RequestPort and request_info for pausing a workflow until a person responds.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr13","title":"Using function tools with human in the loop approvals (Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/agents/tools/tool-approval","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"ApprovalRequiredAIFunction and how an approval request surfaces to the caller.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr14","title":"Compaction (Microsoft Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/concepts/agents/conversations/compaction","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Experimental compaction strategies, triggers, atomic message groups and where they do not apply.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr15","title":"Compaction overview (Claude API)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/compaction","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Server-side compaction on demand and at a token threshold, both in beta.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr16","title":"Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Memory stream, reflection and dynamic retrieval for agents.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr17","title":"openai/swarm (README)","url":"https://github.com/openai/swarm","type":"repo","publisher":"GitHub (OpenAI)","note":"Marked experimental and educational and replaced by the OpenAI Agents SDK.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"deprecated"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Autonomy dial","def":"How much of the control flow the model decides: none (single call), fixed code paths (workflow), or its own loop (agent)."},{"term":"Workflow","def":"LLMs and tools orchestrated through predefined code paths. The developer owns the control flow."},{"term":"Agent","def":"A system where the LLM dynamically directs its own process and tool use. The model owns the control flow."},{"term":"Augmented LLM","def":"The building block of agentic systems: a model with retrieval, tools and memory."},{"term":"Orchestrator-workers","def":"A lead model decides subtasks at run time, delegates them to workers and synthesises the results."},{"term":"Handoff","def":"Transfer of the conversation to another agent that takes ownership. In the OpenAI Agents SDK a handoff is exposed to the model as a tool named `transfer_to_<agent_name>` by default."},{"term":"Agent as tool","def":"A specialist agent called like a function. The caller keeps ownership and receives control back with the result."},{"term":"Magentic orchestration","def":"A manager agent keeps shared context and a task ledger, tracks progress and re-plans. Based on the Magentic-One design."},{"term":"Task ledger","def":"The manager's running record of facts, plan and progress in Magentic orchestration."},{"term":"Context rot","def":"Degradation of model quality as input length grows, observed below advertised context windows."},{"term":"Compaction","def":"Shrinking context by summarising, clearing stale tool results or offloading content, ideally with a restorable pointer."},{"term":"Sub-agent context isolation","def":"Giving a worker its own clean window and returning only a short result, so exploration noise stays out of the lead's context."},{"term":"Episodic memory","def":"Records of specific past events or interactions, retrieved when a similar situation recurs."},{"term":"Procedural memory","def":"Stored how-to knowledge such as rules, instructions or workflow steps, retrieved for recurring tasks."},{"term":"Superstep","def":"One round of a Microsoft Agent Framework workflow. A checkpoint is written at the end of each one."},{"term":"Idempotency key","def":"A client-generated identifier reused on retries so a repeated request returns the saved result instead of repeating the side effect."},{"term":"Gate","def":"A programmatic check between steps of a chain that validates an intermediate result before the next call runs."},{"term":"Sectioning","def":"A parallelisation variant that splits a task into independent subtasks and runs them at the same time."},{"term":"Voting","def":"A parallelisation variant that runs the same task several times and combines the outputs, for higher confidence."},{"term":"ReAct","def":"A loop that interleaves reasoning with tool actions: think, act, observe, repeat until the answer is supported."},{"term":"Plan-and-execute","def":"A loop shape where a planner writes the steps first, an executor runs them and a replan step handles surprises."},{"term":"Reflexion","def":"An approach that stores verbal self-reflections in an episodic memory buffer and reuses them on the next attempt, with no weight updates."},{"term":"Maker-checker loop","def":"A loop where one agent produces work and another checks it against criteria. Also called evaluator-optimizer, generator-verifier or critic loop."},{"term":"Stop reason","def":"The typed cause for ending a run, such as completed, max steps, budget, deadline, repeat or needs human. Returned with the best partial result."},{"term":"Supervisor","def":"A central agent that owns the conversation and calls specialist agents as tools. Control returns to it after each call."},{"term":"Group chat manager","def":"The orchestrator in a group chat that picks the next speaker and decides when to stop."},{"term":"Progress ledger","def":"In Magentic orchestration, the manager's per-round record of whether the task is done, stalled or needs another agent."},{"term":"MAST","def":"The Multi-Agent System Failure Taxonomy: 14 failure modes in three categories, built from annotated multi-agent traces."},{"term":"Context engineering","def":"Choosing what enters the model's window at each step: instructions, tools, memory, retrieved data, history and tool results."},{"term":"Attention budget","def":"The idea that context is finite with diminishing returns, so every token competes for the model's attention."},{"term":"Tool result clearing","def":"Removing stale tool outputs from context, ideally keeping a pointer so they can be fetched again."},{"term":"Working memory","def":"What the model holds in its context window for the current step, including the scratchpad and plan."},{"term":"Semantic memory","def":"Long-term facts and preferences that stay true across sessions, such as a user's preferred units."},{"term":"Memory poisoning","def":"Planting malicious or misleading entries in an agent's long-term memory or knowledge store so they steer later runs."},{"term":"Checkpoint","def":"A saved snapshot of run state that lets a run resume after a crash, restart or long wait."},{"term":"Durable execution","def":"A runtime model that persists progress, retries failed steps, supports long timers and replays deterministic orchestration code."},{"term":"Approval gate","def":"A point where a human must approve a proposed action, ideally bound to the exact arguments, before it runs."},{"term":"Compensating transaction","def":"A step that undoes the effect of an earlier completed step when a later step of the operation fails."}],"levels":[{"id":"w10-l1","order":1,"title":"Turn the autonomy dial","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"A request needs more than one model call, but the steps, the risk and the latency budget are not fixed yet. Decide how much control the model gets before you choose any framework.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Workflows, agents and the complexity ladder","level":2,"id":"workflows-agents-and-the-complexity-ladder"},{"t":"p","md":"Anthropic separates two kinds of **agentic system**. In a **workflow** the LLMs and tools follow code paths you wrote in advance. In an **agent** the LLM dynamically directs its own process and tool use [[r1]]. Its advice is to find the simplest solution that works, because agentic systems trade latency and cost for task performance. The post is dated 2024-12-19 and a later banner warns that the tooling landscape has changed, so reuse the vocabulary, not the tool list.\n\nMicrosoft's architecture guidance draws the same line as a ladder of three levels: a direct model call, a single agent with tools, and multiagent orchestration. Its rule is to use the lowest level of complexity that reliably meets your requirements, and it calls a single agent with tools often the right default for enterprise use cases [[r9]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-d1","title":"The autonomy ladder","alt":"Four rungs from a direct call to a workflow, a single agent and multi-agent, each adding model control and cost.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[1 Direct call plus retrieval] --> B[2 Workflow: code owns the path]\n  B --> C[3 Single agent: model owns the loop]\n  C --> D[4 Multi-agent: models own the split]","caption":"Climb one rung only when a named dial forces it. Each rung adds tokens, latency and failure surface."},{"t":"compare","title":"The five workflow patterns","intro":"All five sit on an augmented LLM (a model with retrieval, tools and memory). They differ in who decides the path [[r1]].","options":[{"name":"Prompt chaining","summary":"Split the task into fixed subtasks. Each call consumes the previous output, with an optional programmatic gate between steps.","pros":["Each call does one easier job","A gate stops a bad step before it propagates","Every step can be tested alone"],"cons":["Latency is the sum of the steps","The path is fixed, so it cannot add steps"],"bestWhen":"The task decomposes cleanly into known subtasks and you accept more latency for accuracy.","avoidWhen":"What earlier steps find decides what later steps must do.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"sum of steps"},{"k":"Control","v":"code"}]},{"name":"Routing","summary":"Classify the input, then send it to a specialised prompt, tool set or model.","pros":["Specialised prompts stay short","Easy queries can go to a small model and hard ones to a larger one"],"cons":["A misroute is silent","You need labelled traffic to measure the classifier"],"bestWhen":"Distinct request classes need different handling and you can classify them accurately.","avoidWhen":"One class dominates, or the classes overlap and the classifier is guessing.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"one extra cheap call"},{"k":"Control","v":"code plus classifier"}]},{"name":"Parallelization","summary":"Run work at once, either as sectioning (independent subtasks) or voting (the same task several times), then aggregate.","pros":["Wall-clock time falls to the slowest branch","Voting raises confidence on judgement calls"],"cons":["More tokens for the same answer","Needs an aggregation or agreement rule"],"bestWhen":"Subtasks are independent, or you need several views of one decision.","avoidWhen":"Branches depend on each other or share one write target.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"slowest branch"},{"k":"Cost","v":"n times the tokens"}]},{"name":"Orchestrator-workers","summary":"A central LLM breaks the task down at run time, delegates to workers and synthesises their results.","pros":["Handles subtasks you could not list in advance","Workers get focused instructions"],"cons":["Plan, fan out and synthesise add several calls","The lead becomes a bottleneck and a single point of failure"],"bestWhen":"The subtasks depend on the input and cannot be enumerated at design time.","avoidWhen":"You already know the steps, so planning only adds cost.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"high"},{"k":"Control","v":"model plans, code runs"}]},{"name":"Evaluator-optimizer","summary":"One call generates, another evaluates against criteria, and the loop revises until the check passes.","pros":["Quality rises when criteria are clear","The critic can be a different model or a test suite"],"cons":["Every revision is a full round trip","Without clear criteria the loop never converges"],"bestWhen":"There is a clear pass or fail check and revision measurably helps.","avoidWhen":"Quality is subjective and there is no external signal to loop on.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"per revision"},{"k":"Needs","v":"clear criteria"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w10-l1-pick","title":"Pick a pattern by task structure and risk","prompt":"Flip the constraints. The ranking and the reasons update. The boring option is on the board on purpose.","dials":[{"id":"steps","label":"Subtask structure","values":[{"id":"fixed","label":"Steps known in advance"},{"id":"open","label":"Subtasks found at run time"}]},{"id":"check","label":"Quality check","values":[{"id":"clear","label":"Clear pass or fail"},{"id":"vague","label":"No clear check"}]},{"id":"variety","label":"Request variety","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One kind of request"},{"id":"many","label":"Several kinds, different handling"}]},{"id":"time","label":"Latency budget","values":[{"id":"fast","label":"Interactive, seconds"},{"id":"slow","label":"Background, minutes or more"}]}],"options":[{"id":"single","name":"Single call with retrieval","summary":"One prompt, optional retrieval and tools."},{"id":"chain","name":"Prompt chain","summary":"Fixed steps with a gate after each."},{"id":"route","name":"Router","summary":"Classify, then pick a specialised path or model."},{"id":"par","name":"Parallelization","summary":"Independent branches or votes at once, then aggregate."},{"id":"orch","name":"Orchestrator-workers","summary":"A lead plans subtasks at run time and delegates."},{"id":"evalopt","name":"Evaluator-optimizer","summary":"Generate, critique against a check, revise."}],"scores":{"single":{"steps":{"fixed":{"s":0,"why":"Works if the steps fit one prompt. Otherwise split into a chain."},"open":{"s":-2,"why":"It cannot decide new subtasks at run time."}},"check":{"clear":{"s":0,"why":"A single pass has no loop to use the check."},"vague":{"s":1,"why":"With nothing to loop on, one pass is the honest choice."}},"variety":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"One kind of request needs no routing."},"many":{"s":-1,"why":"One prompt must cover every case and grows long."}},"time":{"fast":{"s":2,"why":"Lowest latency and cost."},"slow":{"s":0,"why":"Fine, but it leaves quality headroom unused."}}},"chain":{"steps":{"fixed":{"s":2,"why":"Known steps map straight onto a chain."},"open":{"s":-2,"why":"A fixed chain cannot add steps it did not foresee."}},"check":{"clear":{"s":1,"why":"Gates can test each step programmatically."},"vague":{"s":0,"why":"Gates are weak without a check."}},"variety":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"A single path is enough."},"many":{"s":-1,"why":"Branches multiply. Put a router in front."}},"time":{"fast":{"s":0,"why":"Short chains of small steps add little latency."},"slow":{"s":1,"why":"Extra steps are cheap when no one waits."}}},"route":{"steps":{"fixed":{"s":0,"why":"Routing chooses a path, it does not order steps."},"open":{"s":-1,"why":"Predefined paths miss open-ended work."}},"check":{"clear":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral: the check applies inside each path."},"vague":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"variety":{"one":{"s":-2,"why":"Routing one kind of request adds a hop and a misroute risk for nothing."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"Specialised prompts, tools or cheaper models per class beat one catch-all."}},"time":{"fast":{"s":1,"why":"One cheap classification can avoid a large model call."},"slow":{"s":0,"why":"Savings matter less when no one waits."}}},"par":{"steps":{"fixed":{"s":0,"why":"Helps only where some steps are independent."},"open":{"s":0,"why":"Voting still works on any step, sectioning needs known parts."}},"check":{"clear":{"s":1,"why":"Votes can be compared against a rule."},"vague":{"s":0,"why":"Votes need an agreement rule you do not have."}},"variety":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"time":{"fast":{"s":1,"why":"Wall-clock time falls to the slowest branch, at more tokens."},"slow":{"s":0,"why":"Speed is not needed, you only pay more tokens."}}},"orch":{"steps":{"fixed":{"s":-1,"why":"Planning overhead for steps you already know."},"open":{"s":2,"why":"The lead decides subtasks as the input reveals them."}},"check":{"clear":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral: a check can gate the synthesis."},"vague":{"s":-1,"why":"Hard to tell whether worker results combine into a good answer."}},"variety":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"Workers can specialise per subtask."}},"time":{"fast":{"s":-2,"why":"Plan, fan out and synthesise add several model calls."},"slow":{"s":2,"why":"Breadth-first work tolerates the wait."}}},"evalopt":{"steps":{"fixed":{"s":0,"why":"Adds a loop after the fixed steps."},"open":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"check":{"clear":{"s":2,"why":"A clear check gives the loop a stopping rule and a signal."},"vague":{"s":-2,"why":"Without criteria the critic drifts and never converges."}},"variety":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"time":{"fast":{"s":-2,"why":"Each revision is a full round trip."},"slow":{"s":1,"why":"The quality gain is worth the extra passes."}}}},"takeaway":"Read the winner, then read its losing dial. Every pattern has a setting where it is the wrong tool."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes when you pick the wrong rung","items":[{"symptom":"Cost and latency per task are several times the plan, and runs differ each time","cause":"An agent loop was used for steps that were known in advance","fix":"Replace the loop with a chain. Track tokens per task and steps per task, and alert when the median doubles."},{"symptom":"A wrong early answer flows through to a confident wrong final answer","cause":"A chain without gates lets errors compound","fix":"Add a programmatic check between steps and stop the chain on failure. Log which gate rejected."},{"symptom":"Users get generic policy text for account-specific questions","cause":"The router sends ambiguous requests to the wrong path with no confidence threshold","fix":"Measure a confusion matrix on labelled traffic, add a fallback path for low confidence and route ties to the more capable path."},{"symptom":"Revision loop runs to its cap and the output barely changes","cause":"Evaluator-optimizer without a clear criterion or external signal","fix":"Define the pass check (tests, schema, rubric), cap iterations and stop when the score stops improving."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Ask whether the steps are known **before** you say the word agent. Name the rung you chose, the dial that put you there and the dial that would move you up one rung."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-l1-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","difficulty":1,"q":"Which statement matches the way Anthropic separates a workflow from an agent?","options":["Workflows never call any tools, while agents always call tools and keep looping until the goal is met","Workflows use one model call and agents use several","In a workflow code fixes the path, while in an agent the LLM directs its own process and tool use","Workflows run synchronously and agents run in the background"],"answer":2,"explain":"The line is who owns the control flow. Workflows can use tools and many calls, and agents can run synchronously, so the other three describe features that do not separate the two kinds.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w10-l1-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Match each pattern to the situation where it fits best.","pairs":[{"l":"Prompt chaining","r":"Known subtasks where a gate can check each step"},{"l":"Routing","r":"Distinct request classes that need different prompts or models"},{"l":"Orchestrator-workers","r":"Subtasks that depend on the input and cannot be listed in advance"},{"l":"Evaluator-optimizer","r":"A clear pass or fail check that makes revision measurable"}],"explain":"Each pattern buys flexibility with latency or tokens: chains fix the path, routers add a classifier, orchestrators add planning calls and evaluator loops need a criterion to stop.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w10-l1-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"q":"A fixed three-step pipeline with automated validation is accurate and fast enough. Which change in the situation would justify moving to orchestrator-workers?","options":["The team wants the design to look more modern and match what other companies are shipping","The subtasks depend on what the input contains and cannot be listed up front","The validation step has become cheaper to run","The users ask for shorter answers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Orchestrator-workers pays for planning calls, so it only wins when the subtasks are decided at run time. Fashion, cheaper validation and shorter answers do not change whether the steps are knowable.","ref":"r9"},{"id":"w10-l1-q4","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"Users complain that refund questions get generic policy text. What is the most likely cause and the best first fix?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"route=faq  confidence=0.51  query=\"where is my refund for order 1182\"\nroute=faq  confidence=0.48  query=\"refund status\"\nroute=billing confidence=0.93 query=\"update card on file\""},"options":["The FAQ model is too small, so upgrade it","Retrieval is returning stale chunks, so re-embed","The router accepts low-confidence labels, so add a threshold with a fallback path","The chain has too many steps, so remove the gate between the classifier and the retrieval step"],"answer":2,"explain":"Low-confidence routes are being accepted and sent to the FAQ path. A threshold with a fallback and a confusion matrix on labelled traffic addresses that. A larger FAQ model, re-embedding and removing a gate do not change the route taken.","ref":"r1"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r9"],"takeaways":["Workflows keep control flow in your code, agents hand it to the model. Both are agentic systems.","Use the lowest rung that reliably works, and name the dial that would move you up.","Five patterns cover most workflows: chain, route, parallelize, orchestrate, evaluate and optimize.","Every pattern has a losing dial: chains need known steps, routers need classifiable traffic, evaluator loops need a clear check."]},{"id":"w10-l2","order":2,"title":"Loops with brakes","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"The model must decide its own next step, call a tool, read the result and go again. Tool reliability, plan stability and the token budget are not fixed. Choose a loop shape and decide what stops it.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Four loop shapes and the brakes they all need","level":2,"id":"four-loop-shapes-and-the-brakes-they-all-need"},{"t":"compare","title":"ReAct, plan-and-execute, reflection or a reasoning model","intro":"Loops differ in where the thinking happens and what feeds the next step. Reported numbers come from the papers and are not a promise for your task.","options":[{"name":"ReAct","summary":"Interleave a reasoning trace, an action and an observation, so each tool result updates the next thought.","pros":["Adapts to surprising tool output","Simple to build and to read in a trace","On ALFWorld and WebShop it beat imitation and RL baselines by an absolute 34% and 10% success rate with one or two in-context examples [[r16]]"],"cons":["Re-reads a growing context at every step","A bad early observation can steer the whole run"],"bestWhen":"Tool results are unpredictable and the next step genuinely depends on the last one.","avoidWhen":"The tool chain is predictable, so interleaved thinking is wasted tokens.","meta":[{"k":"Tokens","v":"grow each step"},{"k":"Adapts","v":"every step"}]},{"name":"Plan-and-execute (Plan-and-Solve, ReWOO)","summary":"Devise the plan first, then run the steps. ReWOO plans the tool calls up front and runs them without interleaved reasoning.","pros":["Targets the missing-step errors of zero-shot chain of thought [[r38]]","ReWOO reports 5x token efficiency and 4% higher accuracy on HotpotQA [[r18]]","The plan can be shown to a reviewer first"],"cons":["A stale plan runs against facts that changed","Needs a re-plan trigger"],"bestWhen":"The tool chain is predictable and you want fewer tokens or a reviewable plan.","avoidWhen":"Early observations often invalidate later steps.","meta":[{"k":"Tokens","v":"lower"},{"k":"Adapts","v":"only at re-plan"}]},{"name":"Reflection (Reflexion, evaluator loop)","summary":"Run, critique the outcome, store a verbal reflection and try again. Reflexion keeps reflections in an episodic buffer with no weight updates.","pros":["Reports 91% pass@1 on HumanEval against 80% for the GPT-4 baseline [[r17]]","Works with an external signal such as unit tests"],"cons":["Each retry is a full run","Self-critique without a signal tends to agree with itself"],"bestWhen":"A reliable feedback signal exists: tests, a validator, a retrieval check.","avoidWhen":"The only critic is the same model reading its own answer.","meta":[{"k":"Needs","v":"external signal"},{"k":"Cost","v":"per retry"}]},{"name":"Reasoning model at higher effort","summary":"Move part of the loop inside the model and spend reasoning tokens before acting.","pros":["Fewer scripted steps in your code","Often better at planning tool use"],"cons":["Reasoning tokens are billed as output","Latency to the first visible token rises"],"bestWhen":"Evals show a quality gain that justifies the latency and cost.","avoidWhen":"Voice, classification or retrieval steps where speed matters more.","meta":[{"k":"Tokens","v":"billed as output"},{"k":"Tune","v":"effort per model"}]}]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-d2","title":"A loop with brakes","alt":"Flow from task to a budget check, model step, final check, repeated-call check and tool run, with stop exits on budget and repeats.","src":"flowchart TD\n  S[Start with task] --> B{Budget left?}\n  B -->|no| X[Stop with reason]\n  B -->|yes| M[Model picks next step]\n  M --> F{Final answer?}\n  F -->|yes| D[Return result]\n  F -->|no| R{Repeated call?}\n  R -->|yes| X\n  R -->|no| T[Run tool, trim result]\n  T --> B","caption":"The budget check sits before the model call. Every exit carries a stop reason."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"Loop skeleton with four brakes","code":"def run(task, budget):\n    state = start(task)\n    seen = Counter()\n    while True:\n        if state.steps >= budget.max_steps:    return stop('max_steps', state)\n        if state.tokens >= budget.max_tokens:  return stop('max_tokens', state)\n        if clock() - state.t0 >= budget.max_s: return stop('deadline', state)\n        step = model.next_step(state.context)\n        if step.final:                         return finish(step, state)\n        key = (step.tool, canonical(step.args))\n        seen[key] += 1\n        if seen[key] > budget.max_repeat:      return stop('repeated_call', state)\n        obs = call_tool(step, idempotency_key=state.key(step))\n        state = state.append(step, trim(obs))","caption":"Illustrative pseudo-code. The harness enforces every limit, and the stop reason reaches the caller and the logs."},{"t":"formula","title":"Input tokens of a naive loop","expr":"total_input = n * P + d * n * (n - 1) / 2","vars":[{"k":"n","v":"number of model calls in the run"},{"k":"P","v":"tokens in the fixed prefix (instructions, tools, task)"},{"k":"d","v":"tokens added to the context by each step"}],"example":"With P = 6,000, d = 1,500 and n = 20: 20 x 6,000 + 1,500 x 190 = 120,000 + 285,000 = **405,000** input tokens. The second term is quadratic, so clearing, compacting or caching it matters more than shaving the prefix. Manus reports an average input to output token ratio of about 100:1 for its agent [[r7]]."},{"t":"failure","title":"Loop and budget failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"The same tool call with the same arguments repeats until the cap","cause":"The tool error carries no new information, so the model retries","fix":"Detect repeated calls, return structured errors with a hint, cap retries per tool and stop with reason repeated_call."},{"symptom":"A partial draft is shown to the user as if it were complete","cause":"The budget was hit and the harness returned the last message without a stop reason","fix":"Return and log the stop reason, mark the answer incomplete and track the stop-reason mix as a metric."},{"symptom":"Step 6 of the plan fails because step 3 changed the facts","cause":"Plan-and-execute without a re-plan trigger","fix":"Re-plan on tool errors or surprising observations and cap the number of re-plans."},{"symptom":"Critic scores stay flat while cost climbs","cause":"Reflection with no external signal, so the critic agrees with the actor","fix":"Add tests, a schema or a retrieval check as the signal, or use a different critic. Stop when the score stops improving."},{"symptom":"Input tokens per step rise on every iteration","cause":"Every tool result stays in the context for the whole run","fix":"Clear stale tool results, cache the stable prefix and compact history. Level 4 shows how."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Scripting a reasoning model","md":"OpenAI's guide says reasoning-capable GPT-5 models usually work best with a clear goal, strong constraints and an explicit output contract, without prescribing every intermediate step [[r34]]. Treat effort as a cost and latency dial, not only a quality dial. World 18 covers effort and test-time compute."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-l2-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"q":"An agent loop has a 4,000-token fixed prefix and each step adds 2,000 tokens of tool output and reasoning that stay in context. Nothing is cleared or cached. How many input tokens does a 12-call run read in total? (The first call reads only the prefix.)","answer":180000,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"tokens","hint":"total = n x P + d x n(n-1)/2","explain":"n x P = 12 x 4,000 = 48,000. The accumulated observations add d x n(n-1)/2 = 2,000 x 66 = 132,000. The total is 180,000. A linear estimate of 12 x (4,000 + 2,000) = 72,000 badly understates it."},{"id":"w10-l2-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"q":"Select every brake that has to be enforced by the harness rather than left to the model's own judgement.","options":["A step cap that ends the run with the stop reason max_steps","A system prompt line asking the model to stop after ten steps","A token or cost budget tracked from usage metadata","Detection of repeated identical tool calls that aborts the run","The model's own estimate of how much budget is left"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"Limits the model could ignore, miscount or talk itself past must live in code, which is why the step cap, the metered budget and repeat detection qualify. A prompt line and the model's self-estimate are advice, so a looping or confused model can disregard them.","ref":"r9"},{"id":"w10-l2-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"A coding agent retries a fix up to five times with a critic prompt. The trace shows the critic approving or nitpicking the same code every round while cost rises. Which change addresses the root cause?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"round 1  critic: looks good, minor naming nits   tests: not run\nround 2  critic: looks good, minor naming nits   tests: not run\nround 3  critic: looks good                       tests: not run\ncost: 31k -> 58k -> 92k tokens"},"options":["Use a longer critic prompt with a stricter tone and a detailed checklist of style rules","Run the test suite and feed failures back as the signal","Raise the number of rounds from five to ten","Lower the temperature of the actor"],"answer":1,"explain":"The loop has no external signal, so the critic mostly agrees with the actor. Tests give a real pass or fail to loop on. A stricter prompt still reads its own work, more rounds spend more tokens on the same flat signal and temperature does not create a check.","ref":"r17"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r16","r38","r18","r17","r7","r34","r36"],"takeaways":["Pick the loop by tool predictability: ReAct for surprises, plan-first when the chain is predictable, reflection only with a real signal.","A naive loop reads the whole history again at every step, so input tokens grow quadratically.","Budget, step cap, deadline and repeat detection belong in the harness and return an explicit stop reason.","Reasoning models move part of the loop inside the model. Give goals and contracts, then measure before raising effort."]},{"id":"w10-l3","order":3,"title":"Soloist or crowd? Topologies and the multi-agent bill","kind":"problem","minutes":22,"brief":"One agent is struggling, and someone proposes a team of agents. The coupling of the subtasks, the value per run, the trust boundaries and the latency budget are all undecided. Compare the topologies against the evidence before you split anything.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Five ways to share the work, and what the evidence says","level":2,"id":"five-ways-to-share-the-work-and-what-the-evidence-says"},{"t":"compare","title":"Multi-agent topologies","intro":"Microsoft lists sequential, concurrent, group chat (including maker-checker loops), handoff and magentic orchestration [[r9]]. LangChain frames the same space as subagents, handoffs, skills, router and custom workflow [[r32]]. The key question is who owns the conversation after a delegation.","options":[{"name":"Supervisor with agents as tools","summary":"A primary agent calls specialists like functions, keeps ownership and receives control back with each result.","pros":["The caller stays accountable for the final answer","Workers can be read-only and hold least-privilege tools","Parallel calls shorten wall-clock time"],"cons":["Every hop passes through the supervisor","The supervisor's window collects every result"],"bestWhen":"One agent must own the answer and combine several specialist results.","avoidWhen":"A specialist should talk to the user directly for the rest of the conversation.","meta":[{"k":"Owner after delegation","v":"caller"},{"k":"Shape","v":"hub and spoke"}]},{"name":"Handoff mesh","summary":"No central orchestrator: the receiving agent takes full ownership and the full context. In the OpenAI Agents SDK a handoff is exposed as a tool named transfer_to_<agent_name> by default [[r20]].","pros":["The right specialist talks to the user with no return hop","Each specialist owns its tools, policy and owner"],"cons":["Infinite handoff loops and bouncing are a documented risk [[r9]]","Microsoft's handoff supports Agent only, and agents must support local tool execution [[r11]]"],"bestWhen":"Specialists should take over and own the rest of the conversation.","avoidWhen":"The caller must combine several answers or stay accountable.","meta":[{"k":"Owner after delegation","v":"receiver"},{"k":"Shape","v":"mesh"}]},{"name":"Group chat and maker-checker","summary":"Several agents take turns in a shared conversation, often one produces and another reviews.","pros":["Review loops raise quality when criteria are clear","Everyone sees the same thread"],"cons":["Turns multiply tokens","Stalls and non-terminating chats need a stop rule"],"bestWhen":"A produce-and-review loop has a clear acceptance check.","avoidWhen":"The path is deterministic or latency is tight.","meta":[{"k":"Owner after delegation","v":"shared"},{"k":"Needs","v":"termination rule"}]},{"name":"Magentic","tag":"Preview","summary":"A manager keeps shared context and a task ledger, tracks progress and re-plans. Built on the Magentic-One design [[r31]].","pros":["A visible, adjustable plan for open-ended work","Re-plans when a step fails"],"cons":["Slow and token heavy","Microsoft says it is untested how well it performs outside the original Magentic-One design, and the C# types are experimental [[r10]]"],"bestWhen":"Open-ended work where a plan ledger is worth the cost and the wait.","avoidWhen":"The task is time-sensitive or the path is deterministic.","meta":[{"k":"Owner after delegation","v":"manager"},{"k":"Status","v":"C# types experimental, checked 2026-10-13"}]},{"name":"Sequential or concurrent pipeline","summary":"Fixed stages of specialists in order, or independent perspectives run in parallel and merged.","pros":["Predictable and easy to trace","Concurrent runs add independent views"],"cons":["No adaptation to surprises","Sequential stages add latency"],"bestWhen":"The stages are known, or the perspectives are independent.","avoidWhen":"What one stage finds should change the later stages.","meta":[{"k":"Owner after delegation","v":"code"},{"k":"Shape","v":"pipeline"}]}]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-d3","title":"Who owns the conversation","alt":"Sequence showing an agent called as a tool returning control to the triage agent, and a handoff passing ownership to the billing agent.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant U as User\n  participant T as Triage agent\n  participant B as Billing agent\n  alt Agent as tool\n    U->>T: Question\n    T->>B: Call as tool\n    B-->>T: Result\n    T-->>U: Answer\n  else Handoff\n    U->>T: Question\n    T->>B: Handoff with context\n    B-->>U: Answer from the new owner\n  end","caption":"Agent as tool returns control to the caller. A handoff moves ownership and the full context to the receiver."},{"t":"table","caption":"Single versus multi-agent matrix. Numbers are vendor-reported unless marked.","head":["Dimension","Single agent with tools","Orchestrator with read-only workers","Parallel writers or free-form crowd"],"rows":[["Latency","Fewest hops, but tool calls run one after another","Parallel reads cut wall-clock time (Anthropic reports up to 90% on research), yet synchronous subagents became a bottleneck [[r2]]","Hops, negotiation and re-plans add delay"],["Token cost","About 4x chat tokens [[r2]]","About 15x chat tokens, so value per task must pay for it [[r2]]","Highest and least predictable when loops or stalls occur"],["Context isolation","One window holds every result, so rot risk grows","Each worker has a clean window and returns a short result [[r6]]","Isolated, but decisions rest on partial views [[r3]]"],["Debuggability","One linear trace","A lead trace plus worker traces, which needs trace propagation","Many interleaved traces. MAST catalogues 14 failure modes [[r5]]"],["Blast radius","One identity and tool set, all or nothing","Read-only workers cannot change state and can hold least privilege","Every writer can corrupt shared state"],["Consistency","One decision maker, consistent choices","A single writer keeps decisions consistent [[r4]]","Actions carry implicit decisions that conflict [[r3]]"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Tokens vs chat, agent","value":"about 4x","note":"Anthropic, own traffic, vendor-reported"},{"label":"Tokens vs chat, multi-agent","value":"about 15x","note":"Same source. Cost must be justified by value."},{"label":"Gain over single-agent Opus 4","value":"90.2%","note":"Anthropic internal research eval, breadth-first queries, 2025-06-13"},{"label":"Variance explained by token use","value":"80%","note":"BrowseComp. 95% with tool calls and model choice added."}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w10-l3-topology","title":"Single agent, or which crowd?","prompt":"Flip the dials. Parallel writers are not on the board because no setting rescues them.","dials":[{"id":"coupling","label":"Subtask coupling","values":[{"id":"reads","label":"Independent reads or analysis"},{"id":"writes","label":"Coupled writes to one artifact"}]},{"id":"value","label":"Value per run","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Low, cost sensitive"},{"id":"high","label":"High, breadth pays"}]},{"id":"boundary","label":"Ownership boundary","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One team and trust zone"},{"id":"split","label":"Separate teams or trust zones"}]},{"id":"time","label":"Latency budget","values":[{"id":"fast","label":"Interactive"},{"id":"slow","label":"Background"}]}],"options":[{"id":"single","name":"Single agent with tools","summary":"One loop, one window, one decision maker."},{"id":"orch","name":"Orchestrator with read-only workers","summary":"Lead delegates reads and keeps the pen."},{"id":"handoff","name":"Handoff mesh","summary":"Specialists take over the conversation."},{"id":"chat","name":"Group chat or maker-checker","summary":"Agents take turns, often producer and reviewer."},{"id":"magentic","name":"Magentic planning manager","summary":"Task ledger, progress tracking and re-planning."}],"scores":{"single":{"coupling":{"reads":{"s":0,"why":"One agent can read many sources in sequence."},"writes":{"s":2,"why":"One decision maker keeps writes consistent."}},"value":{"low":{"s":2,"why":"The cheapest rung that works."},"high":{"s":-1,"why":"Leaves breadth and parallelism unused when value justifies the spend."}},"boundary":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"No reason to split."},"split":{"s":-2,"why":"One identity and tool set spans zones that should stay separate."}},"time":{"fast":{"s":1,"why":"Fewest hops."},"slow":{"s":0,"why":"Fine, but long runs fill one window."}}},"orch":{"coupling":{"reads":{"s":2,"why":"Read-only workers run in parallel with clean windows."},"writes":{"s":-2,"why":"Parallel workers writing one artifact is the failure Cognition describes. Keep one writer."}},"value":{"low":{"s":-2,"why":"About 15x chat tokens is hard to repay on low-value runs."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Breadth-first work repaid the cost in Anthropic's research eval."}},"boundary":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"split":{"s":1,"why":"Workers can hold separate identities and tools."}},"time":{"fast":{"s":0,"why":"Parallel reads help, but planning and synthesis add calls."},"slow":{"s":1,"why":"Tolerates the wait."}}},"handoff":{"coupling":{"reads":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"writes":{"s":-1,"why":"Ownership moves, so context and decisions must travel with it."}},"value":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Cost depends on how often agents hand off."},"high":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"boundary":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"split":{"s":2,"why":"Each specialist can own its tools, policy and owner."}},"time":{"fast":{"s":1,"why":"The specialist answers the user directly, with no return hop."},"slow":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"chat":{"coupling":{"reads":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"writes":{"s":1,"why":"A checker can review while one agent writes."}},"value":{"low":{"s":-2,"why":"Turns multiply tokens."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Review loops can lift quality when criteria are clear."}},"boundary":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"split":{"s":0,"why":"A shared thread exposes every message to every agent."}},"time":{"fast":{"s":-2,"why":"Turn-taking and stalls add delay."},"slow":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"magentic":{"coupling":{"reads":{"s":1,"why":"A task ledger tracks open-ended gathering."},"writes":{"s":-1,"why":"The planner may assign writes to several agents at once."}},"value":{"low":{"s":-2,"why":"Planning and re-planning are expensive."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"A plan ledger pays only on open-ended, high-value work."}},"boundary":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"split":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"time":{"fast":{"s":-2,"why":"Microsoft advises against it for time-sensitive work."},"slow":{"s":1,"why":"Open-ended runs tolerate re-planning."}}}},"takeaway":"The single agent wins whenever writes are coupled and value is modest. Splitting needs a dial you can name: independent reads with high value, or a trust boundary."},{"t":"failure","title":"Multi-agent failure modes (MAST categories)","items":[{"symptom":"Two workers edit the same artifact and the result contradicts itself","cause":"Parallel writers make implicit decisions without seeing each other's actions (Cognition, 2025-06-12) [[r3]]","fix":"Single-threaded writes. Workers read and report, one agent writes, and traces are shared in full, not only summaries."},{"symptom":"Requests bounce between specialists, or a group chat never ends","cause":"Handoff loops or no termination rule. MAST files unawareness of termination conditions and step repetition under system design issues [[r5]]","fix":"Hop cap, handoff allow-list and a termination condition owned by the orchestrator."},{"symptom":"The run reports success and the output fails a spot check","cause":"No agent verified the result, a task verification failure [[r5]]","fix":"An independent checker with a clean context and an external check such as tests or a schema."},{"symptom":"Run time equals the slowest subagent every time","cause":"Subagents ran synchronously, which Anthropic found created bottlenecks [[r2]]","fix":"Run workers in parallel with timeouts and let the lead continue with partial results."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"why","title":"Anthropic and Cognition fit together","md":"Anthropic's gain came from breadth-first research reads. Cognition argued on 2025-06-12 against parallel agents that write [[r3]]. On 2026-04-22 it said that view still holds for parallel-writer swarms, that several agents can contribute while writes stay single-threaded, and that a review loop works best when the reviewer shares no context with the coder. It reports Devin Review catching on average 2 bugs per pull request, about 58% severe (vendor-reported) [[r4]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-l3-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"q":"Which change in the situation flips the choice from a handoff to an agent-as-tool design?","options":["The specialist should own the rest of the conversation and talk to the user directly in every later turn","The specialist needs the full conversation history to do its job","Handoffs are unavailable in the framework you picked","The caller must stay accountable for the final answer and combine several specialist results"],"answer":3,"explain":"With agent-as-tool the primary agent keeps ownership and control returns to it, which is what accountability and result combination need. The first two describe reasons to hand off, and a handoff already passes the full context. Framework availability is a constraint, not a dial that changes the design.","ref":"r11"},{"id":"w10-l3-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"q":"Select every signal that justifies splitting one agent into several.","options":["The prompt has grown many if-then-else branches that are hard to maintain","Tool selection keeps failing after you clarified the tool descriptions","Parts of the work must run under different identities, teams or trust zones","The single agent looked slow in one demo","A competitor announced a multi-agent product"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"OpenAI advises maximizing a single agent first, then splitting when prompts hold many branches or tool selection fails after clearer descriptions. Separate trust boundaries can justify the extra cost on their own. One slow demo and a competitor's announcement are not engineering signals.","ref":"r19"},{"id":"w10-l3-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Match each topology to the failure it is most associated with.","pairs":[{"l":"Handoff mesh","r":"Bouncing between agents in an infinite handoff loop"},{"l":"Group chat","r":"A conversation that stalls or never terminates"},{"l":"Magentic","r":"Planning and re-planning cost on work that did not need it"},{"l":"Parallel writers","r":"Conflicting implicit decisions in one shared artifact"}],"explain":"Each topology fails where its strength is overused: a mesh has no central stopper, a chat needs a stop rule, a planning manager spends tokens and time, and parallel writers cannot see each other's decisions.","ref":"r5"},{"id":"w10-l3-q4","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":1,"q":"A chat answer to a research question uses about 8,000 tokens. Using Anthropic's reported multiplier for multi-agent systems, roughly how many tokens would a multi-agent run on the same question use?","answer":120000,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"tokens","hint":"Multi-agent systems used about 15x the tokens of chat.","explain":"8,000 x 15 = 120,000 tokens. The multiplier is vendor-reported on Anthropic's own research traffic, so treat it as an order of magnitude and measure your own. Using the 4x agent multiplier instead would give only 32,000.","ref":"r2"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r9","r2","r3","r4","r5","r11","r10","r19","r20","r32","r31"],"takeaways":["Ask who owns the conversation after a delegation: the caller (agent as tool) or the receiver (handoff).","Multi-agent systems used about 15x the tokens of chat in Anthropic's data, so they must be justified by value per run.","Reads parallelize safely, coupled writes do not. Keep a single writer and add read-only workers.","Handoff loops, stalled chats and unverified results are the named failures. Put hop caps, stop rules and an independent checker in the design."]},{"id":"w10-l4","order":4,"title":"Context is a budget, not a bucket","kind":"lab","minutes":22,"brief":"Every step of an agent adds tokens to a window that has a hard limit and a soft quality limit. The number of tool calls, whether dropped content can be restored and whether the work branches are all open. Learn what to clear, compact, offload or isolate.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Spend the attention budget on purpose","level":2,"id":"spend-the-attention-budget-on-purpose"},{"t":"p","md":"Anthropic frames context as a finite resource with diminishing returns: the model has an **attention budget**, and every added token competes with the others. The goal is the smallest set of high-signal tokens that makes the next step succeed. Its long-horizon toolkit is compaction, structured note-taking and multi-agent architectures, plus loading data on demand: keep lightweight identifiers such as paths, queries and links and fetch the content with tools [[r6]].\n\nThe pressure is real below the advertised window. Chroma tested 18 models and found non-uniform degradation as input grows, even on simple tasks, and noted that needle-in-a-haystack tests only measure lexical retrieval [[r8]]. Lost in the Middle (2023) found accuracy highest when the relevant text sits at the start or end of the input and lowest in the middle [[r39]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"The window budget","expr":"used = system + tools + memory + history + retrieval + tool_results + reserved_output   (stay under window)","vars":[{"k":"tools","v":"tool definitions, paid on every request"},{"k":"history","v":"turns kept in the conversation"},{"k":"tool_results","v":"outputs of tool calls still in context"},{"k":"reserved_output","v":"room kept free for the answer and any reasoning tokens"}],"example":"The lab's Tool-heavy agent preset (illustrative sizes): 1,500 + 80 x 500 + 2,000 + 40 x 900 + 6 x 800 + 42,000 + 8,000 = **134,300** tokens in a 128,000 window. It overflows by 6,300 before the user says anything unusual."},{"t":"lab","id":"context-budget","intro":"Illustrative planner, not a tokenizer. Segment sizes, windows and the cached-read discount are inputs you control, not vendor figures. Load the Tool-heavy agent preset and apply tool search. Then compare summarising with dropping on the Long-running research agent preset, and finish by switching to the cache-friendly order."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Models tested, Chroma","value":"18","note":"All degraded as input grew, unevenly (July 2025)"},{"label":"RULER","value":"about half","note":"Of 17 models kept satisfactory performance at 32K despite claiming 32K or more"},{"label":"NoLiMa at 32K","value":"11 of 13","note":"Models claiming 128K or more fell below 50% of their short-context baseline"},{"label":"GPT-4o in NoLiMa","value":"99.3% to 69.7%","note":"Short context to 32K"}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w10-l4-context","title":"Pick a context strategy for a long run","prompt":"Flip the dials. Short runs need none of this, so doing nothing is on the board.","dials":[{"id":"calls","label":"Tool calls per task","values":[{"id":"few","label":"Under 10"},{"id":"many","label":"50 or more"}]},{"id":"restore","label":"Source of dropped output","values":[{"id":"yes","label":"Stable: path, URL or id re-fetches it"},{"id":"no","label":"Ephemeral: live or one-time output"}]},{"id":"shape","label":"Task shape","values":[{"id":"seq","label":"One sequential thread"},{"id":"par","label":"Independent branches"}]},{"id":"detail","label":"Detail that must survive","values":[{"id":"exact","label":"Exact ids, numbers and wording"},{"id":"gist","label":"The gist is enough"}]}],"options":[{"id":"bigger","name":"Bigger window, keep everything","summary":"Do nothing clever and pay for the tokens."},{"id":"clear","name":"Clear stale tool results","summary":"Drop old outputs, keep the call record and a pointer."},{"id":"compact","name":"Compaction (summarise history)","summary":"Replace old turns with a model-written summary."},{"id":"offload","name":"Offload to files or notes","summary":"Write state outside the window, load it on demand."},{"id":"subagent","name":"Sub-agent isolation","summary":"Workers explore in clean windows and return short results."}],"scores":{"bigger":{"calls":{"few":{"s":2,"why":"A short run fits, so there is nothing to engineer."},"many":{"s":-2,"why":"Cost, latency and rot grow with every step."}},"restore":{"yes":{"s":0,"why":"You could drop and re-fetch, but short runs do not need to."},"no":{"s":1,"why":"If dropped output is gone for good, keeping it is defensible."}},"shape":{"seq":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"par":{"s":-1,"why":"Branch noise crowds the lead's window."}},"detail":{"exact":{"s":1,"why":"Nothing is summarised away."},"gist":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"clear":{"calls":{"few":{"s":0,"why":"Little to clear."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"Stale tool output dominates long runs."}},"restore":{"yes":{"s":1,"why":"A pointer lets you re-fetch what you cleared."},"no":{"s":-2,"why":"Cleared output is gone for good."}},"shape":{"seq":{"s":1,"why":"Natural for a single thread."},"par":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"detail":{"exact":{"s":0,"why":"Keep identifiers and error text, clear only the bulk."},"gist":{"s":1,"why":"Used bulk output rarely matters again."}}},"compact":{"calls":{"few":{"s":-1,"why":"An extra model call for little gain."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"Handles long histories when no pointers exist."}},"restore":{"yes":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"no":{"s":1,"why":"Works without pointers, at a cost in detail."}},"shape":{"seq":{"s":1,"why":"One thread has one history to summarise."},"par":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}},"detail":{"exact":{"s":-2,"why":"Summaries lose exact wording, numbers and rare constraints."},"gist":{"s":2,"why":"The gist is what a summary keeps."}}},"offload":{"calls":{"few":{"s":-2,"why":"File tools and note discipline cost more than they save."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"Notes and files carry state across the window."}},"restore":{"yes":{"s":2,"why":"Paths and ids make dropped content restorable."},"no":{"s":1,"why":"Write the ephemeral output to a file first, then drop it from context."}},"shape":{"seq":{"s":1,"why":"A progress file lets a fresh window resume."},"par":{"s":1,"why":"Shared notes let branches coordinate."}},"detail":{"exact":{"s":1,"why":"Exact content stays in the file."},"gist":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"subagent":{"calls":{"few":{"s":-2,"why":"Spawning workers is overhead for a short run."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"Heavy exploration stays out of the lead's window."}},"restore":{"yes":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"no":{"s":1,"why":"The worker absorbs ephemeral output and returns the facts that matter."}},"shape":{"seq":{"s":-1,"why":"Sequential work gains little from isolation."},"par":{"s":2,"why":"Independent branches each get a clean window."}},"detail":{"exact":{"s":-1,"why":"Returns are condensed unless the worker hands back artifacts by reference."},"gist":{"s":1,"why":"Condensed returns are enough."}}}},"takeaway":"Order the techniques by how reversible they are: clear and offload first, compaction next, sub-agents when branches are independent. Doing nothing wins only on short runs."},{"t":"failure","title":"Context failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Cache hit rate collapses after a release and cost per task jumps","cause":"A timestamp or request id sits at the start of the system prompt, or tool definitions reorder between calls","fix":"Keep the prefix stable and append-only, move volatile text to the end, serialise tools and JSON deterministically, and alert on hit rate per deployment [[r7]]."},{"symptom":"The agent forgets an early user constraint after compaction","cause":"The summary dropped a rare constraint","fix":"Pin constraints outside the summarised region and test every compaction with a recall probe of facts that must survive."},{"symptom":"A result needed at step 40 is gone","cause":"Output was cleared with no pointer to its source","fix":"Keep the path, URL or id when clearing, and write ephemeral output to a file before dropping it [[r7]]."},{"symptom":"Answers degrade although the prompt fits the window","cause":"Context rot: quality falls well below the advertised window [[r22]]","fix":"Measure quality at your real input lengths, retrieve on demand and keep only high-signal tokens."},{"symptom":"The model calls a tool that was removed mid-run, and the cache misses","cause":"Tool definitions were added or removed dynamically","fix":"Mask tool availability instead of removing definitions, as Manus recommends [[r7]]."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Tool result clearing as the first step","md":"Anthropic calls tool result clearing one of the safest, lightest forms of compaction [[r6]]. Its context editing feature is in beta as of 2026-10-13: header `context-management-2025-06-27`, strategies `clear_tool_uses_20250919` (with optional `clear_tool_inputs`) and `clear_thinking_20251015`. The page says server-side compaction is now the primary strategy [[r30]]. Manus calls KV-cache hit rate the single most important production metric of an agent, and quotes cached input at $0.30 per million tokens against $3 uncached on 2025-07-18 pricing, so check current prices [[r7]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-l4-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"q":"A tool-heavy agent overflows its window. Retrieval metrics are healthy, and most tokens are stale tool outputs from earlier steps whose sources can be re-fetched. Which step comes first?","options":["Summarise the entire conversation with a second model call and replace the whole history","Clear stale tool results, keeping a pointer to each source","Drop the oldest turns first, including the original task instructions, to free space quickly","Move to a model with a larger context window and keep every tool result as it is"],"answer":1,"explain":"Clearing stale outputs with a pointer is reversible, cheap and targets the bulk of the tokens. Summarising everything costs a model call and loses exact detail, dropping the oldest turns can delete the task itself, and a larger window keeps the cost and the rot.","ref":"r6"},{"id":"w10-l4-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"Cached-token share fell from about 80% to near zero after a deploy, with no change to model or traffic. What is the most likely cause?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"- system: \"You are a support agent.\"\n+ system: \"Current time: {timestamp}. You are a support agent.\""},"options":["A timestamp leads the prompt, so no request shares an identical prefix","The provider throttled cached reads for this account after the deploy raised the request rate","The tool list grew past the cacheable limit","Cache entries expire after a single request"],"answer":0,"explain":"Prompt caches match an identical prefix, and a value that changes on every request at the very start invalidates everything after it. Moving the timestamp to the end restores hits. Throttling, a tool-list limit and single-request expiry are not what the diff shows.","ref":"r7"},{"id":"w10-l4-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":2,"q":"Put the prompt segments in the cache-friendly order, most stable first.","items":["System instructions","Tool definitions","Memory","Conversation history","Retrieved chunks","Tool results"],"explain":"Stable segments go first so the longest identical prefix stays cacheable. History grows only at the end, and retrieval and tool results change on every request, so they come last. This matches the lab's cache-friendly order.","ref":"r7"},{"id":"w10-l4-q4","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"q":"A request carries system 1,500 tokens; 80 tool definitions at 500 each; memory 2,000; 40 history turns at 900 each; 6 retrieved chunks at 800 each; tool results 42,000; and 8,000 reserved for output. The window is 128,000. By how many tokens does it overflow?","answer":6300,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"tokens","hint":"Sum every segment, including the reserved output, then subtract the window.","explain":"Tools 40,000, history 36,000, retrieval 4,800. Total = 1,500 + 40,000 + 2,000 + 36,000 + 4,800 + 42,000 + 8,000 = 134,300. Minus 128,000 gives an overflow of 6,300. Forgetting the reserved output would give 126,300 and a false fit, so always include it.","ref":"r6"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r6","r7","r8","r30","r22","r33","r39"],"takeaways":["Context is a finite attention budget. Aim for the smallest set of high-signal tokens, not the largest window.","Quality degrades below the advertised window, so measure at your real lengths.","Clear stale tool results first and keep a pointer, then offload, then compact, then isolate sub-agents.","Keep the prompt prefix stable and append-only. A volatile token at the start destroys cache hits."]},{"id":"w10-l5","order":5,"title":"Memory without regret","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"The assistant should remember things across sessions, but the personalization value, the delete rules, the network posture and the trust in whatever gets written are all undecided. Decide what to store, who may write it and how it is read back.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Memory types, policies and the attack surface","level":2,"id":"memory-types-policies-and-the-attack-surface"},{"t":"table","caption":"CoALA separates working memory from three long-term types [[r28]]. MemGPT adds the idea of virtual context management across memory tiers [[r29]].","head":["Type","What it holds","Typical write","Typical read"],"rows":[["Working","The live context window: task, recent turns, scratchpad","Every step","Always in context"],["Episodic","Specific past events or interactions","After a session, or on an explicit remember command","Retrieve by similarity or recency when a similar situation recurs"],["Semantic","Durable facts about the user, the domain or the world","Extraction from conversations, then consolidation","At session start or per turn"],["Procedural","Rules and how-to steps for recurring workflows","Reviewed edits and learned corrections","Load for the matching workflow"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["**Write policies:** an explicit user command, extraction at the end of a session, or background consolidation that merges duplicates and resolves contradictions.","**Read policies:** load a profile once at session start, retrieve summaries on every turn, or let the agent call a memory tool on demand.","**Foundry Agent Service memory (preview, checked 2026-10-13)** names three types, all enabled by default: user profile (retrieved at conversation start), chat summary (retrieved per turn) and procedural (retrieved for recurring workflows). It works in three phases: extraction, consolidation and retrieval [[r13]].","**Claude's memory tool** is client side: Claude requests file operations under `/memories` and your application executes them, so path traversal protection is your job. Its GA status was not stated on the page when checked [[r41]]."]},{"t":"flow","title":"A write path with gates","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Candidate","md":"The agent or an extractor proposes a memory from the conversation.","tag":"extract"},{"title":"Screen","md":"Scan for injection, PII and policy. Reject instruction-like text.","tag":"guard"},{"title":"Consolidate","md":"Merge duplicates, resolve contradictions, add provenance and a TTL.","tag":"merge"},{"title":"Store with scope","md":"Tenant and user scope on every record, plus a delete handle.","tag":"isolate"},{"title":"Retrieve","md":"Top-k within scope. Show age and source and treat the text as data, not instructions.","tag":"read"}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w10-l5-memory","title":"Design the memory layer","prompt":"Flip the dials. Stateless is on the board because it wins when personalization is worth little.","dials":[{"id":"value","label":"Personalization value","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Low, stateless is fine"},{"id":"high","label":"High, history changes answers"}]},{"id":"privacy","label":"Delete and retention rules","values":[{"id":"relaxed","label":"Relaxed, a default TTL is fine"},{"id":"strict","label":"Strict: delete on request, audit, residency"}]},{"id":"network","label":"Network posture","values":[{"id":"open","label":"Public endpoints allowed"},{"id":"isolated","label":"Private network or VNet required"}]},{"id":"trust","label":"Write source","values":[{"id":"vetted","label":"Vetted staff or users"},{"id":"untrusted","label":"Untrusted users or documents can influence writes"}]}],"options":[{"id":"thread","name":"Thread state only","summary":"No long-term memory."},{"id":"managed","name":"Managed memory (Foundry, preview)","summary":"Extraction, consolidation and retrieval as a service."},{"id":"library","name":"Library store you host","summary":"Mem0 or a LangGraph store on your infrastructure."},{"id":"files","name":"Client-side memory files","summary":"Your app executes file operations on request."},{"id":"bespoke","name":"Bespoke database with review","summary":"Your schema, a validation gate and a review queue."}],"scores":{"thread":{"value":{"low":{"s":2,"why":"No long-term state to secure, delete or poison."},"high":{"s":-2,"why":"Personalization cannot outlive the thread."}},"privacy":{"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"strict":{"s":2,"why":"Nothing persists beyond the thread, so there is little to delete."}},"network":{"open":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"isolated":{"s":1,"why":"No extra service to isolate."}},"trust":{"vetted":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"untrusted":{"s":1,"why":"Nothing to poison."}}},"managed":{"value":{"low":{"s":-1,"why":"Extraction and consolidation cost for little gain."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"User profile, chat summary and procedural memory come built in."}},"privacy":{"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"Default TTL and item-level CRUD cover basic needs."},"strict":{"s":-1,"why":"It is preview. Confirm deletion, key and residency coverage before promising them."}},"network":{"open":{"s":1,"why":"Fits Agent Service as it is."},"isolated":{"s":-2,"why":"VNet integration is not supported as of 2026-10-13."}},"trust":{"vetted":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"untrusted":{"s":-1,"why":"Extraction from untrusted text stores untrusted memories. Add prompt-injection detection and adversarial tests."}}},"library":{"value":{"low":{"s":-1,"why":"You run and secure a store for little gain."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Mem0 reports gains on LOCOMO (vendor-authored), and you own the stack."}},"privacy":{"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"strict":{"s":1,"why":"You control retention, deletion and region."}},"network":{"open":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"isolated":{"s":1,"why":"Self-host inside your own network."}},"trust":{"vetted":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"untrusted":{"s":-1,"why":"Same poisoning exposure, and you build the defences."}}},"files":{"value":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Cheap but unnecessary."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Notes and preferences in files cover many agents."}},"privacy":{"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"strict":{"s":1,"why":"Plain files are easy to inspect, version and delete."}},"network":{"open":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"isolated":{"s":2,"why":"It runs in your app, so there is no extra network surface."}},"trust":{"vetted":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"untrusted":{"s":0,"why":"Your app executes every operation, so it must block path traversal and review writes."}}},"bespoke":{"value":{"low":{"s":-2,"why":"Heavy to build for little gain."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Full control of schema and retrieval."}},"privacy":{"relaxed":{"s":-1,"why":"More machinery than the rules need."},"strict":{"s":2,"why":"Row-level delete, audit, tenant isolation and a review queue by design."}},"network":{"open":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"isolated":{"s":2,"why":"It runs wherever your database runs."}},"trust":{"vetted":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"untrusted":{"s":2,"why":"A validation gate and a review queue sit in front of every write."}}}},"takeaway":"Treat every memory write as untrusted input. The less you trust the writers, the more a gate and a delete path matter."},{"t":"failure","title":"Memory failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"The assistant pushes a recommendation or link no one asked for, across many users","cause":"Poisoned memory. MINJA plants records through ordinary queries with no direct write access [[r23]]. AgentPoison reports above 80% attack success at a poison rate below 0.1% [[r24]].","fix":"Screen every write, keep provenance, treat recalled text as data, and run adversarial tests. Foundry's docs advise Content Safety prompt-injection detection plus adversarial testing [[r13]]."},{"symptom":"A user asks to be forgotten and an old preference still appears","cause":"Derived summaries survive, or records are not tied to a deletable scope","fix":"Scope every record, support item-level delete and TTL, delete derived artifacts and test deletion end to end."},{"symptom":"One tenant's fact appears for another tenant","cause":"Scope missing or shared on retrieval","fix":"Make scope mandatory on every read and write. Foundry's low-level APIs require an explicit `scope`. Test with cross-scope probes."},{"symptom":"The assistant keeps citing an outdated fact","cause":"No consolidation or recency handling, so contradictions pile up","fix":"Consolidate on write, store timestamps, prefer the newest record and expire with a TTL."},{"symptom":"Latency and tokens per turn climb as memory grows","cause":"Too many memories retrieved every turn","fix":"Cap top-k, load the profile once per session and retrieve per-turn summaries only when needed."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Foundry memory limits, preview as of 2026-10-13","md":"100 scopes per store, 10,000 memories per scope, and 1,000 search plus 1,000 update requests per minute. It needs compatible Azure OpenAI chat and embedding deployments and does not support VNet integration. The latest preview added item-level create, read, update and delete, a store default TTL and direct remember-or-forget commands [[r13]]. Mem0 reports 91% lower p95 latency and over 90% token savings against a full-context baseline on LOCOMO, but the paper is vendor-authored [[r40]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-l5-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","difficulty":1,"q":"Match each memory type to what it stores.","pairs":[{"l":"Working memory","r":"The contents of the live context window"},{"l":"Episodic memory","r":"Records of specific past interactions"},{"l":"Semantic memory","r":"Durable facts about a user or a domain"},{"l":"Procedural memory","r":"Rules and how-to steps for recurring workflows"}],"explain":"The CoALA split ties each type to a different read pattern: working memory is always in context, episodic is recalled by situation, semantic by topic and procedural by workflow.","ref":"r28"},{"id":"w10-l5-q2","type":"single","tag":"security","difficulty":3,"q":"Researchers show an attacker can plant malicious memory records through ordinary queries, with no direct write access to the memory store. Which control addresses that path?","options":["Encrypt the memory store at rest with a customer-managed key so the planted records cannot be read","Restrict the memory API so that only administrators can create records, and keep the agent read-only","Screen every write as untrusted input and test adversarially","Raise the embedding dimension so that poisoned records separate from genuine ones"],"answer":2,"explain":"The agent writes memories on the user's behalf, so the attack arrives through normal use. Screening, provenance and adversarial testing address that. Encryption at rest does not stop poisoned content, an admin-only API breaks the agent's own writes without screening them, and a larger embedding does not detect malicious text.","ref":"r23"},{"id":"w10-l5-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"q":"Personalization is valuable, and the security team requires private-network-only access for the agent's data services. Which choice changes, and why?","options":["Managed Foundry memory stays the best choice because it is the only option that includes extraction","A client-side or self-hosted store, since Foundry memory has no VNet support as of 2026-10-13","Fall back to thread state only, because personalization is impossible once the network is isolated","Keep Foundry memory and raise its scope limit"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Foundry memory page lists VNet integration as unsupported in preview, so an isolation requirement moves you to a store you can place inside the network. Thread-only memory gives up the value you wanted, and the scope limit has nothing to do with network posture.","ref":"r13"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r28","r29","r13","r41","r23","r24","r40","r6","r37"],"takeaways":["Name the memory type before you pick a store: working, episodic, semantic or procedural.","Design the write path first: screen, consolidate, scope, then retrieve.","Every write is untrusted input. MINJA and AgentPoison show poisoning without direct write access.","Foundry memory is preview with limits and no VNet support as of 2026-10-13. Isolation or strict deletion rules can flip you to a store you control."]},{"id":"w10-l6","order":6,"title":"Runs that outlive the request","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"A task will run for hours and may wait a day for a human. Run length, tool failure rate, approval delay and whether side effects can safely repeat are all open. Decide where state lives and how a run resumes.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"State outside the process, side effects outside the replay","level":2,"id":"state-outside-the-process-side-effects-outside-the-replay"},{"t":"compare","title":"Where a long run lives","intro":"Anthropic's lesson from production: agents are stateful and errors compound, so resume from where the run was instead of restarting, with retry logic and regular checkpoints [[r2]].","options":[{"name":"In-process loop","summary":"The loop runs in one process and keeps state in memory.","pros":["Simplest to build and debug","No infrastructure"],"cons":["A crash or deploy loses the run","A human wait ties up a worker"],"bestWhen":"Runs last minutes, restarting is cheap and no paid side effects occur.","avoidWhen":"Runs last hours, wait for people or trigger paid actions.","meta":[{"k":"Resume","v":"none"},{"k":"Idle wait cost","v":"a held worker"}]},{"name":"Framework checkpoints","summary":"Write state at defined points and resume from the last one. Agent Framework writes a checkpoint at the end of each superstep, capturing executor state, pending messages, pending requests and responses, and shared state [[r21]]. LangGraph checkpointers store thread-scoped state [[r43]], and interrupt() needs a checkpointer and a thread_id [[r44]].","pros":["Resume without a new platform","Human-in-the-loop pauses are first class"],"cons":["Side effects can still repeat on resume","An in-memory saver loses state on restart"],"bestWhen":"Runs take minutes to hours and you already run the framework.","avoidWhen":"You need deterministic replay or very long timers.","meta":[{"k":"Resume","v":"last checkpoint"},{"k":"Needs","v":"durable store"}]},{"name":"Durable orchestration","tag":"Check status","summary":"Orchestration code is replayed from a history. Temporal runs code as Durable Execution, with deterministic workflow code and side effects in Activities that carry retry policies, timeouts and heartbeats [[r42]]. Agent Framework's Durable Extension targets Azure Functions or your own compute [[r12]].","pros":["Waits cost no compute and no model tokens while idle [[r12]]","Timers can last months","Strong retry and recovery semantics"],"cons":["Orchestration code must be deterministic","More platform to learn and operate","The Durable Extension page states no GA status and installs with a prerelease flag (--prerelease for .NET, --pre for pip) as of 2026-10-13"],"bestWhen":"Runs last hours to days, wait on approvals or trigger paid or irreversible effects.","avoidWhen":"Runs are short and a restart is cheap.","meta":[{"k":"Resume","v":"replay from history"},{"k":"Idle wait cost","v":"near zero"}]},{"name":"Managed asynchronous run","summary":"A platform holds the run. OpenAI background mode lets you poll the response until it leaves queued and in_progress, and cancel it [[r25]]. Anthropic's Managed Agents (beta, header managed-agents-2026-04-01) separates session, harness and sandbox, and a new harness resumes with wake(sessionId) [[r15]].","pros":["Little orchestration code","State and sandbox are managed for you"],"cons":["Retention rules apply: without an explicit store=true a background response is deleted after roughly 10 minutes [[r25]]","Less control over replay and versions"],"bestWhen":"You want long runs without owning the runtime and can accept the vendor's retention and beta status.","avoidWhen":"Data residency, retention or isolation rules need your own control.","meta":[{"k":"Resume","v":"platform defined"},{"k":"Status","v":"background mode has no beta label on its page, Managed Agents beta, as of 2026-10-13"}]}]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-d4","title":"Life of a long-running run","alt":"State diagram: a run moves between running, waiting for a human and failed, and ends as done or stopped.","src":"stateDiagram-v2\n  [*] --> Running\n  Running --> Waiting: approval needed\n  Waiting --> Running: human responds\n  Running --> Failed: crash or tool error\n  Failed --> Running: resume from checkpoint\n  Running --> Stopped: budget hit\n  Running --> Done: goal met\n  Done --> [*]\n  Stopped --> [*]","caption":"Every transition writes state. Waiting costs nothing when the platform parks the run durably."},{"t":"table","caption":"What makes a resume safe depends on the kind of step.","head":["Step type","Safe to repeat?","What makes resume safe","Example"],"rows":[["Read-only call","Yes","Retry with backoff and a cap","Search, fetch a file"],["Idempotent write","Yes, with a key","Client-generated idempotency key reused on retry [[r26]]","Create a ticket"],["Irreversible effect","No","Persist the key and outcome before moving on, and check state before re-issuing","Charge a card, send an email"],["Multi-step change","Only partly","A compensating step registered for each completed step [[r27]]","Book flight, hotel, car"],["Human decision","Not applicable","A durable wait with a timeout and an escalation path","Approve a refund"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"failure","title":"Long-running failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"After a crash the customer is charged twice","cause":"Resume re-ran the step with a new idempotency key","fix":"Generate the key once per logical operation, persist it with the checkpoint and reuse it. Stripe allows up to 255 characters and may prune v1 keys after 24 hours, so after a long wait check the outcome before re-issuing [[r26]]."},{"symptom":"A resumed run behaves differently from the original","cause":"Replay is non-deterministic: orchestration code reads the clock, random numbers or live data","fix":"Keep orchestration deterministic, move side effects into retried activities and replay stored results [[r42]]."},{"symptom":"The agent tries to finish everything in one pass, or declares success early","cause":"Harness failure modes Anthropic saw: one-shotting the job and declaring done too early. Compaction alone was not enough.","fix":"An initializer that writes a progress file and a first git commit, one increment per session, a clean state at the end and tests before marking done [[r14]]."},{"symptom":"Runs vanish or restart from zero after a deploy","cause":"State lives in memory, or versions are replaced in place while runs sit mid-process","fix":"Durable checkpoint store and rainbow deployments, where old and new versions run together while traffic shifts [[r2]]."},{"symptom":"A model change breaks a harness workaround","cause":"Anthropic saw context anxiety on Sonnet 4.5 that disappeared on Opus 4.5, so harness assumptions go stale [[r15]]","fix":"Re-test workarounds on every model change and delete the ones that no longer help."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"A checkpoint is not durable execution","md":"A checkpoint lets a run resume. It does not make a side effect safe to repeat, and it does not make replay deterministic. In Agent Framework for Python, from 1.13.0 an entry checkpoint is also written before the first superstep and when responses to request events arrive, with minor breaking changes [[r21]]. Pair checkpoints with idempotency keys and compensating steps, and put approvals behind a durable wait so nothing idles on a held worker."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-l6-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","difficulty":2,"q":"A payment tool call times out sometimes. What is the bug in this retry loop?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"for attempt in range(3):\n    key = str(uuid4())\n    try:\n        return payments.charge(amount, idempotency_key=key)\n    except Timeout:\n        continue"},"options":["Three attempts are too few for a payment call that may time out repeatedly under heavy load","A uuid4 value is never accepted as an idempotency key by a payment API","A charge call must never be retried once any timeout has been observed","The key is created inside the loop, so a retry after a timeout can charge a second time"],"answer":3,"explain":"An idempotency key only protects a retry if the same key is reused. A fresh key per attempt tells the server each attempt is a new operation. The attempt count is not the defect, random UUIDs are a normal key format, and retrying is fine when the key is stable.","ref":"r26"},{"id":"w10-l6-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":2,"q":"Put the steps of a safe resume after a crash in order.","items":["Load the latest checkpoint for the run","Check whether the pending step's effect already happened","Re-issue the effect with its stored idempotency key if it did not","Continue the loop and write the next checkpoint"],"explain":"State comes first, then a check against the source of truth, then a repeat that is safe because the key is stored. Writing the next checkpoint last records that the step is complete. Re-issuing before checking risks a duplicate once a key has been pruned.","ref":"r26"},{"id":"w10-l6-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"q":"An approval step can take up to 24 hours. Which design wastes the least while it waits?","options":["A durable wait that parks the run and uses no compute or model tokens while idle","A polling loop in the agent process that calls the model every minute to ask whether the human has answered","An HTTP request held open with a 24-hour timeout on a long-lived worker","Restarting the whole run from the beginning when the human finally replies"],"answer":0,"explain":"Durable orchestration parks the run and costs nothing while idle, and Microsoft says a 24-hour approval costs only seconds of execution on Flex Consumption. Polling burns tokens, a held request ties up a worker and breaks on any deploy, and restarting repeats work and side effects.","ref":"r12"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r21","r43","r44","r42","r12","r25","r15","r26","r27","r14"],"takeaways":["Resume, do not restart: state belongs in a durable store, not in process memory.","A checkpoint helps you resume, but side effects still need idempotency keys or compensating steps.","Park human approvals behind a durable wait so idle time costs no compute or tokens.","Check status before promising production use: the Durable Extension states no GA status and Managed Agents is in beta as of 2026-10-13."]},{"id":"w10-l7","order":7,"title":"Boss: crew or soloist? Defend the topology","kind":"boss","minutes":25,"brief":"An interviewer asks whether a research-and-report workload needs several agents and wants the topology. The dials are not given. Clarify them, compare the options, choose, and say what would flip your answer.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The prompt and the five steps","level":2,"id":"the-prompt-and-the-five-steps"},{"t":"scenario","title":"What you know and what to ask","known":["The assistant researches a topic across internal and external sources","It drafts a report and opens follow-up tickets","A teammate proposes a crew of agents"],"ask":["Are the subtasks mostly reads, or do they write a shared artifact?","Can the subtasks be listed before the run?","What is one run worth against its token cost, and how long may the user wait?","Do any parts sit in a different team, tenant or trust zone?","What must a reviewer be able to replay when a run goes wrong?"],"tension":"Parallel reads add breadth. Agents also add tokens, latency and new ways to fail, and they cannot safely share one pen."},{"t":"table","caption":"Dial-and-Defend applied to this question","head":["Step","What you say"],"rows":[["1 Clarify the dials","Read or write coupling, listable subtasks, value per run, latency, trust boundaries, replay needs"],["2 List the options","Single agent, fixed workflow, orchestrator with read-only workers, handoff or Magentic crew"],["3 Pick and name the flips","Orchestrator with read-only workers and one writer, unless value, latency or boundaries flip it"],["4 Failure modes and detection","Loops, unverified success, conflicting writes, context rot, duplicate side effects, injection"],["5 Metrics and rollout","Compare with the single-agent baseline on a golden set, shadow first, pinned versions"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"The sentence that earns points","md":"Say the baseline out loud: the single agent. A crew must beat it by enough to repay roughly an order of magnitude more tokens, and you should say that number is vendor-reported (Anthropic, about 15x chat) and needs measuring on your own task."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-l7-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"q":"Cognition warned against parallel writers on 2025-06-12 and refined its view on 2026-04-22. Which design uses several agents and still respects that refinement?","options":["Several read-only agents contribute findings while one agent performs every write","Three agents edit different sections of one report in parallel, then a final agent merges them","Every agent in a group chat may call the ticket tool when it sees fit","Parallel agents that share only short summaries of their private decisions"],"answer":0,"explain":"The refined view is that several agents can contribute intelligence while writes stay single-threaded. Parallel section edits, a shared ticket tool and summary-only sharing all let actions carry implicit decisions that conflict.","ref":"r4"},{"id":"w10-l7-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"A multi-agent report run finishes quickly and reports success, but a spot check finds two claims with no support in the cited sources. No agent re-read the sources. Which MAST category fits best?","options":["System design issues, because the crew was given too many overlapping roles to coordinate","Inter-agent misalignment, because the workers ignored the lead","Retrieval quality, because the search index returned weak documents","Task verification, because nothing independently checked the result"],"answer":3,"explain":"MAST groups failures into system design, inter-agent misalignment and task verification. Success was declared without an independent check of the output, which is a verification failure. The roles and the workers' obedience are not what the trace shows, and the index quality is untested by this symptom.","ref":"r5"},{"id":"w10-l7-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"q":"Run value is low, users want answers in seconds, and the team still wants a crew. What is the defensible recommendation?","options":["Add Magentic so a manager can re-plan whenever latency grows","Keep the crew but run every subagent synchronously to avoid races between them","Use a single agent or a fixed workflow, and revisit when value per run or breadth rises","Use a group chat with a maker-checker loop to raise quality before worrying about speed or cost"],"answer":2,"explain":"Multi-agent systems used about 15x the tokens of chat in Anthropic's data and add hops, so low value and a tight latency budget favour the lowest rung. Magentic is slow and untested outside its design, synchronous subagents were a bottleneck for Anthropic, and a review loop adds more turns.","ref":"r2"},{"id":"w10-l7-q4","type":"multi","tag":"security","difficulty":3,"q":"Select every control that shrinks the blast radius of the research crew.","options":["Read-only workers with least-privilege tool allow-lists","Ticket creation behind human approval with an idempotency key","One shared scratchpad that every worker can write to","The same broad credentials for every worker to simplify setup","Treating retrieved page text as data, never as instructions"],"answers":[0,1,4],"explain":"Read-only workers, an approval gate with an idempotency key and treating retrieved text as data all limit what a compromised or confused agent can do. A shared writable scratchpad is a path for one worker's injected text to reach the others, and shared broad credentials turn one compromise into all of them.","ref":"r19"}],"defend":{"prompt":"We want an assistant that researches a topic across internal and external sources, drafts a report and opens follow-up tickets. A teammate wants a crew of agents. Do we need multiple agents? Walk me through the topology you would build.","timebox":"5 min","clarify":["Are the subtasks mostly reads, or do several of them write the same artifact?","Can the subtasks be listed before the run, or does the input decide them?","What is one run worth against its token cost, and how long may the user wait?","Do any parts run under different teams, tenants or trust zones?","What must a reviewer be able to replay when a run goes wrong?"],"options":[{"name":"Single agent with tools and context hygiene","when":"Reads are modest, value per run is moderate and one team owns the whole flow.","tradeoff":"One window carries every result, so rot risk grows, and tool calls run one after another."},{"name":"Fixed workflow: route, parallel gather, single writer, gate","when":"The steps are known in advance and latency matters.","tradeoff":"It cannot add a step the designer did not foresee."},{"name":"Orchestrator with read-only workers and one writer","when":"Breadth-first reads with high value per run and a background latency budget.","tradeoff":"About 15x chat tokens in Anthropic's data (vendor-reported), and the lead becomes a bottleneck."},{"name":"Handoff mesh or Magentic crew","when":"Separate owners or trust zones, or open-ended work where a plan ledger is worth the wait.","tradeoff":"Bouncing, stalls and cost. Microsoft says Magentic's performance outside the original design is untested."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** Before naming a topology I would ask five things. Are the subtasks reads, or do they write a shared artifact? Can I list them in advance? What is a run worth against its token cost, and how long can the user wait? Does any part sit in a different team, tenant or trust zone? What must a reviewer replay when a run fails?"},{"t":"table","head":["Option","Pick when","Loses when"],"rows":[["Single agent, clean context","Moderate reads, moderate value","Reads are broad and the window fills"],["Fixed workflow","Steps known, latency tight","Inputs decide the steps"],["Orchestrator, read-only workers, one writer","Breadth-first reads, high value","Value is low or the user waits in seconds"],["Handoff mesh or Magentic","Separate owners, open-ended planning","Latency matters or the path is deterministic"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**2 and 3. Options and the pick.** I would start from the single-agent baseline and only climb when a dial forces me. Assume the common case: independent source reads, one coupled write (a single report plus a few tickets), one owning team and a background run of minutes with enough value to justify spend. I would then build an orchestrator with read-only workers. A router classifies the topic, a lead plans the research, workers read in parallel in clean windows and return short cited results, and one writer drafts the report. A reviewer with a clean context checks the draft against the cited sources. Ticket creation waits for human approval and uses an idempotency key."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-d5","title":"Recommended topology","alt":"A question goes to a router and a lead, two read-only workers return results to the lead, then a single writer, a clean-context reviewer, human approval and the ticket tool.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Question] --> R[Router]\n  R --> L[Lead plans]\n  L --> W1[Reader A]\n  L --> W2[Reader B]\n  W1 --> L\n  W2 --> L\n  L --> W[Single writer]\n  W --> C[Clean-context reviewer]\n  C --> H[Human approval]\n  H --> T[Ticket tool]"},{"t":"p","md":"**Why not the others.** I would not use a handoff mesh, because no specialist needs to own the conversation. I would not use Magentic, because nothing needs a plan ledger and Microsoft says its performance outside the original design is untested. Writers stay single-threaded, which matches Cognition's 2025-06-12 and 2026-04-22 findings."},{"t":"p","md":"**4. Failure modes and how I would detect them.**"},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["**Unverified success** (MAST task verification): reviewer rejection rate and the share of claims without a supporting citation.","**Runaway loops and cost:** stop-reason mix, steps and tokens per run, repeated-call alerts, hard caps in the harness.","**Conflicting or lossy results from workers:** compare worker outputs with the report's citations, and use a brief with objective, output format, tools and boundaries.","**Context rot in the lead:** quality by input length in evals, with tool result clearing and a notes file.","**Duplicate side effects after a retry:** duplicate-key counter, stored idempotency keys and a check of ticket state before re-issuing.","**Injection through fetched pages:** workers are read-only, hold no secrets and return text that the lead treats as data."]},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Metrics and rollout.** I would track task success and citation precision on a golden set, tokens and wall-clock time per run against the single-agent baseline, the stop-reason mix, reviewer rejection rate and the human override rate on tickets. The crew has to beat the baseline by enough to repay the extra tokens, so I would run both on the same set and report variance across repeated runs. Rollout is shadow mode first, then a small cohort, with prompt, model and tool versions pinned together and rainbow deployments so running jobs finish on the version that started them."},{"t":"p","md":"**What would flip this.** If research is shallow and a few sources matter, I collapse to a single agent with tool result clearing, because multi-agent runs used about 15x chat tokens in Anthropic's data. If parts must run under different identities or teams, I keep the workers but give each its own identity and tool allow-list, or hand off at the boundary. If users need answers in seconds, I drop the planner and use a fixed gather step. If someone asks for several agents to write the report in parallel, I decline and keep one writer. If the plan itself must stay open-ended and high value, I would consider Magentic, but only after measuring it against the baseline."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Label the evidence","md":"Anthropic's 90.2% gain, its 4x and 15x token multipliers and Cognition's review numbers are vendor-reported on their own tasks. Quote them as such and replicate on your workload."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about read or write coupling, listable subtasks, value per run, latency, trust boundaries and replay before naming a topology","w":3},{"pt":"Offers at least three options including the single-agent baseline","w":2},{"pt":"Picks for the stated dials: read-only workers in parallel and exactly one writer","w":3},{"pt":"Names flip conditions: low value, tight latency, a trust boundary, a request for parallel writers","w":3},{"pt":"Names failure modes with detection signals, including unverified success and loops","w":2},{"pt":"Puts brakes and side-effect safety in the harness: budgets, stop reasons, approval and idempotency keys","w":2},{"pt":"Names metrics, compares with a single-agent baseline and proposes a staged rollout with pinned versions","w":2},{"pt":"Cites the evidence accurately and labels vendor-reported numbers","w":1}],"followUps":[{"q":"The lead's context fills up after 30 workers report. What do you change?","a":"Workers return short structured results and hand back artifacts by path or id. The lead clears stale tool results and keeps a notes file. Measure quality by input length, because rot appears below the advertised window."},{"q":"Security says one worker must never see another worker's data. What follows?","a":"Separate identities and tool allow-lists per worker, scoped memory per tenant and no shared scratchpad. The lead receives only permitted outputs. This is a trust boundary dial that can justify splitting even at higher cost."},{"q":"How would you show the crew beats the single agent?","a":"Same golden set and budget class, comparing success, citation precision, cost and time, with variance over repeated runs. Anthropic's 90.2% came from an internal eval on breadth-first queries, so replicate it before trusting it."},{"q":"The writer finishes, then the process dies before tickets are created. How do you resume?","a":"Resume from the last checkpoint, check which tickets exist, re-issue the rest with the stored idempotency keys and register compensating steps for partial changes."}],"redFlags":["Recommends a multi-agent framework before asking about the task structure","Lets several agents write the same report in parallel","Relies on a prompt line to stop loops instead of harness budgets","Quotes vendor multipliers or gains as universal facts","Treats retrieved pages or memory as trusted instructions"]},"refs":["r2","r3","r4","r5","r9","r10","r19","r35"],"takeaways":["Name the single-agent baseline first and climb only when a dial you can state forces it.","Parallelize reads, serialize writes: read-only workers plus one writer survive both Anthropic's and Cognition's evidence.","Put brakes, approvals and idempotency keys in the harness, not in the prompt.","Label vendor-reported numbers, and measure the crew against the baseline on your own golden set."]}],"topics":[{"id":"w10-t01","order":1,"group":"Workflow patterns","title":"Workflows vs agents: the autonomy dial","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A workflow keeps the control flow in your code. An agent lets the model choose its own next step. Both are agentic systems, and the autonomy dial sets how much control the model gets.","useIf":"You are deciding how much control a model gets before you pick any framework: start at the left of the dial and climb only for a reason you can name.","avoidIf":"Do not give a free loop to a task whose steps are known, and do not script an open-ended task that needs the model to adapt.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Anthropic splits agentic systems in two [[r1]]. **Workflows** orchestrate LLMs and tools through predefined code paths. **Agents** are systems where the LLM dynamically directs its own process and tool use. Autonomy is a dial, not a switch: more autonomy buys flexibility on open-ended tasks and costs latency, tokens and predictability. Anthropic's advice is to find the simplest solution that works, because agentic systems trade latency and cost for task performance [[r1]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t01-d1","title":"The autonomy dial","alt":"Four steps from a direct model call, through a workflow and a single agent, to multi-agent orchestration. Control moves from code to the model.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Direct model call] --> B[Workflow: code owns the path]\n  B --> C[Single agent: model owns the path]\n  C --> D[Multi-agent orchestration]","caption":"Each step right adds flexibility, latency, cost and failure modes."},{"t":"p","md":"The Azure Architecture Center uses the same ladder with three levels: direct model call, single agent with tools, multiagent orchestration. Its rule is to use the lowest level of complexity that reliably meets your requirements. It calls a single agent with tools often the right default for enterprise use cases and tells you to set iteration limits against infinite tool-call loops [[r9]]. OpenAI's guide agrees: maximize a single agent's capabilities first, then split [[r19]]."},{"t":"h","text":"Questions that place a task on the dial","level":3,"id":"questions-that-place-a-task-on-the-dial"},{"t":"list","style":"checks","items":["Who decides the next step: your code or the model?","Are the steps known before the run starts?","What does a wrong action cost, and can it be undone?","What latency and token budget does one run get?","Can you evaluate the result well enough to trust a loop?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Read the date on the source","md":"Anthropic's post is dated 2024-12-19. A later banner (checked 2026-10-13) says much of the tooling landscape has changed and points to Claude Managed Agents. The vocabulary still holds; do not copy its tool advice blindly [[r1]]."}],"whereToUse":["First design review of any feature that needs more than one model call","Choosing between a hard-coded pipeline and an autonomous tool loop","Explaining to stakeholders why a feature ships as a workflow and not as an agent","Setting the review bar: more autonomy needs stronger evals, brakes and approvals","Hardening a prototype agent into a workflow once its paths stabilise"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Steps are known in advance: use a workflow","The next step depends on what tools return and cannot be listed up front: use an agent","Per-run value is high enough to pay for roughly 4x the tokens of chat that agents used in Anthropic's data [[r2]]"],"avoid":["Picking an agent because it sounds modern when a three-step chain meets the requirement","Giving an agent write tools before you have brakes, approvals and an evaluation set","Building a workflow with dozens of branches that only a model could navigate"],"flips":["The workflow keeps gaining branches: move that branch into a bounded agent loop","Agent traces follow the same order in almost every run: freeze that path as a workflow","Per-run stakes rise: add approvals or drop back toward the workflow end"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Rungs of the dial","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Direct model call","One pass can do it: classify, summarise, extract","The task needs fresh data or dependent steps","Lowest cost and latency"],["Workflow (chain, route, parallelise)","Steps are known, volume is high, an audit trail is needed","Inputs vary so much that branches explode","Predictable and cheap to test, brittle on unplanned cases"],["Single agent with tools","The path depends on tool results inside one domain","Latency is tight or actions are costly to repeat","About 4x chat tokens in Anthropic's data, loops need brakes [[r2]]"],["Multi-agent orchestration","Breadth-first reads, parallel work, separate trust boundaries","Subtasks are tightly coupled or value per run is low","About 15x chat tokens [[r2]], coordination failures [[r5]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A prototype agent takes 40 seconds and costs several times the plan","cause":"A fixed three-step task was given a free loop","fix":"Replace the loop with a chain, keep one model call per step, and compare latency and tokens per run before and after."},{"symptom":"The workflow needs a new branch every week","cause":"The task is open-ended but was scripted","fix":"Move the open-ended step into a bounded agent loop inside the workflow and keep the rest deterministic."},{"symptom":"Nobody can say why a run did what it did","cause":"Autonomy was added without traces or stop reasons","fix":"Log every step, tool call and stop reason before you raise autonomy."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t01-s1","title":"High volume, known steps","situation":"A team processes thousands of inbound requests a day. Every request passes the same four checks, and an auditor wants to see each step.","constraints":["Steps are known and stable","Volume is high and cost per run matters","An audit trail is required"],"unknowns":["How often does a request need a step outside the four?","What is the latency target per request?","Can a failed check be retried on its own?"],"question":"Which rung of the dial do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Workflow with one model call per check","verdict":"best","why":"The path is known, so code owns it. Each step is testable, logged and cheap, which matches the audit need.","tradeoff":"A request that needs a fifth check has to be added by a developer."},{"name":"Single agent with the four checks as tools","verdict":"good","why":"It works and adapts to odd requests.","tradeoff":"Order is no longer guaranteed, token use and latency rise, and the audit trail becomes a trace you must interpret."},{"name":"Multi-agent team, one agent per check","verdict":"poor","why":"It multiplies tokens and adds coordination for steps that never need a decision.","tradeoff":"Right only when each check needs its own tools, owner or trust boundary."}],"flips":[{"if":"Twenty percent of requests need ad hoc investigation","then":"Keep the workflow and add a bounded agent branch for the odd cases."},{"if":"Volume drops to a few per day and each is high value","then":"A single agent becomes affordable and the flexibility pays."}],"takeaway":"Known steps belong in code. Spend autonomy where the path is unknown."},{"id":"w10-t01-s2","title":"Unpredictable tools, low volume","situation":"An internal assistant answers occasional hard questions by combining search, a database and a calculator. Which tool comes next depends on what the last one returned. Each run saves an expert time.","constraints":["The path depends on tool results","A few hundred runs a day with high value per run","Tools are read-only, so a wrong step is cheap"],"unknowns":["Is there a latency target, or may a run take a minute?","Can you evaluate answers against a golden set?"],"question":"Which rung do you choose, and what do you add to it?","options":[{"name":"Single agent with tools plus a step cap and budget","verdict":"best","why":"The model has to choose the next tool from results, and the value per run covers the extra tokens. Read-only tools keep the blast radius small.","tradeoff":"Latency and cost vary per run, and you must evaluate trajectories, not only final answers."},{"name":"Fixed workflow with a branch per tool order","verdict":"good","why":"Predictable and testable for the orders you listed.","tradeoff":"Branches multiply and unlisted orders fail. It becomes right once the paths settle into a few stable ones."},{"name":"Multi-agent crew with a planner and three workers","verdict":"poor","why":"Nothing shows the single agent fails, and the crew costs far more tokens and adds handoff faults.","tradeoff":"Right when one agent's tool list overloads it or the work splits into independent reads."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tools gain write access to production data","then":"Add approval gates, or move the writing steps into a workflow."},{"if":"A voice interface sets a two second latency target","then":"Pre-fetch with a workflow and keep the agent loop off the hot path."}],"takeaway":"Climb one rung at a time and name the dial that justified the climb."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t01-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which statement matches the workflow versus agent split in Anthropic's definitions?","options":["A workflow uses one model call and an agent uses several","Workflows use tools and agents do not","An agent is a workflow that runs longer than 60 seconds","In a workflow, code paths are predefined, and in an agent the LLM directs its own process and tool use"],"answer":3,"explain":"The split is about who owns the control flow, not call count, tool use or duration. A workflow can call a model many times and use tools; an agent can finish in one call. [[r1]]","ref":"r1","difficulty":1},{"id":"w10-t01-q2","type":"multi","tag":"flip","q":"Which two observations justify moving a design up from a workflow toward an agent?","options":["The next step depends on what the previous tool returned and cannot be listed in advance","The team wants the system to look more advanced","The workflow gains a new branch every week for inputs nobody predicted","Volume has doubled and cost per run matters more"],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"Unpredictable paths and endless new branches are the dials that favour a model-owned path. Appearance is not a dial, and higher volume with tighter cost pushes the other way, toward the workflow end. [[r9]]","ref":"r9","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Workflow vs agent in one line each?","back":"Workflow: LLMs and tools run through predefined code paths. Agent: the LLM dynamically directs its own process and tool use [[r1]]."},{"front":"What does Microsoft's rule for complexity say?","back":"Use the lowest level of complexity that reliably meets your requirements. A single agent with tools is often the right enterprise default [[r9]]."},{"front":"What do agents and multi-agent systems cost in tokens, per Anthropic?","back":"About 4x chat for agents and about 15x for multi-agent systems, vendor-reported in Anthropic's own traffic [[r2]]."}],"related":["w10-l1","w10-t02","w10-t07","w10-t11"],"refs":["r1","r9","r19","r2","r5"]},{"id":"w10-t02","order":2,"group":"Workflow patterns","title":"Prompt chaining","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Split a task into fixed steps. Each model call works on the previous output, and a programmatic gate between steps checks it before the chain continues.","useIf":"The task splits cleanly into known sequential subtasks and you would rather trade some latency for a more accurate result per step.","avoidIf":"Steps are independent (run them in parallel), the path depends on intermediate results, or an early error cannot be caught before later steps build on it.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"In **prompt chaining** a task is decomposed into a fixed sequence of subtasks. Each LLM call processes the output of the previous one, and you can add a programmatic **gate** between steps to check the work is on track. The pattern trades latency for accuracy by making each call an easier task [[r1]]. The Azure Architecture Center calls the same shape sequential orchestration, also known as a pipeline or linear delegation, and notes that which step runs next is defined by the workflow, not chosen by an agent [[r9]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t02-d1","title":"A chain with gates","alt":"Input flows through draft, gate, refine and gate to the output. A failed gate retries the previous step or stops with an error.","src":"flowchart LR\n  I[Input] --> S1[Step 1: draft]\n  S1 --> G1{Gate}\n  G1 -->|pass| S2[Step 2: refine]\n  G1 -->|fail| R[Retry or stop]\n  S2 --> G2{Gate}\n  G2 -->|pass| O[Output]\n  G2 -->|fail| R"},{"t":"p","md":"A gate is cheap code, not another opinion: schema validation, length and format checks, a regex, a lookup, or a small classifier. Because every intermediate result is a stored value, you can retry only the failing step and you can see which step broke. The costs are additive latency (the sum of every step) and compounding error: if each step is right 95 percent of the time and errors are independent, five steps give 0.95^5, about 77 percent, before gates catch anything."},{"t":"formula","title":"Chain latency and reliability","expr":"latency = sum(step_latency) + sum(gate_latency)     end_to_end_ok = product(step_ok)","vars":[{"k":"step_ok","v":"probability a step is acceptable, assuming independent steps (an illustration, not a measured value)"}],"example":"Four steps of 2.5 s each plus four 0.05 s gates: **10.2 s**. Five steps at 0.95 each: **0.774**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Pass less forward","md":"Hand each step only what it needs, ideally as a structured object. Passing the whole transcript down the chain grows input tokens at every hop and hides the contract between steps."}],"whereToUse":["Draft, check, then polish flows such as outline, validate, write","Extract, normalise, then enrich pipelines where each stage needs the previous result","Generate a document, then translate it or reformat it","Any stage that a cheap validator can approve before an expensive stage runs","Replacing one overloaded mega-prompt that mixes several jobs"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Steps are known, ordered and dependent on each other","A deterministic gate can catch a bad intermediate result","A single prompt does several jobs and quality is uneven across them"],"avoid":["Stages are independent and could run in parallel","The work needs backtracking or iteration over intermediate results","A single well-written call already meets quality, so extra calls only add latency"],"flips":["The next step depends on content: switch to routing or orchestrator-workers","Latency is tight and steps are independent: parallelise them","A gate cannot be written because quality has no check: consider evaluator-optimizer with an external signal"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Prompt chain (this topic)","Fixed dependent steps, gates available","Path varies by input, or stages are independent","Latency adds up, errors compound without gates"],["Single mega-prompt","Task is small and one pass is enough","One call mixes several jobs and quality drops","Cheapest, hardest to debug"],["Parallelisation","Subtasks are independent","A step needs the previous output","Token cost multiplies"],["Routing","Inputs fall into distinct categories","Every input needs the same steps","Misroute risk"],["Agent loop","Steps cannot be listed in advance","A fixed order would do","Variable cost, needs brakes"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A bad first step produces a confident but wrong final answer","cause":"No gate between steps, so error flows forward [[r9]]","fix":"Add a deterministic check after each risky step and stop or retry on failure."},{"symptom":"Step three fails to parse step two's output","cause":"Free-text hand-offs between steps","fix":"Pass structured output with a schema and validate it at the gate."},{"symptom":"Latency is far above the target","cause":"Independent stages were chained sequentially","fix":"Run independent stages in parallel and chain only the dependent ones."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t02-s1","title":"Bulk generation with a format contract","situation":"A pipeline turns raw records into customer-facing text, then translates it. Output must match a strict format, and bad records must never reach the translation step. Volume is high and runs in batch.","constraints":["Steps are fixed and dependent","A format check is cheap to write","Batch run, so added latency is acceptable"],"unknowns":["What share of drafts fails the format check?","Is a rewrite allowed, or must a failure go to a person?"],"question":"How do you structure the calls?","options":[{"name":"Chain: generate, validate with code, translate","verdict":"best","why":"The gate stops bad records before the costly step and each stage is testable on its own.","tradeoff":"Two sequential calls per record, and the gate needs maintenance when the format changes."},{"name":"One prompt that generates and translates together","verdict":"good","why":"Half the calls and simple to run.","tradeoff":"You cannot gate the intermediate text, so a bad draft is translated and found late."},{"name":"Agent that decides which steps to run","verdict":"poor","why":"The order never varies, so the loop adds cost and nondeterminism.","tradeoff":"Right only if some records need entirely different handling."}],"flips":[{"if":"Records fall into very different types that need different drafts","then":"Add a router before the chain."},{"if":"Drafts and translations for many languages are independent","then":"Fan the translation step out in parallel."}],"takeaway":"A gate turns a chain from a pipe into a quality control line."},{"id":"w10-t02-s2","title":"Interactive, latency sensitive","situation":"A user waits in a chat window for an answer that currently takes four sequential calls of about two seconds each. Quality is fine, but the product team wants a faster reply.","constraints":["Interactive use with a latency target near four seconds","Two of the four steps do not depend on each other","Quality must not drop"],"unknowns":["Which steps depend on which?","Can any step use a smaller, faster model?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Run the independent steps in parallel and keep the dependent ones chained","verdict":"best","why":"It cuts wall-clock time to roughly three calls without changing what each step does.","tradeoff":"Token use stays the same but peak rate-limit pressure rises, and the merge step needs care."},{"name":"Collapse all four steps into one prompt","verdict":"poor","why":"It saves time but gives up the per-step quality that made the chain accurate.","tradeoff":"Right when evals show the single prompt matches quality for this task."},{"name":"Stream the final step and leave the rest","verdict":"good","why":"Perceived latency improves because text appears early.","tradeoff":"Total time to the first token is unchanged, since the final step still waits for three others."}],"flips":[{"if":"A smaller model passes evals on the early steps","then":"Swap models there first, it shortens every call."},{"if":"All four steps are dependent","then":"Parallelism is not available, so shrink prompts or use a faster model."}],"takeaway":"Chain only what depends on what. Everything else can run side by side."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t02-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What is the job of a gate between two steps of a chain?","options":["To check an intermediate result with code before the next step runs","To let the model decide whether to continue","To merge outputs from parallel branches","To increase the temperature of the next call"],"answer":0,"explain":"A gate is a programmatic check on the intermediate output. Letting the model decide would move control flow back to the model, merging is aggregation in parallelisation, and temperature is unrelated. [[r1]]","ref":"r1","difficulty":1},{"id":"w10-t02-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A chain has five steps. Each step produces an acceptable result 95 percent of the time, independently, and there are no gates. What percentage of runs end acceptable?","answer":77.4,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"%","hint":"Multiply the per-step probabilities.","explain":"0.95^5 = 0.7738, about 77.4 percent. Independence is an assumption: gates and retries raise the figure, and correlated errors can lower it."},{"id":"w10-t02-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which change should push you from a prompt chain to routing?","options":["Every request needs the same four steps","The gates are failing too often","Requests fall into distinct categories that need different steps","Latency is above the target"],"answer":2,"explain":"Distinct categories with different handling is the routing dial. A fixed order for everyone is the chain's home case, failing gates call for better prompts or checks, and latency alone suggests parallelisation. [[r1]]","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does prompt chaining trade for accuracy?","back":"Latency. Each call becomes an easier task, but total time is the sum of the steps plus gates [[r1]]."},{"front":"What is another name for prompt chaining in Azure's pattern guide?","back":"Sequential orchestration, also called a pipeline or linear delegation. The workflow, not an agent, picks the next step [[r9]]."},{"front":"Why can a five-step chain at 95 percent per step still be unreliable?","back":"0.95^5 is about 77 percent under independence. Gates between steps stop errors from compounding."}],"related":["w10-l1","w10-t01","w10-t03","w10-t04","w10-t06"],"refs":["r1","r9"]},{"id":"w10-t03","order":3,"group":"Workflow patterns","title":"Routing","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Classify the input first, then send it down a specialised path: a different prompt, tool set, model or agent for each category.","useIf":"Inputs fall into distinct categories that are better handled separately, and a classifier can tell them apart with acceptable accuracy.","avoidIf":"Every input needs the same handling, categories overlap heavily, or a wrong route is costly and you cannot detect it.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Routing** classifies an input and directs it to a specialised follow-up task, which lets you separate concerns and tune each path on its own. Anthropic gives two examples: sending different customer-service query types down different paths, and sending easy questions to a smaller model and hard ones to a larger model to save cost [[r1]]. The router does not do the work. It only decides who does. Keep the label set small and the category definitions mutually exclusive, so the classifier has one clear answer for each input."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t03-d1","title":"A router with a fallback","alt":"Input goes to a router. High confidence routes go to path A or B. Low confidence goes to a general fallback path.","src":"flowchart LR\n  I[Input] --> R{Router}\n  R -->|billing| A[Billing path]\n  R -->|technical| B[Technical path]\n  R -->|low confidence| F[General fallback]\n  A --> O[Answer]\n  B --> O\n  F --> O"},{"t":"p","md":"The router can be rules or keywords, an embedding similarity lookup, a small LLM classifier, or a trained model. RouteLLM trains routers on preference data to choose between a stronger and a weaker model, and reports cost reductions of over 2x in certain cases without losing response quality [[tr9]]. Whatever you use, plan for the miss: a confidence threshold, a safe default path, and a log of every route so you can build a confusion matrix against labelled traffic."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Router versus handoff","md":"A router classifies once and steps aside. In a handoff the agents themselves decide to transfer control and may bounce. If the right path is identifiable from the first input, Microsoft advises deterministic routing or a simple dispatcher that does not take an active role, instead of a handoff mesh [[r9]]."}],"whereToUse":["Customer-service flows with distinct request types, each with its own tools and policy","Model cascades that send easy queries to a small model and hard ones to a large one","Choosing between retrieval corpora, SQL, and a plain chat path for a question","Language or locale specific prompts and guardrails","Front door of a multi-agent system that dispatches to specialists"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Categories are distinct and each benefits from its own prompt, tools or model","A cheap classifier reaches high accuracy on labelled traffic","Cost or latency differs a lot between easy and hard inputs"],"avoid":["Categories overlap so heavily that routing accuracy stays low","A misroute is costly and silent, and nothing downstream can detect it","One prompt already handles all inputs well"],"flips":["Misroutes are common and expensive: add a confidence threshold and a clarifying question or general fallback","Routing becomes dynamic during the conversation: consider handoff","The router costs more than it saves: collapse the paths back into one"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Router types and neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Rules or keywords","Categories are crisp and stable","Wording varies widely","Free and fast, brittle"],["Embedding similarity","Labelled examples per category exist","Categories are subtle or new","Needs example upkeep"],["LLM classifier","Categories need judgement","Volume is high and latency tight","One extra call per request"],["Trained router (for model choice)","You have preference or outcome data","Traffic shifts away from training data","Training and drift monitoring [[tr9]]"],["Handoff between agents","The right specialist emerges mid-task","Route is clear at the start","Loops and bouncing [[r9]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Hard questions get shallow answers","cause":"The router sent them to the small model","fix":"Add an escalation rule: low confidence or a failed check retries on the large model."},{"symptom":"Quality drops only for one request type","cause":"That type is split across two categories with unclear boundaries","fix":"Merge or redefine categories, then measure per-route accuracy on labelled traffic."},{"symptom":"Routing accuracy decays over months","cause":"New request types appeared that no category covers","fix":"Route unknowns to a fallback, sample them for review and add categories deliberately."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t03-s1","title":"Cost cascade at scale","situation":"A high-volume assistant uses one large model for everything. Most queries are simple lookups, and the monthly bill is the main complaint.","constraints":["Large share of easy queries","Cost is the pressure, latency is secondary","A wrong answer on a hard query is visible to the user"],"unknowns":["How are easy and hard defined and labelled?","Can the system detect a weak answer and retry?"],"question":"How do you route?","options":[{"name":"Router to a small model by default, with escalation to the large model on low confidence or a failed check","verdict":"best","why":"It captures most of the savings and has a safety net for the hard tail.","tradeoff":"Extra classification cost and some added latency on escalated queries."},{"name":"Static split by query length","verdict":"poor","why":"Length is a weak proxy: short questions can be hard and long ones easy.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only after you prove length predicts difficulty on your traffic."},{"name":"Keep the large model for all queries","verdict":"good","why":"Highest quality and no routing risk.","tradeoff":"You keep paying for capability most queries do not need."}],"flips":[{"if":"The small model matches the large one on your golden set","then":"Drop the router and use the small model alone."},{"if":"Wrong answers carry legal or safety risk","then":"Route by risk class and keep the large model plus a check on the high-risk class."}],"takeaway":"A router saves money only with a measured miss path."},{"id":"w10-t03-s2","title":"Distinct domains, costly misroutes","situation":"An assistant fronts three tool sets: account changes, product questions and general chat. Account changes move money, and users phrase requests loosely.","constraints":["Three domains with different tools and permissions","A misroute to account changes can trigger a real action","Users write ambiguous requests"],"unknowns":["What does a wrong route cost in each direction?","Is a clarifying question acceptable to the user?"],"question":"How do you design the front door?","options":[{"name":"LLM router with a confidence threshold, a clarifying question below it, and approval on account actions","verdict":"best","why":"Ambiguity is expected, so the design asks instead of guessing, and the costly path has its own gate.","tradeoff":"More turns for unclear requests and one extra call per request."},{"name":"Keyword rules only","verdict":"poor","why":"Loose phrasing defeats keywords and the failure is silent on a money path.","tradeoff":"Right for crisp inputs such as slash commands or form fields."},{"name":"One agent with all tools and no router","verdict":"good","why":"Simplest to build and handles ambiguity through conversation.","tradeoff":"Tool overload, broad permissions and a larger blast radius for one prompt injection."}],"flips":[{"if":"Requests arrive through a form with a category field","then":"Skip the classifier and route on the field."},{"if":"Specialists need to take over a long conversation","then":"Use handoff so the specialist converses with the user."}],"takeaway":"Route on confidence, and make the expensive path harder to reach."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t03-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A router sends 70 percent of traffic to a small model. What must you add to protect answer quality on the remaining hard queries?","options":["A larger context window for the small model","More examples in the router prompt only","A second router that votes with the first","An escalation path on low confidence or a failed check"],"answer":3,"explain":"The savings depend on sending the hard tail to the stronger model, so you need a measured miss path. A bigger window does not add capability, more router examples do not catch downstream failure, and a second router adds cost without a quality check on the answer.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t03-q2","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Per-route accuracy is high on test data, but users report wrong answers for one request type. Which are plausible causes?","options":["That request type straddles two categories","A new request type appears in production that no category covers","The router temperature is set to zero","Production wording differs from the labelled examples"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Overlapping categories, uncovered types and distribution shift all hide behind good test scores. A temperature of zero makes routing more repeatable, not less accurate by itself.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What two examples does Anthropic give for routing?","back":"Different customer-service query types down different paths, and easy questions to a smaller model with hard ones to a larger model [[r1]]."},{"front":"What three things should every router have?","back":"A confidence threshold, a safe fallback path and a route log to measure per-route accuracy."},{"front":"Router versus handoff?","back":"A router classifies once and steps aside. Handoff lets agents transfer control during the task and can loop [[r9]][[r11]]."}],"related":["w10-l1","w10-t01","w10-t13","w10-t12"],"refs":["r1","r9","r11","tr9"]},{"id":"w10-t04","order":4,"group":"Workflow patterns","title":"Parallelization","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Run several model calls at the same time and combine the results. Sectioning splits a task into independent parts. Voting repeats one task to get diverse answers.","useIf":"Subtasks are independent, or you want several independent attempts at the same question, and cost and rate limits allow the extra calls.","avoidIf":"Branches need each other's output, share state they could corrupt, or you have no rule for combining conflicting results.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Anthropic describes two variations of **parallelization** [[r1]]. In **sectioning** you break a task into independent subtasks that run at once, for example screening a request for policy problems in one call while another call answers it. In **voting** you run the same task several times and combine the outputs, for example several reviewers checking code for vulnerabilities. Microsoft's concurrent orchestration is the same fan-out and fan-in shape, also called scatter-gather or map-reduce, and its documentation lists brainstorming, ensemble reasoning and quorum voting as fits [[r9]][[tr2]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t04-d1","title":"Fan-out and fan-in","alt":"One input fans out to three parallel branches. An aggregator merges their results into one output.","src":"flowchart LR\n  I[Input] --> A[Branch A]\n  I --> B[Branch B]\n  I --> C[Branch C]\n  A --> G[Aggregate]\n  B --> G\n  C --> G\n  G --> O[Output]"},{"t":"formula","title":"Latency and cost of a fan-out","expr":"latency = max(branch_latency) + aggregate_latency     cost = sum(branch_cost) + aggregate_cost","vars":[{"k":"aggregate","v":"code or an LLM call that merges, votes or ranks the branch outputs"}],"example":"Branches of 4 s, 6 s and 5 s plus a 2 s merge take **8 s**, against 17 s in sequence. The token bill is still the sum of all three."},{"t":"p","md":"Voting has a research root: self-consistency samples several reasoning paths and takes the most consistent answer, and the paper reports gains such as +17.9 percent on GSM8K over greedy chain-of-thought [[tr5]]. The catch is that you pay for every vote, and votes from the same model share its blind spots. Sectioning needs a clean boundary: if two branches edit the same artifact or touch the same external system, they need a conflict rule, or they should not run in parallel [[r9]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Check quota before you fan out","md":"Parallel branches hit tokens-per-minute and requests-per-minute limits together. Cap concurrency and handle throttling (HTTP 429) with backoff, or the speed gain turns into retries."}],"whereToUse":["Running a guardrail check alongside the main answer call","Summarising or classifying many independent documents or sections","Multi-perspective review, such as security, cost and readability reviewers","Voting on high-stakes classifications where a single call is unreliable","Prefetching several retrieval sources at once"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Branches do not depend on each other","Wall-clock time matters and quota allows the concurrency","A clear aggregation rule exists: merge, majority vote, ranking or a judge"],"avoid":["Agents must build on each other's work in sequence","Branches write to shared state without a conflict strategy","Quota or cost makes parallel calls infeasible","Aggregation logic is so complex that it lowers quality [[r9]]"],"flips":["Subtasks turn out to depend on each other: switch to a chain","Votes agree almost always: cut the voters, you are paying for no information","The split cannot be known in advance: move to orchestrator-workers"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Sectioning (this topic)","Independent parts, latency matters","Parts depend on each other","Tokens add up, merge step needed"],["Voting (this topic)","A single call is unreliable on a high-stakes label","Errors are correlated across votes","N times the cost for one answer"],["Prompt chain","Steps depend on each other","Steps are independent and latency is tight","Latency is the sum"],["Orchestrator-workers","Subtasks are decided at run time","Subtasks are known and fixed","Lead call plus synthesis"],["One larger, slower call","Quota is tight, task is small","Task has clear independent parts","Higher per-call latency"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Bursts of 429 errors and slower-than-sequential runs","cause":"Fan-out exceeded rate limits and every branch retried","fix":"Cap concurrency, add jitter to retries and queue branches."},{"symptom":"The merged answer contradicts itself","cause":"Branches made conflicting decisions with no rule for resolving them","fix":"Define precedence or a tie-break, or serialise the branches that touch the same thing."},{"symptom":"Voting adds cost but no accuracy","cause":"Voters are the same model with the same prompt, so errors are correlated","fix":"Vary prompts, models or evidence, and measure whether votes ever disagree."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t04-s1","title":"Many independent sections","situation":"A job reads a 60-section document and must produce a short finding for each section, then one combined report. Sections do not reference each other. The model quota allows about 20 concurrent calls.","constraints":["Sections are independent","Quota caps concurrency near 20","Report merge is a final step"],"unknowns":["Is there a wall-clock deadline?","Do findings need a shared glossary or style?"],"question":"How do you run it?","options":[{"name":"Sectioning with concurrency capped at the quota, then one merge call","verdict":"best","why":"Independent parts parallelise cleanly and the cap avoids self-inflicted throttling.","tradeoff":"Total tokens equal sequential processing, and style can drift across sections unless the prompt pins it."},{"name":"Sequential chain over all 60 sections","verdict":"good","why":"Simple, steady and consistent in style.","tradeoff":"Latency is about 60 calls long for no dependency reason."},{"name":"Fire all 60 calls at once","verdict":"poor","why":"It overruns the quota and triggers throttling and retries.","tradeoff":"Fine only when quota comfortably exceeds the fan-out, for instance in a batch tier."}],"flips":[{"if":"A deadline is hours away","then":"A batch API or queue can lower cost, at the price of latency."},{"if":"Sections reference earlier ones","then":"Chain the dependent sections and parallelise the rest."}],"takeaway":"Parallelise what is independent, and size the fan-out to your quota."},{"id":"w10-t04-s2","title":"Voting on a risky label","situation":"A classifier decides whether a message needs human review. A missed case is costly, and the single-call model is right about 90 percent of the time.","constraints":["Cost of a miss is high","Latency of two seconds is acceptable","Volume is moderate"],"unknowns":["Do the votes disagree on the hard cases or all fail together?","Is a human review queue available for disagreements?"],"question":"Do you add voting?","options":[{"name":"Three diverse votes with a human queue for disagreements","verdict":"best","why":"Disagreement is a useful signal, and diversity in prompts or models reduces correlated errors.","tradeoff":"Triple the classification cost and added review work."},{"name":"Run the same prompt five times at temperature zero","verdict":"poor","why":"Near-identical outputs add cost without new information.","tradeoff":"Sampling at higher temperature is closer to self-consistency, but still shares the model's blind spots."},{"name":"Keep one call and tighten the prompt","verdict":"good","why":"Cheapest, and prompt fixes may close part of the gap.","tradeoff":"No signal on uncertain cases, so misses stay silent."}],"flips":[{"if":"Votes never disagree on a labelled sample","then":"Drop to one call and spend the budget elsewhere."},{"if":"Volume grows ten times","then":"Vote only when a cheap first call is low confidence."}],"takeaway":"Votes are worth paying for only when they can disagree for informative reasons."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t04-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Three branches take 4 s, 6 s and 5 s and run in parallel. Merging takes 2 s. How many seconds does the fan-out take end to end?","answer":8,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"s","hint":"Parallel time is the slowest branch plus the merge.","explain":"max(4, 6, 5) + 2 = 8 s. Sequential would be 4 + 6 + 5 + 2 = 17 s. The token cost is the sum of all branches either way."},{"id":"w10-t04-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which is the strongest reason NOT to parallelise two agents?","options":["They use different prompts","Each takes more than a second","Both write to the same document with no conflict rule","Their outputs are in different formats"],"answer":2,"explain":"Concurrent writes to shared state without a conflict strategy are named in Microsoft's list of cases to avoid. Different prompts, durations and formats are normal in parallel branches. [[r9]]","ref":"r9","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t04-q3","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What is the difference between sectioning and voting?","options":["Sectioning runs different independent subtasks, voting runs the same task several times","Sectioning is sequential, voting is parallel","Sectioning needs a judge model, voting does not","They are the same pattern with different names"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sectioning divides work into independent parts, while voting repeats the same task for diverse outputs. Both are parallel, and either may use code or a model to aggregate. [[r1]]","ref":"r1","difficulty":1}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name the two variations of parallelization.","back":"Sectioning (independent subtasks at once) and voting (the same task run several times for diverse outputs) [[r1]]."},{"front":"Formula for fan-out latency?","back":"max(branch latency) plus the aggregation step. Cost is the sum of all branches plus aggregation."},{"front":"Why might voting add cost but no accuracy?","back":"Votes from the same model and prompt share blind spots, so errors correlate. Diversify prompts, models or evidence and check that votes ever disagree."}],"related":["w10-l1","w10-t02","w10-t05","w10-t14"],"refs":["r1","r9","tr2","tr5"]},{"id":"w10-t05","order":5,"group":"Workflow patterns","title":"Orchestrator-workers","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A lead model breaks a task into subtasks at run time, delegates them to worker calls and synthesises the results. The subtasks are not known in advance.","useIf":"The task is complex or breadth-first, the set of subtasks depends on the input, and the value per run covers the extra tokens.","avoidIf":"The subtasks are known up front (parallelise or chain them), the work is tightly coupled writes, or value per run is low.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"In **orchestrator-workers** a central LLM dynamically breaks down a task, delegates the pieces to worker LLMs and synthesises their results. It looks like parallelisation, but the subtasks are not predefined: the orchestrator decides them from the input, for example which files a code change touches or which sources a research question needs [[r1]]. Anthropic's research system is the large-scale example: a lead agent plans, spawns subagents that search in parallel, and synthesises the findings [[r2]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t05-d1","title":"Plan, delegate, synthesise","alt":"A lead agent plans subtasks, sends briefs to workers that run in parallel, and receives their results for synthesis.","src":"flowchart TB\n  U[Task] --> L[Lead: plan subtasks]\n  L -->|brief| W1[Worker 1]\n  L -->|brief| W2[Worker 2]\n  L -->|brief| W3[Worker 3]\n  W1 --> S[Lead: synthesise]\n  W2 --> S\n  W3 --> S\n  S --> A[Answer]"},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Gain on Anthropic's internal research eval","value":"90.2%","note":"Opus 4 lead with Sonnet 4 subagents vs single Opus 4. Vendor-reported [[r2]]"},{"label":"Token use vs chat","value":"about 15x","note":"Multi-agent systems, Anthropic's data [[r2]]"},{"label":"Variance in BrowseComp explained by tokens","value":"80%","note":"Spend is a main driver of quality [[r2]]"}]},{"t":"p","md":"The hard parts are the brief and the synthesis. A worker only knows what the lead tells it, so each brief should state the objective, the output format, the tools and sources to use and the boundary of the task. Anthropic's prompt rules scale effort to difficulty: a simple fact-finding task gets one agent with 3 to 10 tool calls, a direct comparison 2 to 4 subagents with 10 to 15 calls each, and complex research more than 10 subagents [[r2]]. Subagents that ran synchronously created bottlenecks, which is why independent workers should run in parallel with timeouts [[r2]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Read-only workers are the safe default","md":"Cognition argues that parallel agents making implicit decisions on the same artifact produce conflicting results, and later narrowed this to parallel writers: multiple agents contributing intelligence works when writes stay single-threaded [[r3]][[r4]]. Let workers read and report, and let one agent write."}],"whereToUse":["Breadth-first research that fans out across many sources","Code changes that touch an unknown set of files, with workers investigating each","Report generation where sections depend on what the lead finds","Data investigation where each lead finding spawns new questions","Any task where listing the subtasks in advance would be guesswork"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Subtasks cannot be predicted, but they can run independently once chosen","Value per run is high enough for several times the tokens","Workers are read-only or write to disjoint outputs"],"avoid":["The subtasks are fixed: use a chain or parallelisation","Workers must edit one shared artifact","Simple lookups, where one agent with a few tool calls is enough [[r2]]"],"flips":["Subtasks become predictable: freeze them as a parallel workflow","Workers make conflicting decisions: reduce to one writer plus read-only helpers","Synthesis loses detail from workers: pass artifacts by reference, not paraphrase"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Orchestrator-workers (this topic)","Subtasks decided at run time, breadth-first work","Tightly coupled writes, low-value tasks","About 15x chat tokens [[r2]], brief quality decides results"],["Parallelisation (sectioning)","Subtasks known in advance","The split depends on input","Merge step"],["Single agent with tools","One domain, a few tool calls","Breadth exceeds one context window","Context fills up in long runs"],["Supervisor with specialists","Fixed set of specialist roles","Roles are not known in advance","Lead is a bottleneck [[r32]]"],["Magentic (task ledger)","Open-ended work needing a visible plan","Time-sensitive or deterministic paths [[r9]]","Slower, untested beyond the original design"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Workers duplicate each other or leave gaps","cause":"Vague briefs with no boundaries or output format","fix":"Give each worker an objective, a boundary, a format and the tools to use, and have the lead check coverage."},{"symptom":"The lead call is slow and the run waits on the slowest worker","cause":"Workers run one after another or with no timeouts","fix":"Run independent workers in parallel with timeouts and let the lead proceed on partial results [[r2]]."},{"symptom":"Final answer misses details a worker found","cause":"The lead received paraphrased summaries only","fix":"Return structured findings or file references the lead can open, and cite sources."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t05-s1","title":"Breadth-first research, high value","situation":"Analysts ask for a landscape review across dozens of sources. Each answer is read by decision makers, and a miss is costly. Runs are rare and may take several minutes.","constraints":["Breadth-first, subtopics unknown in advance","High value per run","Several minutes of latency is acceptable","Workers only read"],"unknowns":["Is there an evaluation set that shows the crew beats a single agent?","How are sources cited and checked?"],"question":"What topology do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Orchestrator with parallel read-only workers and a synthesis step","verdict":"best","why":"Breadth parallelises well, tokens explain much of the quality, and read-only workers avoid write conflicts.","tradeoff":"Roughly 15x chat tokens in Anthropic's data, plus brief design and tracing effort."},{"name":"Single agent with search tools and a large context","verdict":"good","why":"Cheaper and simpler to debug, and sufficient for narrow questions.","tradeoff":"Context fills with search results and breadth suffers on large questions."},{"name":"Peer group chat among three analyst agents","verdict":"poor","why":"Debate over a shared transcript costs a lot and does not add coverage.","tradeoff":"Fits review or critique of a finished draft more than discovery."}],"flips":[{"if":"Questions are narrow fact lookups","then":"A single agent with 3 to 10 tool calls is enough."},{"if":"Sources sit in different trust boundaries","then":"Keep workers per boundary and pass only approved summaries to the lead."}],"takeaway":"Pay for orchestration only where breadth and value justify it."},{"id":"w10-t05-s2","title":"Coupled edits across files","situation":"A coding agent must make a change that spans many files. Pieces share names, types and tests, and any inconsistency breaks the build. Teams want it faster.","constraints":["Writes are tightly coupled","Consistency matters more than speed","Reads of unfamiliar code can be delegated"],"unknowns":["Can the build and tests act as an automatic check?","How large is the codebase relative to the context window?"],"question":"How do you use workers?","options":[{"name":"One writer agent with read-only workers that investigate and report","verdict":"best","why":"Investigation parallelises, while single-threaded writes keep decisions consistent, as Cognition's later view suggests.","tradeoff":"The writer is a serial bottleneck and must read worker reports fully."},{"name":"One worker per file, each editing in parallel","verdict":"poor","why":"Parallel writers make implicit, conflicting choices about shared names and types.","tradeoff":"Works for truly independent files, such as separate migrations."},{"name":"A single agent that does everything","verdict":"good","why":"Fully consistent and easy to trace.","tradeoff":"Context fills while exploring, which is what read-only workers relieve."}],"flips":[{"if":"Files are truly independent","then":"Parallel writers become safe and faster."},{"if":"A reviewer with fresh context is available","then":"Add it after the write, since a clean-context reviewer worked best in Cognition's report."}],"takeaway":"Parallelise reads, serialise writes."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t05-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What separates orchestrator-workers from simple parallelisation?","options":["The orchestrator decides the subtasks at run time from the input","Workers run in parallel","Workers use different models","The result is aggregated by code"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both run branches in parallel and both may aggregate. The distinguishing feature is that the subtasks are not predefined: the lead chooses them dynamically. [[r1]]","ref":"r1","difficulty":1},{"id":"w10-t05-q2","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"A research crew returns answers that miss obvious sources and repeat the same search. Which fixes address the likely cause?","options":["Write briefs with objective, boundary, output format and tools","Run all workers on one shared prompt with no task split","Have the lead check coverage against the plan before synthesis","Remove the lead and let workers chat freely"],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"Duplicated and missing work comes from vague briefs and unchecked coverage. Giving every worker the same prompt guarantees duplication, and removing the lead removes the one place that tracks coverage.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does the orchestrator do that a parallel workflow does not?","back":"It decides the subtasks at run time from the input, delegates them and synthesises the results [[r1]]."},{"front":"What should a worker brief contain?","back":"The objective, output format, tools and sources to use, and the boundary of the task, so workers do not duplicate or leave gaps."},{"front":"Anthropic's effort rules for subagent counts?","back":"Simple fact-finding: 1 agent with 3 to 10 tool calls. Direct comparison: 2 to 4 subagents with 10 to 15 calls. Complex research: more than 10 subagents [[r2]]."}],"related":["w10-l3","w10-t04","w10-t11","w10-t12"],"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r10","r32"]},{"id":"w10-t06","order":6,"group":"Workflow patterns","title":"Evaluator-optimizer","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"One call generates an answer and another evaluates it against criteria. Feedback loops back to the generator until the answer passes or a stop rule fires.","useIf":"You have clear, checkable criteria and a first draft is measurably improved by feedback, so each loop earns its cost.","avoidIf":"Criteria are vague, the evaluator shares the generator's blind spots, or one pass is already good enough.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"In **evaluator-optimizer** one LLM call generates a response and another provides evaluation and feedback in a loop. Anthropic says it works well when there are clear evaluation criteria and when iterative refinement gives measurable value, for example literary translation with nuance the first pass misses [[r1]]. Microsoft's name for the same loop is **maker-checker**, also known as generator-verifier, critic loops or reflection loops [[r9]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t06-d1","title":"The critic loop with stop rules","alt":"A generator produces a draft, an evaluator scores it, and the loop ends on pass, on the iteration cap or on no improvement. Otherwise feedback goes back to the generator.","src":"flowchart LR\n  G[Generate or revise] --> E[Evaluate against criteria]\n  E --> D{Stop?}\n  D -->|pass| O[Return best draft]\n  D -->|cap or no gain| F[Fallback: escalate or warn]\n  D -->|fail| G"},{"t":"p","md":"Three details decide whether the loop helps. First, the evaluator needs acceptance criteria that produce consistent pass or fail decisions, ideally from an external signal such as tests, a schema, a retrieval check or a rubric with named dimensions. Second, you need stop rules: a pass threshold, an iteration cap, a no-improvement check and a budget. Microsoft recommends an iteration cap with defined fallback behaviour, such as escalating to a human or returning the best draft with a quality warning [[r9]]. Third, independence helps: Cognition reports a review loop that caught about 2 bugs per pull request, around 58 percent of them severe, working best when the reviewer shares no context with the coder. That figure is vendor-reported [[r4]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Diminishing returns","md":"Each loop costs a generation and an evaluation call, and the second revision usually improves less than the first. Plot score against iteration on your own data and cap the loop where the curve flattens."}],"whereToUse":["Code generation where tests and linters give an objective score","Translation or tone-sensitive writing with a rubric of named criteria","Structured extraction checked against a schema and business rules","Answer drafting checked for groundedness against retrieved sources","Compliance review with a fixed checklist before content is released"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Acceptance criteria are explicit and the evaluator can apply them consistently","An external signal (tests, schema, sources) can check the draft","Quality gain per loop justifies the extra calls"],"avoid":["No one can say what good looks like, so the evaluator invents standards","The evaluator is the same model and prompt as the generator and has the same blind spots","Latency is tight and one pass meets the bar"],"flips":["The evaluator is too lenient or too harsh: calibrate it on labelled examples","Scores plateau after one loop: cap at one revision","Failures are high stakes: send cap-hit cases to a person"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Evaluator-optimizer (this topic)","Clear criteria, measurable gain per loop","Criteria are vague or one pass is enough","Two or more calls per round"],["Gate in a chain","A deterministic check can accept or reject","Quality needs judgement","Cheap, but no revision"],["Self-reflection in one call","A quick sanity pass with no external signal needed","Reasoning errors the model cannot see","Little gain without feedback [[tr7]]"],["Voting","Independent attempts reveal disagreement","A flaw needs a targeted fix","N times the cost"],["Human review","Stakes are high, volume is low","Volume is high","Slow, expensive"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The loop always passes on the first try","cause":"The evaluator is lenient or shares the generator's context and bias","fix":"Use a different prompt or model, hide the generator's reasoning and calibrate on known bad drafts."},{"symptom":"The loop runs to the cap and the output gets worse","cause":"Feedback is vague, so revisions drift","fix":"Require specific, actionable feedback tied to criteria, and keep the best-scoring draft."},{"symptom":"Cost per task doubles with no quality gain","cause":"No stop rule on score plateau","fix":"Stop when score improvement falls below a threshold and track gain per iteration."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t06-s1","title":"Code with a test suite","situation":"A generator writes a function and a test suite exists that fails or passes it. The team wants higher first-time quality without a person in the loop.","constraints":["Objective pass or fail signal","Moderate latency tolerance","Retries are cheap"],"unknowns":["How many iterations usually reach a pass?","What happens when tests still fail at the cap?"],"question":"How do you build the loop?","options":[{"name":"Generate, run tests, feed failures back, cap at a few rounds, escalate at the cap","verdict":"best","why":"The tests are an external signal that does not share the model's blind spots, and the cap bounds cost.","tradeoff":"Each round is a generation plus a test run, and tests only cover what they cover."},{"name":"Ask the model to critique its own code with no tests","verdict":"poor","why":"Intrinsic self-correction often fails to fix reasoning errors without external feedback [[tr7]].","tradeoff":"Fine for style and readability notes, not for correctness."},{"name":"A separate judge model scores the code, no tests","verdict":"good","why":"Independence helps and catches some bugs.","tradeoff":"Judges miss runtime failures that tests catch directly."}],"flips":[{"if":"No tests exist","then":"Generate tests first, or use a static analyser as the signal."},{"if":"Stakes are high","then":"Add a clean-context reviewer and a human approval after the loop."}],"takeaway":"A loop is only as good as its signal."},{"id":"w10-t06-s2","title":"Subjective writing, tight latency","situation":"A user-facing feature polishes marketing copy. Taste varies by reader, there is no rubric, and users wait for the result.","constraints":["No objective criteria","Interactive latency","Cost per request matters"],"unknowns":["Can the team write a rubric with named dimensions?","Do users accept regeneration on demand?"],"question":"Do you add an evaluator loop?","options":[{"name":"Skip the loop, offer a regenerate button and optional user feedback","verdict":"best","why":"Without criteria the evaluator invents standards, and latency is tight. The user is the best evaluator here.","tradeoff":"Quality varies per draft and you rely on the user to iterate."},{"name":"Two automatic revision rounds by a critic model","verdict":"poor","why":"It doubles or triples latency and cost for gains you cannot measure.","tradeoff":"Right once you have a rubric and evidence that critic scores track user preference."},{"name":"One critic pass against a short style rubric","verdict":"good","why":"A single pass adds modest latency and can enforce brand rules.","tradeoff":"Rubric upkeep, and one more call on every request."}],"flips":[{"if":"Brand rules are explicit and checkable","then":"Gate on those rules in code and revise only on failure."},{"if":"Requests are asynchronous","then":"Latency pressure drops and a bounded loop becomes affordable."}],"takeaway":"No criteria, no critic loop."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t06-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the steps of a bounded evaluator-optimizer round in order.","items":["Generate or revise a draft","Evaluate the draft against the criteria","Check stop rules (pass, cap, no improvement)","Feed specific feedback back to the generator"],"explain":"A draft exists before it can be scored. Stop rules are checked after each evaluation and before another revision. Feedback only goes back when the loop continues. [[r9]]","ref":"r9","difficulty":1},{"id":"w10-t06-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Scores rise from 6 to 8 after revision one, then to 8.2 after revision two and 8.2 after revision three. What is the best stop rule?","options":["Always run three revisions","Stop only when the score reaches 10","Remove the evaluator after round one","Stop when gain per round falls below a set threshold, and cap total rounds"],"answer":3,"explain":"Gain per round and a hard cap together bound cost at the plateau. A fixed three rounds wastes calls, a perfect score may never arrive and removing the evaluator drops the signal. [[r9]]","ref":"r9","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t06-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"An evaluator approves nearly every draft. Which changes make it more discriminating?","options":["Hide the generator's reasoning from the evaluator","Use a different prompt or model for the evaluator","Calibrate it on labelled drafts known to be bad","Increase the generator temperature"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Independence and calibration against known failures make an evaluator useful. Raising generator temperature changes the drafts, not the evaluator's leniency.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Other names for evaluator-optimizer in Microsoft's guide?","back":"Maker-checker, generator-verifier, critic loops and reflection loops. It needs clear acceptance criteria and an iteration cap with a fallback [[r9]]."},{"front":"When does Anthropic say this pattern works well?","back":"When evaluation criteria are clear and iterative refinement gives measurable value [[r1]]."},{"front":"What helped Cognition's review loop most?","back":"A reviewer that shares no context with the coder. The reported average was 2 bugs per PR, about 58 percent severe, vendor-reported [[r4]]."}],"related":["w10-l1","w10-t09","w10-t14","w10-t16"],"refs":["r1","r9","r4","tr7"]},{"id":"w10-t07","order":7,"group":"Reasoning loops","title":"ReAct","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"ReAct interleaves reasoning with actions: the model thinks, calls a tool, reads the observation and repeats until it can answer. It is the loop inside most tool-using agents.","useIf":"The next step depends on what the last tool returned, so the path cannot be planned in advance, and each loop is affordable.","avoidIf":"The tool chain is predictable (plan first or use a workflow), latency is very tight, or tools are unreliable and nothing bounds the loop.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**ReAct** (reason plus act) lets a model alternate between a reasoning step and an action. The model writes a thought, picks a tool and arguments, the harness runs the tool and appends the result as an **observation**, and the model reasons again. On the ALFWorld and WebShop benchmarks the paper reports absolute success-rate gains of 34 percent and 10 percent over imitation and reinforcement-learning baselines using one or two in-context examples [[r16]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t07-d1","title":"The ReAct loop","alt":"The model reasons, chooses a tool call, receives an observation and loops back until it gives a final answer. Brakes in the harness can stop the loop.","src":"flowchart LR\n  T[Reason] --> A[Act: tool call]\n  A --> O[Observation]\n  O --> T\n  T -->|enough evidence| F[Final answer]\n  B[Harness brakes] -.-> T"},{"t":"p","md":"In modern APIs the loop is native. The model returns tool calls, your code executes them, and you send the results back. With reasoning models the thought is hidden reasoning tokens, billed as output, and OpenAI's guide says to pass reasoning items back with the function call outputs so the model keeps its chain across calls [[r34]]. Anthropic describes agents as LLMs using tools on environmental feedback in a loop, and stresses getting ground truth from the environment at each step, with stopping conditions such as a maximum number of iterations [[r1]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The loop has a cost shape you must respect. Each step rereads the growing history, so input tokens grow roughly with the square of the number of steps unless you trim results or rely on caching. The harness, not the prompt, owns the brakes: step cap, token budget, deadline and repeat detection. See the loop guards topic for the details."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Observations are untrusted input","md":"A tool result may contain text that looks like instructions. Treat it as data, keep tool permissions narrow, and require approval for high-impact actions. A loop that acts on whatever it reads is the setting for indirect prompt injection."}],"whereToUse":["Research and browsing agents where each result changes the next query","Debugging and operations agents that inspect, hypothesise and re-inspect","Data analysis agents that explore a schema before writing queries","Customer-support agents that look up an order, then decide which action fits","Any tool-using assistant whose tool order cannot be listed in advance"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The next action depends on the last observation","Tools are reliable enough that errors are recoverable","A bounded loop with traces and evals is in place"],"avoid":["The sequence of tool calls is the same every time","Each step is expensive or irreversible and no approval exists","Latency budget leaves room for only one or two model calls"],"flips":["Tool chains become predictable: freeze them as plan-and-execute or a workflow","Step counts and tokens balloon: trim old tool results or add a plan step","Tools fail often: improve tool descriptions and error messages before adding steps"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["ReAct loop (this topic)","Tool outputs steer the next step","The chain is predictable or latency is tight","Input tokens grow each step, needs brakes"],["Plan-and-execute","Tool chains are stable and checkable","Surprises break the plan","Replanning logic, stale plans [[r18]]"],["Workflow (chain or route)","Steps are known","Inputs vary in structure","Branch growth"],["Reasoning model with tools","A hard problem benefits from deeper thinking per step","Effort is wasted on simple lookups","Reasoning tokens billed as output [[r34]]"],["Reflection on top of ReAct","A checker exists after the loop","No external signal","More calls, bias risk [[tr7]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The agent repeats the same tool call with the same arguments","cause":"Observations do not change its belief, or the error message is unhelpful","fix":"Detect repeated calls, return a clear error that names what to change, and stop with an explicit reason after a repeat threshold."},{"symptom":"The agent calls a tool that does not exist or passes invalid arguments","cause":"Overlapping or vaguely described tools","fix":"Tighten tool names, descriptions and schemas, validate arguments and return actionable errors."},{"symptom":"Runs get slower and costlier with every step","cause":"The full history, including large tool results, is resent each time","fix":"Trim or clear stale tool results, keep a stable prefix for cache hits and cap steps."},{"symptom":"It answers early on thin evidence","cause":"No check that the answer is supported by observations","fix":"Add a final verification step, or require citations to observation ids."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t07-s1","title":"Exploratory investigation","situation":"An internal assistant investigates incidents by querying logs, a ticket system and a runbook index. Findings from one query decide the next, and a run may take a few minutes.","constraints":["Tool order unpredictable","Read-only tools","Minutes of latency are acceptable","Moderate volume"],"unknowns":["What step cap and token budget fit typical runs?","Do traces show where runs stall?"],"question":"Which loop shape do you pick?","options":[{"name":"ReAct loop with step cap, budget and repeat detection","verdict":"best","why":"Observations steer each step, read-only tools make errors cheap and the brakes bound cost.","tradeoff":"Variable cost per run and a need to evaluate trajectories."},{"name":"Plan-and-execute with a fixed upfront plan","verdict":"good","why":"Fewer tokens and a reviewable plan.","tradeoff":"Findings invalidate the plan often, so replanning logic is required."},{"name":"A fixed workflow that runs every query in order","verdict":"poor","why":"It wastes calls on irrelevant tools and cannot follow a lead.","tradeoff":"Right when incidents of one type always need the same four lookups."}],"flips":[{"if":"Most runs follow the same five tool calls","then":"Freeze that path as a plan or workflow and keep ReAct for the exceptions."},{"if":"Tools can change production state","then":"Add approval gates before any write action."}],"takeaway":"Let observations steer, and let the harness hold the brakes."},{"id":"w10-t07-s2","title":"Voice assistant, tight latency","situation":"A voice assistant must answer in about two seconds. A request needs an order lookup, and sometimes a second lookup. Callers are impatient.","constraints":["Latency target near two seconds","One or two tool calls per request","Tools are fast and reliable"],"unknowns":["Can the first lookup run before the model call?","How often is the second lookup needed?"],"question":"How do you use tools here?","options":[{"name":"Prefetch the likely lookup in code, then one model call, with one optional follow-up call","verdict":"best","why":"It removes a model round trip from the common path and keeps a small bounded loop for the rare case.","tradeoff":"Prefetching can fetch data that is never used, and the code path must be maintained."},{"name":"Open-ended ReAct loop with a generous step cap","verdict":"poor","why":"Each extra model call adds latency the call cannot afford, and the cap hides slow tails.","tradeoff":"Right for text channels where a longer wait is acceptable."},{"name":"A single prompt with all data stuffed in","verdict":"good","why":"One call, so latency is lowest.","tradeoff":"Context bloat and stale or unneeded data on every request."}],"flips":[{"if":"The channel changes to chat with a ten second budget","then":"A short ReAct loop becomes acceptable."},{"if":"Lookups grow to many possible tools","then":"Route first, then run a small loop on the routed path."}],"takeaway":"Every loop step is a model call, so count them against the latency budget."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t07-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order one pass of the ReAct loop.","items":["The model reasons about what it needs","The model emits a tool call","The harness executes the tool","The result is appended as an observation"],"explain":"Reasoning leads to a tool call, the harness runs it and the observation goes back into context for the next pass. The model never executes tools itself. [[r16]]","ref":"r16","difficulty":1},{"id":"w10-t07-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A ReAct agent calls the same search with the same query six times, then times out. What is the best first fix?","options":["Raise the model temperature","Remove the step cap","Detect repeated identical calls and stop or change course with a clear error","Give the agent more tools"],"answer":2,"explain":"Repeat detection plus an informative error addresses the loop and returns a stop reason. Temperature does not fix the missing signal, removing the cap makes it worse, and more tools add overload.","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t07-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which conditions favour ReAct over plan-and-execute?","options":["Tool outputs change what you should do next","The tool chain is identical for every request","Surprises are common and re-planning would be frequent","A human must approve the whole plan before any step"],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"ReAct earns its cost when observations steer and surprises are common. A fixed chain favours a plan or workflow, and plan approval before execution is the plan-first pattern's strength.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does ReAct interleave?","back":"Reasoning traces and actions. The model thinks, calls a tool, reads the observation and repeats [[r16]]."},{"front":"Why do ReAct input tokens grow so fast?","back":"Each step resends the history, so total input grows roughly quadratically with steps unless results are trimmed or cached."},{"front":"Who owns the brakes in a ReAct agent?","back":"The harness: step cap, token budget, deadline and repeat detection, returning an explicit stop reason. A prompt asking the model to stop is not a brake."}],"related":["w10-l2","w10-t08","w10-t10","w10-t09"],"refs":["r16","r1","r34","r18","tr7"]},{"id":"w10-t08","order":8,"group":"Reasoning loops","title":"Plan-and-execute","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A planner writes the steps first, then an executor carries them out, often with cheaper calls and without reasoning after every tool result. A replan step handles surprises.","useIf":"Tool chains are fairly predictable, you want a reviewable plan before side effects, or token cost per run matters more than step-by-step flexibility.","avoidIf":"Each tool result changes what you should do next, or the environment shifts faster than a plan can stay valid.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Plan-and-execute** splits thinking from doing. A planner produces an ordered list of steps; an executor runs them, usually one tool call or small subtask at a time; a replanner revises the remaining steps if a step fails or reveals new facts. Plan-and-Solve prompting was introduced to reduce the missing-step errors of zero-shot chain of thought by devising a plan first and then executing the subtasks [[r38]]. ReWOO goes further by decoupling reasoning from tool observations, so the plan can reference earlier results by placeholder and tools run without a model call between them. It reports 5x token efficiency and 4 percent higher accuracy on HotpotQA against interleaved reasoning [[r18]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t08-d1","title":"Plan, execute, replan","alt":"A planner writes a plan, an executor runs each step, a check decides whether to continue, replan or finish.","src":"flowchart LR\n  P[Planner: write steps] --> E[Executor: run next step]\n  E --> C{Result as expected?}\n  C -->|yes, more steps| E\n  C -->|surprise| R[Replan remaining steps]\n  R --> E\n  C -->|done| F[Final answer]"},{"t":"p","md":"The plan is also an artifact. You can show it to a user, store it, diff it and require approval before anything runs. Magentic orchestration in Microsoft Agent Framework builds on this idea with a task ledger and a flag for human review of the plan [[r10]]. The weakness is brittleness: a plan written before the first observation encodes assumptions that may be wrong, and a long plan executed blindly compounds a bad assumption. Replanning on failure, and short plans for uncertain tasks, are the usual defences."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Reasoning models blur the line","md":"OpenAI advises giving reasoning models a clear goal, constraints and an output contract instead of scripting every step [[r34]]. A strong reasoning model may plan internally. An explicit plan still helps when you need approval, audit or cheaper executors."}],"whereToUse":["Multi-step API or data tasks with stable tool chains","Tasks that need a plan reviewed before any write or spend","Long jobs where a small model can execute steps a larger model planned","Reports and analyses that follow a known outline","Agents where token cost matters and tool results are small and checkable"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Steps and tools are predictable enough to plan","Someone needs to see or approve the plan first","Execution steps are simple enough for a cheaper model"],"avoid":["Early observations usually overturn the plan","The environment changes during execution","The task is short enough that a plan is more tokens than the work"],"flips":["Replans happen on most runs: switch to ReAct","Plans repeat across runs: freeze them as a workflow","Steps need judgement mid-way: let the executor reason, not only call tools"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Plan-and-execute (this topic)","Predictable chains, plan review, cost pressure","Frequent surprises","Stale plans, replan logic"],["ReAct","Observations steer every step","Predictable chains, tight cost","Quadratic input growth"],["ReWOO style (plan with placeholders)","Tool results need no reasoning between calls","A result changes the plan","Complex planner prompt [[r18]]"],["Workflow","Steps fully fixed","Inputs vary","Branch growth"],["Magentic task ledger","Open-ended multi-agent work needing a visible plan","Time-sensitive or deterministic work [[r9]]","Untested beyond its original design"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Step four fails because step two returned something the plan did not expect","cause":"The plan assumed a result shape before seeing it","fix":"Validate each step's result, replan the remainder on mismatch, and keep plans short for uncertain tasks."},{"symptom":"The executor follows the plan after the goal has changed","cause":"No replan trigger when the user or the facts change","fix":"Check the plan against new input at each step boundary and replan on conflict."},{"symptom":"Planning costs more than the work","cause":"A large model writes a long plan for a three-call task","fix":"Skip planning for short tasks, or use a small model with a template for the plan."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t08-s1","title":"Stable chain, cost pressure","situation":"A nightly job reconciles records across four internal systems. The tool order is nearly always the same, tool results are small, and the bill is the concern.","constraints":["Predictable tool chain","Batch, so latency is relaxed","Cost per run is monitored","Read and write tools"],"unknowns":["How often does a step return something unexpected?","Is approval needed before writes?"],"question":"Which loop shape do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Plan-and-execute with a cheap executor and replan on mismatch","verdict":"best","why":"Stable chains benefit from planning once and running cheaply. The replan path covers rare surprises.","tradeoff":"More engineering than a simple loop, and the plan needs validation."},{"name":"Plain ReAct on a large model","verdict":"good","why":"Works and adapts.","tradeoff":"Pays for reasoning after every tool result when the chain rarely changes."},{"name":"One huge prompt that does all four systems in one call","verdict":"poor","why":"Tool calls still need executing, and one call cannot react to a failed step.","tradeoff":"Fine only for tasks with no tools, where one call can do the work."}],"flips":[{"if":"Reconciliation steps vary a lot per record","then":"Use ReAct per record."},{"if":"The chain is fully fixed","then":"Replace the planner with a workflow in code."}],"takeaway":"Plan once when the world is stable. Replan only when it is not."},{"id":"w10-t08-s2","title":"Review before side effects","situation":"An agent will provision resources and send messages on a user's behalf. The user wants to see what it intends to do before anything happens. Runs are low volume.","constraints":["Side effects cannot all be undone","A human reviews before execution","Low volume"],"unknowns":["Can the plan be shown in a readable form?","What happens if the user edits a step?"],"question":"How do you structure the run?","options":[{"name":"Generate a plan, require approval, execute steps with checks and replan on failure","verdict":"best","why":"The plan is a reviewable artifact and approval happens before any side effect.","tradeoff":"Added user friction and a plan that may need editing."},{"name":"ReAct loop with an approval prompt on each write","verdict":"good","why":"Gates only the risky calls and adapts to results.","tradeoff":"Many interruptions, and the user never sees the whole intent up front."},{"name":"Autonomous loop with logging only","verdict":"poor","why":"Logs help after the fact but cannot undo messages already sent.","tradeoff":"Right for read-only tasks or fully reversible actions."}],"flips":[{"if":"Actions are cheap and reversible","then":"Drop plan approval and rely on audit and compensation."},{"if":"Approval waits take hours","then":"Persist the plan and run state durably so the wait costs nothing."}],"takeaway":"A plan is the cheapest place to put a human."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t08-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does ReWOO change compared with an interleaved reasoning loop?","options":["It decouples reasoning from tool observations, so tool calls run without a model call between them","It removes tools from the agent","It trains the model on each tool","It runs all steps in parallel"],"answer":0,"explain":"ReWOO plans with placeholders for tool results and runs tools without interleaved reasoning, reporting 5x token efficiency and 4 percent higher accuracy on HotpotQA. It still uses tools and is not inherently parallel. [[r18]]","ref":"r18","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t08-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Replans occur on 70 percent of runs. What is the sensible change?","options":["Make plans longer","Disable replanning","Add a second planner","Move toward a ReAct loop, since observations are steering the work"],"answer":3,"explain":"Frequent replans show observations change the path, which is the ReAct case. Longer plans bake in more wrong assumptions, disabling replanning breaks failure handling and a second planner adds cost.","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the main weakness of plan-and-execute?","back":"Brittleness. A plan written before the first observation can rest on wrong assumptions, so you need replanning and short plans for uncertain tasks."},{"front":"What did ReWOO report?","back":"5x token efficiency and 4 percent higher accuracy on HotpotQA versus interleaved reasoning, by decoupling reasoning from tool observations [[r18]]."},{"front":"Why is a plan useful beyond cost?","back":"It is an artifact you can show, store, diff and approve before side effects run."}],"related":["w10-l2","w10-t07","w10-t15","w10-t22"],"refs":["r38","r18","r10","r34"]},{"id":"w10-t09","order":9,"group":"Reasoning loops","title":"Reflection and self-critique","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"The model reviews its own output or trajectory, writes down what went wrong and tries again. It helps when the critique rests on an external signal and often fails when it rests on the model's own judgement alone.","useIf":"A reliable signal exists after an attempt (tests, tool results, retrieval, a validator) that the critique can use.","avoidIf":"The only evidence is the model's own opinion of its reasoning, or latency and cost leave no room for a second pass.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Reflection** adds a critique step after an attempt. **Reflexion** stores verbal reflections in an episodic memory buffer and uses them in the next attempt, with no weight updates. The paper reports 91 percent pass@1 on HumanEval against 80 percent for a GPT-4 baseline, in a setting where code execution gives feedback [[r17]]. **Self-Refine** has one model generate, give feedback and refine, and reports about 20 percent absolute improvement on average across seven tasks [[tr8]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The counter-evidence matters. A study of intrinsic self-correction, where the model corrects itself with no external feedback, found that LLMs struggle to self-correct reasoning and that performance sometimes degrades after self-correction [[tr7]]. The pattern is consistent: critique works when it is anchored to something the model did not generate, and it is weak when the model must find its own reasoning errors. Reflection is cheap to try and easy to over-trust, so the useful design question is always what evidence the critique rests on."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t09-d1","title":"Anchor the critique","alt":"An attempt is checked by an external signal such as tests or sources. The signal result feeds a critique and a retry. A critique with no signal is a weak path.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Attempt] --> X[External signal: tests, sources, schema]\n  X --> C[Critique using the signal]\n  C --> R[Retry with notes]\n  R --> A\n  A -.->|no signal| W[Self-opinion only: weak]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Where critique has a signal","head":["Task","Signal the critique can use","Strength"],"rows":[["Code generation","Unit tests, compiler and linter output","Strong"],["Grounded answers","Retrieved passages, citation checks","Strong"],["Structured extraction","Schema validation, business rules","Strong"],["Math or logic with no checker","Only the model's own judgement","Weak, prefer voting or a stronger model"],["Open writing","A rubric with named criteria","Medium"]],"compact":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Use a clean-context reviewer","md":"Cognition reports a review loop works best when the reviewer shares no context with the coder, which avoids copying the coder's assumptions. The numbers are vendor-reported [[r4]]."}],"whereToUse":["Coding agents that retry after a failed test run","RAG answer drafting checked for support against retrieved sources","Extraction pipelines that fix output after schema or rule violations","Long agent runs that write lessons learned into notes for the next attempt","Review steps where a second model with fresh context audits the first"],"whenToUse":{"use":["An objective signal exists after the attempt","The cost of a retry is lower than the cost of a wrong answer","Reflections can be stored and reused, for example as notes"],"avoid":["The only evidence is the model's own confidence","One extra pass would break the latency budget","Critic and generator share the same blind spots and context"],"flips":["No signal exists: add one (tests, retrieval check) or use voting and a stronger model instead","Reflection helps once but not twice: cap at one revision","Stakes are high: add independent review by a different model or a person"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Tool-verified reflection (this topic)","A test or source gives real feedback","No checker exists","Extra calls per retry"],["Intrinsic self-critique","Cheap polish for style or clarity","Reasoning or factual errors","Can degrade correct answers [[tr7]]"],["Independent critic model","Different model or context removes shared bias","Judge is weaker than the generator","Second model cost"],["Voting or best-of-N","Errors are independent across samples","Errors are systematic","N times the cost"],["Human review","High stakes, low volume","High volume","Slow, costly"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A correct answer gets worse after the critique round","cause":"The model second-guesses itself with no external evidence [[tr7]]","fix":"Only revise when a signal fails, and keep the original if the check passes."},{"symptom":"Reflections pile up and mislead later attempts","cause":"Notes are never pruned or scored","fix":"Keep reflections short, tied to a failure, and expire or rank them."},{"symptom":"The critic approves flawed work every time","cause":"It shares the generator's model, context and bias","fix":"Use a different prompt or model with fresh context, and calibrate on known bad outputs."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t09-s1","title":"Code agent with a test runner","situation":"A coding assistant writes a patch and can run the project's tests. First-try success is moderate, and reruns take seconds.","constraints":["Objective pass or fail from tests","Cheap retries","Moderate latency tolerance"],"unknowns":["How many retries usually help?","Do the tests cover the behaviour being changed?"],"question":"How do you add reflection?","options":[{"name":"Run tests, feed failures into a short reflection, retry with a cap","verdict":"best","why":"The test output is external evidence, so the reflection targets real errors and the cap bounds cost.","tradeoff":"Coverage gaps in the tests become blind spots, and each retry adds latency."},{"name":"Ask the model to double-check its patch with no execution","verdict":"poor","why":"Self-checking without feedback often misses reasoning errors and can talk itself out of a correct fix.","tradeoff":"Reasonable for cosmetic review, not correctness."},{"name":"Run five independent patches and pick by majority","verdict":"good","why":"Diversity can surface a working patch.","tradeoff":"Five times the generation cost, and majority is weak when patches differ textually."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tests are slow or flaky","then":"Reduce retries, or reflect against static analysis and type checks first."},{"if":"Tests do not exist","then":"Generate and review tests first, or use an independent reviewer."}],"takeaway":"Reflect against evidence, not against vibes."},{"id":"w10-t09-s2","title":"Reasoning question, no checker","situation":"An assistant answers multi-step reasoning questions with no ground truth to check against. Users ask for higher accuracy and the team proposes a self-review step.","constraints":["No external signal","Interactive latency","Accuracy matters"],"unknowns":["Does a stronger model or higher reasoning effort close the gap?","Do eval results show gain or loss from self-review?"],"question":"Do you add a self-review pass?","options":[{"name":"Measure it on a golden set, and prefer a stronger model, more reasoning effort or voting if it does not help","verdict":"best","why":"Evidence says intrinsic self-correction is unreliable without feedback, so test it before shipping it.","tradeoff":"Evaluation effort and possibly higher per-call cost from effort or voting."},{"name":"Ship a self-review pass by default","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds latency and can degrade answers that were right.","tradeoff":"Right if your own evals show a net gain on this task."},{"name":"Add an independent verifier model with fresh context","verdict":"good","why":"Independence reduces shared blind spots.","tradeoff":"A second model call and a verifier that may be weaker than the generator."}],"flips":[{"if":"A checker becomes available, such as retrieval of sources","then":"Reflect against that evidence."},{"if":"Latency budget grows","then":"Higher reasoning effort or voting becomes affordable."}],"takeaway":"Reflection without a signal is a hypothesis, so test it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t09-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does the study of intrinsic self-correction conclude about reasoning tasks without external feedback?","options":["Self-correction reliably improves accuracy","LLMs struggle to self-correct, and performance sometimes degrades","Self-correction works only for code","It works only with temperature above one"],"answer":1,"explain":"The paper finds models struggle to self-correct reasoning without external feedback and may get worse. It does not restrict the finding to code or to a temperature range. [[tr7]]","ref":"tr7","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t09-q2","type":"match","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Match each task to the signal a critique can use.","pairs":[{"l":"Code generation","r":"Unit test output"},{"l":"Grounded answer","r":"Retrieved passages"},{"l":"Structured extraction","r":"Schema validation"},{"l":"Math with no checker","r":"No external signal"}],"explain":"Tests, sources and schemas are evidence the model did not generate. With no checker the critique rests on the model's opinion, where voting or a stronger model is the better lever."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does Reflexion store, and what does it not do?","back":"It stores verbal reflections in an episodic memory buffer. It does no weight updates. Paper: 91 percent pass@1 on HumanEval vs 80 percent for the GPT-4 baseline [[r17]]."},{"front":"When does self-critique fail?","back":"When the model must find its own reasoning errors with no external feedback. Performance can even degrade [[tr7]]."},{"front":"What makes a reviewer more useful?","back":"Independence: a different prompt or model, and fresh context that does not share the generator's assumptions [[r4]]."}],"related":["w10-l2","w10-t06","w10-t07","w10-t16"],"refs":["r17","tr8","tr7","r4"]},{"id":"w10-t10","order":10,"group":"Reasoning loops","title":"Loop guards, budgets and stop conditions","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Any loop that can call a model needs brakes owned by the harness: a step cap, a token and cost budget, a deadline and repeat detection. Each brake ends the run with an explicit stop reason and a usable partial result.","useIf":"A model chooses its own next step, calls tools that cost money or time, or talks to other agents in a loop.","avoidIf":"Never skip it for an agent loop. Skip only for a fixed chain, whose length is already bounded by your code.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A loop that depends on a model to decide when to stop will sometimes not stop. Anthropic notes it is common to include stopping conditions such as a maximum number of iterations to keep control [[r1]], and Microsoft tells you to set iteration limits to guard against infinite tool-call loops [[r9]]. Frameworks expose the brake under different names. The OpenAI Agents SDK raises `MaxTurnsExceeded` when `max_turns` is passed, and `max_turns=None` disables the limit [[tr3]]. LangGraph's recursion limit caps super-steps per run and raises `GraphRecursionError`; from version 1.0.6 the default is 1000 steps, which is a safety net, not a budget [[tr4]]. Group chat in Agent Framework has `MaximumIterationCount` [[tr1]], handoff autonomous mode takes a `turnLimit` [[r11]], and Magentic exposes max rounds, max stalls and max resets [[r10]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t10-d1","title":"Brakes checked before every step","alt":"Before each loop step the harness checks step count, token and cost budget, deadline and repetition. If any brake fires, the run stops with a reason and a partial result.","src":"flowchart TB\n  S[Next step requested] --> C{Brakes}\n  C -->|steps over cap| X[Stop: max_steps]\n  C -->|tokens or cost over budget| Y[Stop: budget]\n  C -->|past deadline| Z[Stop: deadline]\n  C -->|same call repeated| W[Stop: repeat]\n  C -->|all clear| R[Run step]\n  R --> S"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"Brakes in the harness, not the prompt","code":"def check_brakes(run, limits):\n    if run.steps >= limits.max_steps:\n        return 'max_steps'\n    if run.input_tokens + run.output_tokens >= limits.max_tokens:\n        return 'budget'\n    if run.cost >= limits.max_cost:\n        return 'budget'\n    if run.elapsed >= limits.deadline:\n        return 'deadline'\n    if run.same_call_count >= limits.max_repeats:\n        return 'repeat'\n    return None   # keep going\n\ndef step(run, limits):\n    reason = check_brakes(run, limits)\n    if reason:\n        return finish(run, stop_reason=reason, partial=run.best_so_far)\n    return run_next_step(run)","caption":"Return a stop reason and the best partial result so callers can retry, escalate or show it."},{"t":"p","md":"Count the right tokens. Reasoning tokens are billed as output, and OpenAI advises reserving at least 25,000 tokens for reasoning plus output when starting out, because hitting `max_output_tokens` returns `status: incomplete` and can happen before any visible output [[r34]]. Count input tokens as well: in a naive loop, input dominates because every step resends the history."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"A cap is not an outcome","md":"Throwing an exception when the cap fires discards the work. Return a typed stop reason, the best partial result and the trace, then let policy decide: retry with a bigger budget, ask a person, or show the partial answer with a warning."}],"whereToUse":["Every agent loop with tool calls, whether ReAct, plan-and-execute or reflection","Group chats and handoff meshes, where several agents can keep passing control","Autonomous background runs that no one watches in real time","Per-user or per-tenant spend limits on agentic features","Evaluation harnesses, so a stuck test case cannot burn the whole budget"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A model decides when to continue","Tools or model calls cost money or time","Runs are unattended or multi-agent"],"avoid":["Using only a huge default cap and calling it a budget","Relying on a prompt line such as stop after five tries","Setting the cap so low that normal tasks hit it and users see failures"],"flips":["Normal runs hit the cap often: raise it or fix the loop, then keep repeat and budget brakes","Cost is the main risk: budget by cost, not steps","Runs are interactive: add a short deadline and stream partial progress"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Kinds of brake","head":["Brake","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Step or turn cap","You need a hard, simple bound","Steps vary a lot in cost","Cheap, blunt"],["Token or cost budget","Spend is the risk","Counted late or only on output tokens","Needs metering in the harness"],["Wall-clock deadline","A user or SLA waits","Slow tools make it fire for the wrong reason","Partial work needs handling"],["Repeat or no-progress detection","Loops are stuck, not long","Legitimate retries look like repeats","Needs a signature of calls and state"],["Prompt instruction only","Never the only one","Always: the model can ignore it","No enforcement"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A stuck agent runs for hours before anyone notices","cause":"The only brake is a very high default cap, such as 1000 steps in LangGraph [[tr4]]","fix":"Set a task-specific step cap, a cost budget and a deadline, and alert on stop reasons."},{"symptom":"Runs end with an incomplete status and no text","cause":"The output limit was consumed by reasoning tokens [[r34]]","fix":"Reserve headroom for reasoning plus output and treat incomplete as a stop reason with a retry policy."},{"symptom":"The cap fires and the user sees a raw error","cause":"The exception was not turned into a result","fix":"Return the stop reason and the partial result, then route to retry, escalate or warn."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t10-s1","title":"Unattended agent with paid tools","situation":"A scheduled agent calls paid third-party tools and a large model. No one watches it, and a runaway loop would show up first on the invoice.","constraints":["Unattended runs","Per-call tool cost","Failures are found late"],"unknowns":["What is a normal run's step count and cost?","Who gets alerted when a stop reason fires?"],"question":"Which brakes do you set?","options":[{"name":"Step cap near 2x normal, a cost budget per run, a deadline, repeat detection and alerts on every stop reason","verdict":"best","why":"Layered brakes catch different failures, and tying them to normal behaviour keeps false stops rare.","tradeoff":"You must measure normal runs first and tune thresholds over time."},{"name":"Only a step cap at the framework default","verdict":"poor","why":"A default such as 1000 super-steps lets a runaway spend a lot before it trips.","tradeoff":"A framework default suits a throwaway script where cost does not matter."},{"name":"A cost budget only","verdict":"good","why":"It bounds spend directly.","tradeoff":"A cheap-but-stuck loop can still run for a long time and block resources."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tools are free and runs are short","then":"A step cap and deadline are enough."},{"if":"Runs are billed to different tenants","then":"Apply budgets per tenant as well as per run."}],"takeaway":"Brake on what hurts: cost, time and repetition, not only steps."},{"id":"w10-t10-s2","title":"Interactive chat agent","situation":"A chat agent answers questions with up to a handful of tool calls. Users stay only for about thirty seconds, and a few conversations contain odd inputs that make the agent circle.","constraints":["Interactive, latency sensitive","Tool calls are cheap","Rare but visible loops"],"unknowns":["What does a typical run's step count look like?","Can partial answers be shown?"],"question":"How do you set the guards?","options":[{"name":"Low step cap, a thirty-second deadline, repeat detection and a graceful fallback message with the partial result","verdict":"best","why":"It matches the user's patience and turns loops into a clear message instead of a hang.","tradeoff":"A few legitimate long tasks will hit the cap, so track them."},{"name":"No cap, trust the model to finish","verdict":"poor","why":"A circling model never gets cut off, and the user waits for nothing.","tradeoff":"Only acceptable in a controlled demo."},{"name":"A generous cap of 50 steps with no deadline","verdict":"good","why":"Rarely interrupts a legitimate task.","tradeoff":"A loop can burn a minute of wall-clock before it stops."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tasks are known to be long, such as research","then":"Move the run to the background with progress updates and larger budgets."},{"if":"Users abandon at fifteen seconds","then":"Tighten the deadline and stream partial progress sooner."}],"takeaway":"Tune brakes to the user's patience and to normal run shape."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t10-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A naive loop starts with a 2,000 token prompt. Each step appends 1,500 tokens of call and result, and every model call rereads the full context. How many input tokens are read across 10 steps in total?","answer":87500,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"tokens","hint":"Step k reads 2,000 + 1,500 x (k - 1) tokens.","explain":"Sum over k = 1 to 10: 10 x 2,000 + 1,500 x (0 + 1 + ... + 9) = 20,000 + 1,500 x 45 = 87,500. Growth is quadratic, which is why budgets must count input tokens and why trimming and caching matter."},{"id":"w10-t10-q2","type":"multi","tag":"bug","q":"Which of these loop designs have a real bug or gap?","options":["The only brake is a prompt line telling the model to stop after five tries","The harness raises an exception on max steps and drops the partial result","A cost budget is checked before each step and returns a stop reason","max_turns is set to None in production to avoid failures"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"A prompt line cannot enforce a stop, dropping partial work wastes the spend, and disabling the turn limit removes the brake [[tr3]]. Checking a budget before each step and returning a reason is the sound design.","ref":"tr3","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t10-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A reasoning-model agent frequently returns status incomplete with no visible text. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The tools are down","The output token limit was used up by reasoning tokens","The step cap is too high","Temperature is too low"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reasoning tokens count as output, so a tight max_output_tokens can be exhausted before any visible output appears. OpenAI advises reserving at least 25,000 tokens when starting out. [[r34]]","ref":"r34","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does LangGraph's recursion limit count, and what is the default?","back":"Maximum super-steps per run, raising GraphRecursionError at the limit. The default is 1000 steps from version 1.0.6 [[tr4]]."},{"front":"What does the OpenAI Agents SDK do at max_turns?","back":"It raises MaxTurnsExceeded. Passing max_turns=None disables the limit [[tr3]]."},{"front":"What should a brake return besides stopping?","back":"A typed stop reason and the best partial result, so policy can retry, escalate or warn."},{"front":"Why count input tokens in a budget?","back":"A naive loop resends the whole history each step, so input tokens usually dominate and grow roughly quadratically."}],"related":["w10-l2","w10-t07","w10-t14","w10-t15","w10-t21"],"refs":["r1","r9","r10","r11","r34","tr1","tr3","tr4"]},{"id":"w10-t11","order":11,"group":"Multi-agent","title":"Single vs multi-agent: the decision","minutes":10,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Start with one agent. Add agents only when a named dial forces it: breadth that exceeds one context, tool overload, separate trust boundaries, or independent reads that can run in parallel.","useIf":"Someone proposes a team of agents and you must decide whether the extra cost, latency and failure modes are justified.","avoidIf":"Do not use a crew to fix a weak prompt, poor tools or missing evals, and do not parallelise coupled writes.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The vendors who build agents agree on the default. Microsoft says to use the lowest level of complexity that reliably meets your requirements and calls a single agent with tools often the right default for enterprise use cases [[r9]]. OpenAI says to maximise a single agent's capabilities first, and to split when prompts hold many if-then-else branches or when tool selection fails after you clarify tool descriptions. Tool overload depends on similarity: some systems handle more than 15 distinct tools while others struggle with fewer than 10 overlapping ones [[r19]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"The six dials, one agent against many","head":["Dial","Single agent","Multi-agent","Evidence"],"rows":[["Latency","Serial loop, one context","Parallel reads cut time, handoffs and synthesis add hops","Parallel subagents cut research time by up to 90 percent, vendor-reported [[r2]]"],["Cost","About 4x chat tokens","About 15x chat tokens","Anthropic's own traffic [[r2]]"],["Context isolation","One window fills up","Each worker gets a fresh window","Anthropic and LangChain list isolation as a benefit [[r6]][[r32]]"],["Debuggability","One trace","Many traces and message hops","MAST: 14 failure modes across 7 frameworks [[r5]]"],["Blast radius","One identity holds all tools","Per-agent tools and identities","Separate security boundaries justify the cost [[r9]]"],["Consistency","Single-threaded decisions","Parallel writers make conflicting choices","Cognition 2025-06-12 and 2026-04-22 [[r3]][[r4]]"]]},{"t":"p","md":"The evidence is not contradictory. Anthropic's lead-plus-subagents system beat a single agent by 90.2 percent on its internal research eval, which is breadth-first reading. Cognition argued against parallel designs in 2025 and, on 2026-04-22, kept that view for parallel writers while reporting that agents contributing intelligence works when writes stay single-threaded and a clean-context reviewer checks the work [[r2]][[r3]][[r4]]. Both are vendor-reported."},{"t":"dials","id":"w10-t11-pick","title":"Single agent or which crowd?","prompt":"Flip the dials to see which topology wins.","dials":[{"id":"coupling","label":"Subtask coupling","values":[{"id":"reads","label":"Independent reads"},{"id":"writes","label":"Coupled writes"}]},{"id":"value","label":"Value per run","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Low"},{"id":"high","label":"High"}]},{"id":"boundary","label":"Trust boundaries","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One boundary"},{"id":"split","label":"Separate teams or data"}]}],"options":[{"id":"single","name":"Single agent with tools","summary":"One loop, one context, one identity."},{"id":"writer","name":"One writer plus read-only helpers","summary":"Helpers investigate, one agent writes."},{"id":"orch","name":"Orchestrator-workers","summary":"Lead plans, workers run in parallel."},{"id":"mesh","name":"Handoff mesh of specialists","summary":"Control transfers between owners."}],"scores":{"single":{"coupling":{"reads":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but long reading fills one context."},"writes":{"s":2,"why":"Single-threaded decisions stay consistent."}},"value":{"low":{"s":2,"why":"Cheapest option wins when value per run is low."},"high":{"s":0,"why":"Fine, but may leave quality on the table."}},"boundary":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"No need to split permissions."},"split":{"s":-2,"why":"One identity would hold every team's tools."}}},"writer":{"coupling":{"reads":{"s":1,"why":"Helpers parallelise reading."},"writes":{"s":2,"why":"One writer, helpers only read: Cognition's working shape."}},"value":{"low":{"s":-1,"why":"Extra calls rarely pay back."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Extra reading improves quality."}},"boundary":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"split":{"s":-1,"why":"Helpers share the writer's trust boundary by default."}}},"orch":{"coupling":{"reads":{"s":2,"why":"Breadth-first reads are the best case."},"writes":{"s":-2,"why":"Parallel writers conflict on shared artifacts."}},"value":{"low":{"s":-2,"why":"About 15x chat tokens needs high value."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Tokens buy breadth and quality."}},"boundary":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"split":{"s":1,"why":"Workers can hold separate tools and identities."}}},"mesh":{"coupling":{"reads":{"s":-1,"why":"Specialists work one at a time, not in parallel."},"writes":{"s":0,"why":"One owner at a time, but ownership moves."}},"value":{"low":{"s":-1,"why":"Every hop is another model call."},"high":{"s":0,"why":"Acceptable if specialists add value."}},"boundary":{"one":{"s":-1,"why":"Hops add risk with no boundary benefit."},"split":{"s":2,"why":"Each specialist keeps its own tools and permissions."}}}},"takeaway":"Name the dial that forces each added agent. If you cannot, stay on one."}],"whereToUse":["Architecture reviews where a team proposes a crew of agents","Interview answers that ask whether a workload needs multiple agents","Budget reviews: estimating the token multiplier before building","Deciding when a growing tool list should be split across specialists","Explaining to stakeholders why a single agent is the default"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Independent reads can run in parallel and value per run is high","One agent's tool list is overloaded after you improved descriptions [[r19]]","Different teams, data or permissions need separate agents and identities"],"avoid":["Subtasks are coupled writes to one artifact","A prompt, tool or eval problem is the real cause of poor quality","Value per run does not cover roughly 15x tokens"],"flips":["Writes are coupled: keep one writer and make the rest read-only","Trust boundaries are separate: multi-agent is justified even at higher cost","Your evals show no gain over the single-agent baseline: collapse back to one"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Topologies compared","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Single agent with tools","One domain, coupled work, tight budget","Breadth overflows context or tools overload","Long runs fill the context"],["Single writer plus read-only helpers","Reads parallelise, writes must stay consistent","Little reading to parallelise","Writer is a serial bottleneck"],["Orchestrator-workers","Breadth-first, high-value research","Coupled writes, low value","About 15x chat tokens [[r2]]"],["Handoff mesh","Specialists take over a conversation","Route is obvious at the start","Loops and bouncing [[r9]]"],["Group chat","Review and debate of a draft","No objective stop test","Transcript cost grows each round [[r9]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The crew costs far more than the single agent and quality is about the same","cause":"No baseline comparison on a golden set","fix":"Measure the single-agent baseline first and keep the crew only if it wins on your eval."},{"symptom":"Two agents produce contradicting pieces of one deliverable","cause":"Parallel writers made implicit decisions without seeing each other's actions [[r3]]","fix":"One writer, read-only helpers, and full traces shared instead of summaries."},{"symptom":"Nobody can find where a run went wrong","cause":"Failures hide in handoffs, verification gaps or misaligned goals [[r5]]","fix":"Trace every hop, add an independent verifier and review traces with a MAST-style checklist."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t11-s1","title":"A tool list that keeps growing","situation":"A single agent has grown to dozens of tools from several teams. It picks the wrong tool about one request in five, and each team wants its own agent.","constraints":["One trust boundary","Many tools, several with overlapping purposes","Tool descriptions have not been reviewed"],"unknowns":["Do overlapping tool names and descriptions explain the errors?","Can tools be loaded on demand instead of all at once?"],"question":"What do you do first?","options":[{"name":"Clarify and de-duplicate tool descriptions, load tools on demand, and split into specialists only if selection still fails","verdict":"best","why":"OpenAI's guidance is to split after clearer descriptions fail, and overlap is the usual cause of tool overload.","tradeoff":"It takes careful tool review work, and the split may still be needed later."},{"name":"Split immediately into one agent per team, behind a supervisor","verdict":"good","why":"It reduces tool lists per agent and gives each team ownership.","tradeoff":"More hops and tokens, and the supervisor must still route correctly."},{"name":"Raise the model's reasoning effort and keep every tool","verdict":"poor","why":"More thinking does not fix ambiguous tool descriptions and it raises cost on every call.","tradeoff":"Reasonable once descriptions are clean and errors persist on hard cases."}],"flips":[{"if":"Teams need separate permissions","then":"Split by trust boundary regardless of tool count."},{"if":"Errors vanish after clearer descriptions","then":"Stay with one agent."}],"takeaway":"Fix the tools before you add agents."},{"id":"w10-t11-s2","title":"Separate data owners","situation":"Two teams own sensitive data in different systems with different permission models. One assistant must answer questions that touch both, and a prompt injection in one source must not reach the other.","constraints":["Separate trust boundaries","Different permission models","Cross-domain questions are common"],"unknowns":["Does each system support delegated user identity?","How are results combined without leaking raw data?"],"question":"What do you build?","options":[{"name":"One agent per data owner with its own identity and tools, called by a supervisor that sees approved results only","verdict":"best","why":"Permissions stay with the owner and an injection in one source cannot reach the other tools.","tradeoff":"More tokens, more hops and a combining step to maintain."},{"name":"One agent with the union of both teams' permissions","verdict":"poor","why":"A single injection could reach everything, and the blast radius is the whole union.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only when both systems sit under one owner and one trust boundary."},{"name":"One agent using the user's delegated identity for both systems","verdict":"good","why":"Downstream systems enforce the user's rights, so access is bounded by the caller.","tradeoff":"An injected instruction can still misuse the user's combined rights in one context."}],"flips":[{"if":"One team owns both systems","then":"A single agent becomes reasonable."},{"if":"Rights are enforced only in the agent","then":"Do not rely on delegation, split the agents."}],"takeaway":"Trust boundaries can justify agents that parallel reads never would."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t11-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which reading fits both Anthropic's research result and Cognition's argument?","options":["Multi-agent always beats a single agent","Single agents always beat multi-agent","Parallel reads across agents can pay off, but parallel writers to one artifact conflict","Both posts say never use more than two agents"],"answer":2,"explain":"Anthropic's gain came from breadth-first reading with a lead and subagents. Cognition's concern was implicit conflicting decisions by parallel writers, and its 2026-04-22 follow-up kept single-threaded writes. [[r2]][[r4]]","ref":"r4","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t11-q2","type":"multi","tag":"flip","q":"Which dial changes justify splitting one agent into several?","options":["Tool selection keeps failing after tool descriptions were clarified","The team wants more impressive diagrams","Different data owners need separate identities and permissions","The prompt has several if-then-else branches that a single prompt handles poorly"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"OpenAI names branching prompts and failing tool selection as signals, and Microsoft names separate security boundaries. Diagram appeal is not a dial. [[r19]][[r9]]","ref":"r19","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t11-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A single agent run costs 40,000 tokens. Using Anthropic's reported multipliers, roughly how many tokens would a multi-agent run use if a typical chat is 10,000 tokens and multi-agent systems use about 15x chat?","answer":150000,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"tokens","hint":"Apply 15x to the chat baseline, not to the agent run.","explain":"The multipliers are relative to chat: 15 x 10,000 = 150,000 tokens. The agent run at 40,000 is about 4x chat, so the multi-agent run is roughly 3.75x the agent run. These are vendor-reported averages from one system. [[r2]]","ref":"r2","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"OpenAI's two signals to split a single agent?","back":"Prompts with many if-then-else branches, and tool selection that still fails after clearer tool descriptions. Tool overload depends on overlap [[r19]]."},{"front":"Cognition's updated view in 2026-04-22?","back":"Parallel writers still fail. Multiple agents contributing intelligence works when writes stay single-threaded and a clean-context reviewer checks [[r4]]."},{"front":"What is the token cost of each rung, per Anthropic?","back":"About 4x chat for an agent and about 15x for a multi-agent system, vendor-reported [[r2]]."}],"related":["w10-l3","w10-l7","w10-t01","w10-t05","w10-t12","w10-t16"],"refs":["r2","r3","r4","r5","r6","r9","r11","r19","r32"]},{"id":"w10-t12","order":12,"group":"Multi-agent","title":"Supervisor pattern","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A central supervisor agent talks to the user and calls specialist agents as tools. Control always returns to the supervisor, which stays accountable for the final result.","useIf":"One agent must own the outcome, audit trail and conversation, while specialists supply expertise, tools or isolated context.","avoidIf":"A specialist should take over and talk to the user directly, or every result flowing through one context would overflow it.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"In the **supervisor** pattern (also called manager or agent-as-tool) the main agent exposes each specialist as a tool. LangChain describes it as subagents: the main agent calls subagents as tools, and all routing passes through it [[r32]]. OpenAI's guide names the same shape the manager pattern and contrasts it with decentralised handoffs [[r19]]. Microsoft's handoff documentation states the difference in ownership: with agent-as-tools the primary agent keeps ownership and control returns to it, whereas in a handoff the receiving agent takes full ownership [[r11]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t12-d1","title":"Supervisor with specialists as tools","alt":"The user talks to a supervisor. The supervisor calls specialist agents as tools and receives their results. Large outputs are stored as artifacts and referenced.","src":"flowchart TB\n  U[User] <--> S[Supervisor agent]\n  S -->|brief| A[Specialist A]\n  S -->|brief| B[Specialist B]\n  S -->|brief| C[Specialist C]\n  A -->|summary + artifact id| S\n  B -->|summary + artifact id| S\n  C -->|summary + artifact id| S\n  S --> R[(Artifact store)]"},{"t":"p","md":"The supervisor's strength is also its limit. Everything passes through one context, so it needs the right level of detail. If specialists return long free text, the supervisor's window fills and quality falls. If it paraphrases results, detail is lost: Cognition's first principle is to share full agent traces, not only messages [[r3]]. A practical split is a short structured result plus an artifact reference that the supervisor can open when it needs the details. Each specialist call is also a model round trip, and Anthropic found that synchronous subagents created bottlenecks [[r2]], so independent specialists should run in parallel with timeouts."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"What a specialist tool contract holds","md":"A clear name and description for routing, an input schema with the objective and constraints, an output schema with a status, a short summary and artifact ids, and the specialist's own budget and timeout."}],"whereToUse":["Assistants that must keep one conversation and one accountable answer","Regulated flows where an audit trail needs a single owner of the decision","Enterprise assistants that wrap several domain agents behind one front door","Workflows where specialists differ in tools, models or permissions","Systems that want to add or retire specialists without changing the user experience"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The caller must stay accountable and see every result","Specialists have distinct tools, prompts or permissions","Results can be summarised without losing what the next step needs"],"avoid":["The user should converse with the specialist directly","Latency budget cannot absorb an extra hop per delegation","Outputs are huge and cannot be summarised or referenced"],"flips":["Users need to talk to specialists directly: use handoff","The supervisor's context overflows: return artifact references and summaries, or add a second tier","Only a few simple tools: collapse the specialists back into one agent"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Supervisor (this topic)","One owner, audit trail, specialists as tools","Specialist must converse with the user","Hub bottleneck, extra hop per call"],["Handoff","A specialist should take over the user","You need one accountable owner","Loops and bouncing [[r9]]"],["Orchestrator-workers","Subtasks are decided at run time","Roles are fixed and named","Dynamic briefs, 15x tokens [[r2]]"],["Single agent with skills","One agent loads on-demand prompts and knowledge","Specialists need different tools or identities","One context carries it all [[r32]]"],["Group chat","Review and debate of a draft","Clear delegation is enough","Transcript cost grows [[r9]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The supervisor loses track as results arrive","cause":"Each specialist returns long free text into one context","fix":"Return a status, a short summary and an artifact id, and let the supervisor fetch details on demand."},{"symptom":"Answers contradict specialist findings","cause":"The supervisor paraphrased results and dropped details [[r3]]","fix":"Pass structured fields and artifact references, and quote evidence rather than restating it."},{"symptom":"Every request pays for a supervisor hop it does not need","cause":"Simple requests are routed through the full delegation loop","fix":"Route obvious cases directly, or let the supervisor answer simple ones itself."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t12-s1","title":"One accountable owner","situation":"An assistant coordinates a refund decision across policy, order history and fraud-check specialists. The company must be able to show who decided and why, and users expect one consistent voice.","constraints":["Single accountable owner","Specialists have different tools and permissions","Audit trail required","Moderate latency tolerance"],"unknowns":["Can each specialist return structured evidence?","Which actions need human approval?"],"question":"Which topology do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Supervisor calling the specialists as tools and recording each result","verdict":"best","why":"Control returns to one owner, so the decision and its inputs sit in one trace, and each specialist keeps its own permissions.","tradeoff":"An extra hop per specialist and a hub that must not be overloaded."},{"name":"Handoff mesh among the three specialists","verdict":"poor","why":"Ownership moves between agents, which muddies accountability and risks bouncing.","tradeoff":"Right when the user should talk directly to whichever specialist takes over."},{"name":"One agent holding all three toolsets","verdict":"good","why":"Simplest and fastest.","tradeoff":"One identity holds every permission, which enlarges the blast radius of an injection."}],"flips":[{"if":"The three domains share one trust boundary and few tools","then":"A single agent is simpler and cheaper."},{"if":"The user must debate the outcome with a specialist","then":"Hand off to that specialist, with the supervisor keeping the audit record."}],"takeaway":"When one party must own the outcome, keep control at the hub."},{"id":"w10-t12-s2","title":"A hub that fills up","situation":"A supervisor calls a dozen research specialists that each return several thousand tokens of findings. Answer quality has fallen and runs are slow, but the specialists themselves score well in isolation.","constraints":["Many specialists with large outputs","Hub quality degrades with context size","Latency is climbing"],"unknowns":["Which findings does the supervisor actually need verbatim?","Can specialists run in parallel?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Specialists return status, a short summary and artifact ids, run in parallel, and the supervisor opens artifacts only when needed","verdict":"best","why":"It keeps the hub's context small while keeping full detail retrievable, and parallel calls cut wall-clock time.","tradeoff":"More engineering for artifact storage and a risk that the summary omits a needed fact."},{"name":"Keep passing full outputs and switch to a larger context window","verdict":"poor","why":"Quality degrades with length well below advertised windows, and cost and latency rise.","tradeoff":"Fine for short runs where the total stays small."},{"name":"Replace the supervisor with a handoff mesh","verdict":"good","why":"No single hub to fill.","tradeoff":"You lose the central owner and add loop risk, and each agent still carries the shared history."}],"flips":[{"if":"A summary keeps dropping critical facts","then":"Add structured fields the specialist must always fill."},{"if":"Specialists number over twenty","then":"Add a router tier so each supervisor sees fewer tools."}],"takeaway":"Keep the hub's context for decisions, and keep the evidence in storage."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t12-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"In an agent-as-tool design, who owns the conversation after the specialist returns?","options":["The specialist, which talks to the user","The user, who chooses the next agent","The primary agent, since control returns to it","No one, ownership is shared in the transcript"],"answer":2,"explain":"With agent-as-tools the primary agent keeps ownership and control returns to it. In a handoff, the receiving agent takes full ownership. [[r11]]","ref":"r11","difficulty":1},{"id":"w10-t12-q2","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"A supervisor's answers get worse as it calls more specialists. Which causes are plausible?","options":["Specialist outputs are long and fill its context","It paraphrases results and loses details","Specialists run in parallel","The supervisor has too many overlapping specialist tools to choose from"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Context fill, lossy paraphrase and tool overload all degrade a hub. Running independent specialists in parallel reduces latency and does not by itself reduce quality. [[r3]][[r19]]","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Supervisor versus handoff in one line?","back":"Supervisor: specialists are tools and control returns to the primary agent. Handoff: the receiving agent takes full ownership [[r11]]."},{"front":"What should a specialist return to a supervisor?","back":"A status, a short structured summary and artifact references, so the hub keeps its context small and can open details on demand."},{"front":"What is the supervisor's main bottleneck?","back":"All routing and results pass through one context and one model hop per delegation, so overload and latency grow with the number of specialists [[r32]]."}],"related":["w10-l3","w10-t05","w10-t11","w10-t13"],"refs":["r32","r19","r11","r3","r2","r9"]},{"id":"w10-t13","order":13,"group":"Multi-agent","title":"Handoff and swarm","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"In a handoff, one agent transfers control and the conversation to another agent that takes full ownership. Agents form a mesh with no central orchestrator, and each hop is a model call that can loop.","useIf":"The right specialist is not known up front, emerges during the conversation, and should talk to the user directly.","avoidIf":"The route is identifiable from the first message (use a router), you need one accountable owner, or work should run in parallel.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Handoff** means an agent decides to pass the task to another agent, and the receiver takes over. Microsoft Agent Framework describes handoff as a mesh topology with no central orchestrator: the receiving agent takes full ownership and has the full context, and handoff supports `Agent` only, with agents that support local tool execution [[r11]]. The Azure guide adds that agents in this pattern do not typically work in parallel, and full control moves from one agent to another [[r9]]. The OpenAI Agents SDK exposes a handoff to the model as a tool, named `transfer_to_<agent_name>` by default [[r20]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t13-d1","title":"A handoff mesh with escape hatches","alt":"A user reaches a triage agent. Triage hands off to billing or technical agents. Specialists can return to triage or escalate to a human.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[User] --> T[Triage agent]\n  T -->|transfer_to_billing| B[Billing agent]\n  T -->|transfer_to_technical| X[Technical agent]\n  B -->|back to triage| T\n  X -->|back to triage| T\n  B -->|escalate| H[Human agent]\n  X -->|escalate| H"},{"t":"p","md":"Two design choices control behaviour. **State passing**: by default the next agent sees the history, but you can shape it. The Agents SDK offers `input_filter` to trim what the receiver sees, `input_type` for structured handoff arguments, `on_handoff` for side effects, `is_enabled` to switch a target on or off and `nest_handoff_history` [[r20]]. **Loop control**: The Azure guide lists infinite handoff loops and bouncing between agents as a risk [[r9]]. Handoff is interactive by default and returns to the user when an agent answers without handing off. Autonomous mode keeps an agent working until it hands off, a termination condition holds or the per-agent turn limit is hit, for example `WithAutonomousMode(turnLimit: 10)` in the C# builder [[r11]]. OpenAI's Swarm library introduced the idea of agents and handoffs and is labelled experimental and educational, replaced by the Agents SDK [[tr17]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Each handoff is a model call","md":"A conversation that hops through three specialists pays for three agent turns before the answer. Cap hops, define which agents may hand to which, and log every transfer with its reason."}],"whereToUse":["Customer-support flows where a triage agent hands to billing, technical or account specialists","Domain tutors or advisors where the user converses with the specialist","Multi-skill assistants where expertise requirements appear during the conversation","Escalation paths that end in a human agent","Prototypes built on the Agents SDK or Agent Framework handoff orchestration"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The best agent is unknown until the conversation reveals it","Specialists should own and continue the dialogue","A clear signal tells an agent it has reached its limit [[r9]]"],"avoid":["The right agent is clear from the first input, so a deterministic router suffices [[r9]]","Poor routing would badly hurt the user experience","Avoiding loops and bouncing is hard to guarantee"],"flips":["You need one accountable owner: use a supervisor with agents as tools","Multiple operations must run at once: use concurrent orchestration","Bouncing appears in traces: add a hop cap and an allow-list of handoff targets"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Handoff (this topic)","Specialist should take over, route emerges","Route is known up front, one owner needed","Loops, bouncing, a model call per hop [[r9]]"],["Router","Categories are clear from the first message","Route changes mid-conversation","Misroute risk [[r1]]"],["Supervisor","One accountable owner, results come back","Specialist must converse with the user","Hub bottleneck [[r32]]"],["Group chat","Several agents refine one draft","Linear delegation is enough","Transcript cost grows [[r9]]"],["Single agent with skills","One agent can load on-demand knowledge","Specialists need separate tools or permissions","One context carries all [[r32]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A conversation bounces between two agents","cause":"Each agent's instructions say the other is better suited, with no hop limit","fix":"Set a hop cap, restrict handoff targets, add a termination condition and an escalate-to-human path."},{"symptom":"The new agent repeats questions the user already answered","cause":"History was filtered too hard or not passed at all","fix":"Pass a structured summary of facts gathered, and use the history filter deliberately, not by default."},{"symptom":"Hand-offs go to the wrong specialist","cause":"Overlapping specialist descriptions","fix":"Rewrite descriptions with clear boundaries, and evaluate handoff decisions on labelled conversations."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t13-s1","title":"Conversation that changes topic","situation":"A support assistant starts with account questions, and halfway through the user raises a billing dispute and then a technical fault. Specialists have different tools and policies. Users expect continuity.","constraints":["Topic emerges mid-conversation","User should talk to the specialist","Different tools and policies per specialist","A human escalation exists"],"unknowns":["How many hops does a typical conversation need?","What state must pass between agents?"],"question":"What orchestration do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Handoff mesh with a hop cap, target allow-list, structured state passing and human escalation","verdict":"best","why":"Specialists take over the dialogue as topics change, and the guards contain the loop risk.","tradeoff":"A model call per hop and a need to evaluate handoff decisions."},{"name":"Supervisor with specialists as tools","verdict":"good","why":"One consistent voice and accountable owner.","tradeoff":"Users never talk to the specialist, and every turn pays a hub hop."},{"name":"A router that classifies only the first message","verdict":"poor","why":"The topic changes later, so the first classification becomes wrong.","tradeoff":"Right when the topic is fixed by the first message."}],"flips":[{"if":"Accountability must sit with one agent","then":"Use a supervisor and keep the specialist as a tool."},{"if":"Traces show frequent bouncing","then":"Tighten descriptions, cap hops and review routing evals."}],"takeaway":"Handoff fits conversations whose topic moves, if you cap the hops."},{"id":"w10-t13-s2","title":"Route known from the first message","situation":"An internal bot receives requests from a form that already tags the department. Latency matters, and each department has its own agent. No one expects the topic to change.","constraints":["Route known at the start","Latency sensitive","Stable categories"],"unknowns":["Does the tag come from a trusted field?","What happens if a request is mistagged?"],"question":"How do you route?","options":[{"name":"Deterministic dispatcher on the form tag, with a fallback agent for mistagged requests","verdict":"best","why":"The route is known, so no model call is needed to choose, and latency stays low.","tradeoff":"A mistagged request needs a fallback path."},{"name":"Triage agent with handoff mesh","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds a model hop and loop risk where a field lookup would do.","tradeoff":"Right if the tag were missing or untrusted and content had to decide."},{"name":"One agent with every department's tools","verdict":"good","why":"Simple to ship.","tradeoff":"Tool overload and broad permissions in one agent."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tags are unreliable","then":"Add a classifier router before the department agents."},{"if":"Requests often cross departments","then":"Introduce handoff or a supervisor."}],"takeaway":"If code can pick the route, do not pay a model to pick it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t13-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"In the OpenAI Agents SDK, how is a handoff exposed to the model by default?","options":["As a tool named transfer_to_<agent_name>","As a system message","As a separate API endpoint","As a retrieval index"],"answer":0,"explain":"Handoffs are modelled as tools, and the default tool name is transfer_to_ followed by the agent name, which you can override with tool_name_override. [[r20]]","ref":"r20","difficulty":1},{"id":"w10-t13-q2","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Traces show conversations bouncing between two agents. Which changes address it?","options":["Add a hop cap","Restrict which agents each agent may hand off to","Add a termination condition","Give both agents longer instructions about each other"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"A cap, a target allow-list and a termination condition are the named controls for loops. Longer mutual instructions often make bouncing worse. [[r11]]","ref":"r11","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does the receiving agent get in an Agent Framework handoff?","back":"Full ownership of the task and the full context. It is a mesh topology with no central orchestrator [[r11]]."},{"front":"Name four handoff options in the OpenAI Agents SDK.","back":"tool_name_override, on_handoff, input_type and input_filter, plus is_enabled, tool_description_override and nest_handoff_history [[r20]]."},{"front":"What is OpenAI Swarm's status?","back":"Experimental and educational, replaced by the OpenAI Agents SDK, per its README checked 2026-10-13 [[tr17]]."}],"related":["w10-l3","w10-t03","w10-t12","w10-t10"],"refs":["r11","r9","r20","r1","r32","tr17"]},{"id":"w10-t14","order":14,"group":"Multi-agent","title":"Group chat and debate","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Several agents share one conversation, and a manager picks who speaks next until a stop rule fires. It suits review, maker-checker loops and structured debate, and it costs more with every round and every agent.","useIf":"The goal is better judgement from several perspectives on one artifact, there is an objective stop test, and the agents are mostly read-only.","avoidIf":"Plain delegation or a linear pipeline is enough, latency is tight, or nothing can say when the discussion is done.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Group chat** orchestration models a collaborative conversation among agents coordinated by an orchestrator that decides who speaks next. In Agent Framework the agents form a star topology around the orchestrator, speaker selection can be round-robin, prompt-based or custom, and all agents see the full conversation history [[tr1]]. The Azure guide says the pattern suits brainstorming, debate, quality control and maker-checker loops, that agents are typically read-only, and that all output lands in one accumulating thread, which gives transparency and auditability [[r9]]. **Debate** is a special case: multiple model instances propose answers and argue over several rounds to reach a common one, which the original paper reports improves reasoning and factual validity [[tr6]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t14-d1","title":"Star topology with a chat manager","alt":"A chat manager sits in the middle of three agents. It selects the next speaker, all agents read the shared transcript, and a stop rule ends the chat.","src":"flowchart TB\n  M{Chat manager}\n  M --> A[Maker]\n  M --> B[Checker]\n  M --> C[Domain reviewer]\n  A --> T[(Shared transcript)]\n  B --> T\n  C --> T\n  T --> M"},{"t":"formula","title":"Why chat cost grows faster than the number of messages","expr":"input_tokens = sum over turns i of (task + message_size x i)","vars":[{"k":"turn i","v":"every agent rereads the whole transcript before replying"}],"example":"3 agents, 4 rounds, 12 messages of 300 tokens on a 500 token task: 12 x 500 + 300 x (0 + ... + 11) = **25,800 input tokens**, while the transcript itself is only 4,100 tokens."},{"t":"p","md":"Control matters more than creativity. Microsoft advises limiting group chat to three or fewer agents, because more agents make loops harder to control, and says to avoid it when the chat manager has no objective way to tell the task is complete [[r9]]. Stop rules exist in code: `MaximumIterationCount` on the C# round-robin manager, and `termination_condition` and `max_rounds` in the Python builder [[tr1]]. A maker-checker loop also needs acceptance criteria, an iteration cap and a fallback such as escalation to a person [[r9]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Let a human chair","md":"The Azure guide notes humans can take the chat manager role to steer the conversation, which turns group chat into a supervised review session [[r9]]."}],"whereToUse":["Design or proposal reviews with distinct reviewer roles","Maker-checker loops for content, code review or compliance checks","Decision-support sessions where a person joins the thread","Ideation where agents with different knowledge sources build on each other","Factual or reasoning tasks where debate is measured to help"],"whenToUse":{"use":["An objective test says when the discussion is done","Agents are read-only or write only to the transcript","Value of a second and third perspective outweighs the transcript cost"],"avoid":["Linear pipelines or simple delegation are enough","Real-time response is required","More than about three agents are needed to coordinate [[r9]]"],"flips":["The stop test is vague: fix the acceptance criteria first, or use a fixed round count","Cost climbs with rounds: cap rounds and summarise the transcript between them","Agents need to take actions: split them out, keep chat for judgement only"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Group chat (this topic)","Review, debate, maker-checker, human can join","No stop test, many agents, tight latency","Quadratic transcript cost [[r9]]"],["Evaluator-optimizer","One generator and one critic, clear criteria","Several perspectives are needed","Two calls per round"],["Voting (concurrent)","Independent opinions, no discussion needed","Reasoning needs cross-examination","N times the cost, no interaction"],["Supervisor","Delegation to specialists","You want perspectives to react to each other","Hub bottleneck"],["Single stronger model","Quality is limited by capability, not perspective","A critique role adds independent checks","Higher per-call cost"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The chat never ends or rambles","cause":"No objective completion test and no iteration cap [[r9]]","fix":"Set maximum rounds, a termination condition on a checkable state and a fallback on cap."},{"symptom":"Agents agree politely and add no value","cause":"Roles are vague and share the same model, prompt and context","fix":"Give agents distinct criteria, evidence sources or models, and measure whether the chat improves outcomes."},{"symptom":"Token cost far exceeds the value of the result","cause":"Every agent rereads the whole transcript every turn","fix":"Limit agents and rounds, summarise earlier rounds and compare against a single stronger call."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t14-s1","title":"Review with a person in the loop","situation":"A team wants proposals reviewed from cost, risk and user-impact perspectives before a human decision. The reviewers do not change systems, and a manager will join the thread.","constraints":["Read-only agents","Human can join","Objective stop: all three reviewers sign off or the manager stops it","Latency of minutes is fine"],"unknowns":["How many rounds usually settle the review?","What happens when reviewers disagree at the cap?"],"question":"Which orchestration fits?","options":[{"name":"Group chat with three reviewers, a round cap and a human chair","verdict":"best","why":"It matches the pattern's strengths: read-only roles, multiple perspectives, an audit-friendly thread and human steering.","tradeoff":"Transcript cost grows each round and the cap needs a fallback."},{"name":"Three independent reviewers in parallel, merged by one summariser","verdict":"good","why":"Cheaper, because reviewers do not reread each other.","tradeoff":"No cross-examination, so disagreements surface only in the merge."},{"name":"Handoff mesh among reviewers","verdict":"poor","why":"Reviewers take turns owning the task rather than building on each other, and loop risk rises.","tradeoff":"Right for conversations where one reviewer should take over the user."}],"flips":[{"if":"Reviews are independent and volume is high","then":"Run parallel reviewers and merge."},{"if":"No human is available at the cap","then":"Return the transcript with unresolved issues flagged."}],"takeaway":"Chat suits review where the transcript is the product."},{"id":"w10-t14-s2","title":"Debate for a factual question","situation":"A product wants to improve accuracy on a factual question-answering feature, and a proposal says to have three models debate for four rounds. Latency is interactive and cost per query matters.","constraints":["Interactive latency","Cost sensitive","Ground truth can be checked with retrieval"],"unknowns":["Does debate beat a retrieval check on your golden set?","What share of queries are hard enough to need more than one pass?"],"question":"Do you adopt multi-round debate?","options":[{"name":"Add retrieval-based verification first, and debate only on queries the verifier flags","verdict":"best","why":"An external check beats opinion, and debate cost is paid only where it may help.","tradeoff":"Needs a verifier and routing logic."},{"name":"Run three-model, four-round debate on every query","verdict":"poor","why":"It multiplies latency and tokens on all traffic for gains you have not measured here.","tradeoff":"Right for offline, high-stakes analysis where cost and time are secondary."},{"name":"Keep one call and tune the prompt","verdict":"good","why":"Cheapest and fastest.","tradeoff":"No added accuracy mechanism for hard cases."}],"flips":[{"if":"The task is offline and high stakes","then":"Debate or maker-checker becomes affordable."},{"if":"Retrieval verification does not catch the errors","then":"Measure debate on the failing slice only."}],"takeaway":"Debate is a costly last lever, not a default."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t14-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"What does Microsoft's guide say about the number of agents in a group chat?","options":["Use at least ten for diversity","The number does not affect control","Always match the number of tools","Consider limiting it to three or fewer, since more agents make loops harder to control"],"answer":3,"explain":"The guide recommends limiting group chat to three or fewer agents to keep control, and to avoid it when the manager cannot objectively decide completion. [[r9]]","ref":"r9","difficulty":1},{"id":"w10-t14-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Three agents run a round-robin chat for 4 rounds, so 12 messages. The task prompt is 500 tokens, each message adds 300 tokens and each agent reads the whole transcript before replying. How many input tokens are read in total?","answer":25800,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"tokens","hint":"Message i (starting at 0) reads 500 + 300 x i tokens.","explain":"Sum over i = 0 to 11 of (500 + 300 i) = 12 x 500 + 300 x 66 = 6,000 + 19,800 = 25,800. The final transcript holds only 500 + 3,600 = 4,100 tokens, but it is reread at every turn."},{"id":"w10-t14-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A group chat keeps running because reviewers never agree. Which change helps most?","options":["Add more reviewers","Define acceptance criteria the manager can test, and cap rounds with a fallback","Raise the temperature of all agents","Let agents choose their own speaking order with no manager"],"answer":1,"explain":"A manager needs an objective completion test and a cap, otherwise the loop has no end [[r9]]. More reviewers and higher temperature add noise, and removing the manager removes the stop control.","ref":"r9","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does Agent Framework group chat choose the next speaker?","back":"An orchestrator in a star topology uses round-robin, prompt-based or custom selection. All agents see the full conversation history [[tr1]]."},{"front":"Name the stop controls in Agent Framework group chat.","back":"MaximumIterationCount on the C# round-robin manager, and termination_condition and max_rounds in the Python builder [[tr1]]."},{"front":"Why does group chat cost grow faster than its message count?","back":"Every agent rereads the whole transcript before each reply, so input tokens grow roughly quadratically with turns."}],"related":["w10-l3","w10-t06","w10-t10","w10-t16"],"refs":["tr1","r9","tr6"]},{"id":"w10-t15","order":15,"group":"Multi-agent","title":"Dynamic task ledgers (Magentic)","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A manager agent builds a task ledger of facts and a plan, tracks progress each round, picks the next specialist and replans when work stalls. It targets open-ended problems with no predetermined path.","useIf":"The problem is open-ended, the plan must emerge and be revised, and agents need tools that act on external systems.","avoidIf":"The path is deterministic, the task is time-sensitive, or you cannot afford the planning overhead and an unproven pattern.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Magentic** orchestration comes from Magentic-One, in which an Orchestrator plans, tracks progress and re-plans to recover from errors, while specialised agents drive a browser, files and code execution. The paper reports statistically competitive results on GAIA, AssistantBench and WebArena and ships AutoGenBench for evaluation [[r31]]. In Microsoft Agent Framework the manager keeps shared context, builds a **task ledger**, tracks progress and re-plans. It uses the same architecture as group chat, but the manager plans instead of only picking speakers [[r10]]. The Azure guide places it at the open-ended end, where agents typically have tools that make changes in external systems and the focus is as much on building and documenting the approach as on carrying it out [[r9]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t15-d1","title":"The Magentic loop","alt":"The manager gathers facts and writes a plan, then loops: update the progress ledger, pick an agent, let it act, and check for a stall. Repeated stalls trigger a replan, and too many resets end the run.","src":"flowchart TB\n  T[Task] --> P[Facts and plan: task ledger]\n  P --> L[Progress ledger: next agent?]\n  L -->|next agent| A[Specialist acts]\n  A --> L\n  L -->|stalled| R[Replan]\n  R --> L\n  L -->|done| F[Final answer]\n  R -->|too many resets| X[Stop]"},{"t":"p","md":"The builder exposes the brakes: a maximum number of coordination rounds, a maximum number of consecutive stalls before replanning and a maximum number of plan resets. The documentation sample sets 10, 3 and 2, shown as `max_round_count=10, max_stall_count=3, max_reset_count=2` in Python and `WithMaxRounds(10)`, `WithMaxStalls(3)` and `WithMaxResets(2)` in C# [[r10]]. There is also a flag for human review of the plan before execution (`RequirePlanSignoff` in C#). Custom prompts are supported, but a custom `progress_ledger_prompt` must preserve the built-in JSON response schema [[r10]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Experimental status, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Microsoft states it is untested how well Magentic performs outside the original Magentic-One design. The C# types are experimental and samples suppress diagnostic MAAIW001. The Azure guide advises against it when the task is time-sensitive or the path is deterministic [[r9]]."}],"whereToUse":["Open-ended investigations that mix browsing, files and code execution","Complex analyses where the approach is as valuable as the answer","Tasks where a human should review the plan before costly steps","Research on generalist agent teams, evaluated on benchmarks such as GAIA","Prototypes where you want a visible plan and progress record"],"whenToUse":{"use":["No predetermined plan exists and it must adapt","Specialists need real tools (browser, code, files)","Time is available for planning rounds and replans"],"avoid":["The task is time-sensitive or the path is deterministic [[r9]]","A fixed workflow or plain handoff would do","You need a mature, well-tested pattern for production without your own evals"],"flips":["Plans repeat across runs: freeze them as a workflow","Stalls dominate traces: tighten agent roles and tools, or lower reset limits","Stakes are high: require plan sign-off and approvals on write tools"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Magentic (this topic)","Open-ended, plan must emerge and adapt","Time-sensitive or deterministic work","Planning overhead, experimental status [[r10]][[r9]]"],["Group chat","Review and debate of a draft","Work needs a plan and tool use","Manager only picks speakers [[tr1]]"],["Plan-and-execute","One agent, stable tool chain","Several specialists with different tools","Brittle plans"],["Orchestrator-workers","Parallel read-only subtasks","Long sequential plan with replanning","Coordination cost [[r2]]"],["Workflow","Known path","Open-ended","Branch growth"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The run loops through replans with no progress","cause":"Stall detection fires but agents lack the tool or role to make progress","fix":"Lower max resets, improve specialist tools and descriptions, and return a partial result with the ledger."},{"symptom":"Plans look reasonable but the result is wrong","cause":"No independent verification of intermediate findings","fix":"Add a verifier agent or external check on key facts before the final answer."},{"symptom":"Costs are far above a single agent","cause":"Manager and every specialist reread a large shared context","fix":"Cap rounds, compact the ledger, and compare against a single-agent baseline on a golden set."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t15-s1","title":"Open-ended multi-tool investigation","situation":"Analysts ask for investigations that need web research, data files and some code, and nobody can list the steps in advance. Each result is high value, and runs may take many minutes. A lead wants to see the plan first.","constraints":["No predetermined plan","Specialists need browser, file and code tools","High value per run","Plan review required"],"unknowns":["Does it beat a single agent on a golden set?","Which actions need approval?"],"question":"What orchestration do you use?","options":[{"name":"Magentic-style manager with a task ledger, plan sign-off, round, stall and reset limits","verdict":"best","why":"The plan has to emerge and adapt, the pattern provides a visible ledger and the brakes bound runaway planning.","tradeoff":"Slower and costlier than a single agent, and Microsoft calls performance outside the original design untested."},{"name":"Single agent with all tools and a long context","verdict":"good","why":"Simpler and cheaper, and may be enough.","tradeoff":"Long runs strain one context and give no explicit plan to review."},{"name":"Fixed workflow of search, analyse, report","verdict":"poor","why":"The path is not known, so a fixed order fails on cases it did not anticipate.","tradeoff":"Right once investigations settle into a repeating shape."}],"flips":[{"if":"A baseline single agent matches quality","then":"Drop the manager and keep the simpler design."},{"if":"Investigations repeat the same steps","then":"Convert the common plan to a workflow."}],"takeaway":"Use a planner only where the plan cannot be written in advance."},{"id":"w10-t15-s2","title":"Known runbook, tight deadline","situation":"An operations team wants an agent to execute an incident runbook whose steps are well documented. Responses must start within seconds and complete in minutes.","constraints":["Deterministic path","Time-sensitive","Write actions on production systems"],"unknowns":["Which steps need human approval?","How often does a step fail and need an alternative?"],"question":"Which pattern fits?","options":[{"name":"Workflow with fixed steps, approvals on writes and a bounded agent branch for exceptions","verdict":"best","why":"The path is known and time matters, so code owns the order and the model handles only the exceptions.","tradeoff":"New runbook steps need code changes."},{"name":"Magentic orchestration with a planning manager","verdict":"poor","why":"Planning rounds add latency and variability to a path that does not need planning, and the docs advise against it for deterministic or time-sensitive work.","tradeoff":"Right for novel incidents with no runbook."},{"name":"Single ReAct agent with all runbook tools","verdict":"good","why":"Flexible and simpler than a manager.","tradeoff":"Step order is not guaranteed and write actions need strict gates."}],"flips":[{"if":"An incident has no runbook","then":"Use an exploratory agent or Magentic with plan sign-off."},{"if":"Runbook steps are conditional on many factors","then":"Add routing steps within the workflow."}],"takeaway":"Planning is a cost. Pay it only for uncertainty."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t15-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does the Magentic manager do that a group chat manager does not?","options":["Selects which agent speaks next","Builds a task ledger, tracks progress and replans","Limits the number of rounds","Lets agents see the shared conversation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both managers pick speakers, share context and can cap rounds. The distinguishing function is planning: building the ledger, tracking progress and re-planning. [[r10]]","ref":"r10","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t15-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which signals argue against using Magentic orchestration?","options":["The task is time-sensitive","The path is deterministic","The path cannot be planned in advance and agents need tools","A fixed workflow already solves the task"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Time pressure, deterministic paths and an existing workflow are reasons to avoid it [[r9]]. An unplannable path with tool-using agents is its intended home [[r9]].","ref":"r10","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does the Magentic manager keep, and what does it do on a stall?","back":"A task ledger and a progress record. After consecutive stalls it replans, and too many plan resets end the run [[r10]]."},{"front":"Which sample limits does the Agent Framework Magentic doc show?","back":"max_round_count=10, max_stall_count=3 and max_reset_count=2 in Python, with matching WithMaxRounds, WithMaxStalls and WithMaxResets in C# [[r10]]."},{"front":"What is Magentic's status caveat, checked 2026-10-13?","back":"Untested outside the original Magentic-One design. C# types are experimental, with diagnostic MAAIW001 suppressed in samples [[r10]]."}],"related":["w10-l3","w10-t08","w10-t14","w10-t10"],"refs":["r10","r31","r9","r2","tr1"]},{"id":"w10-t16","order":16,"group":"Multi-agent","title":"Multi-agent failure modes","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"MAST, a failure taxonomy built from real traces, groups 14 failure modes into system design issues, inter-agent misalignment and task verification. Most are visible in traces if you look for them.","useIf":"You are reviewing, debugging or evaluating a multi-agent system and need a checklist of how such systems actually fail.","avoidIf":"You have one agent with no handoffs. A multi-agent taxonomy then adds little, and single-agent loop failures matter more.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"*Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail?* introduces **MAST**, the Multi-Agent System Failure Taxonomy. The authors built it from 150 traces with expert annotators (inter-annotator agreement kappa 0.88), then released **MAST-Data**, more than 1,600 annotated traces from 7 multi-agent frameworks, plus an LLM-as-a-judge annotator. The taxonomy has 14 failure modes in 3 categories. The paper was first submitted on 2025-03-17 and revised on 2025-10-26 [[r5]]. Its opening observation is that performance gains from multi-agent systems on popular benchmarks are often minimal, so understanding failures matters."},{"t":"table","caption":"The three MAST categories, mapped to detection and control","head":["Category","Example modes in the paper","What to look for in a trace","Control"],"rows":[["System design issues","Disobey task or role specification, step repetition, loss of conversation history, unaware of termination conditions","Repeated steps, ignored constraints, runs that never end","Clear roles and specs, hop and step caps, termination rules"],["Inter-agent misalignment","Conversation reset, failure to ask for clarification, task derailment, information withholding, ignoring another agent's input, reasoning-action mismatch","One agent has a fact another never received, goals drift, an action contradicts the stated reasoning","Structured messages, shared state, full traces, clarification steps"],["Task verification","Premature termination, no or incomplete verification, incorrect verification","Run declares success without a check that could fail","Independent verifier, external checks, completion criteria"]]},{"t":"p","md":"The verification finding is the most actionable. The paper describes a generated chess program that passed superficial checks even though the verifier was prompted to be thorough, and argues for more rigorous verification with external knowledge and collected test output [[r5]]. This matches Cognition's report that a review loop works best when the reviewer shares no context with the coder [[r4]] and Anthropic's advice to resume from checkpoints because errors compound in stateful agents [[r2]]. Treat the taxonomy as a review checklist: sample traces, label them by mode, and fix the largest bucket first. The authors' LLM judge can automate labelling, but you still calibrate it against your own annotations."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Read the numbers with care","md":"Frequencies in the paper depend on the frameworks, models and tasks sampled. Use the modes as a vocabulary for your own traces, not as universal rates."}],"whereToUse":["Post-incident reviews of a multi-agent run that went wrong","Designing evaluation sets that target coordination and verification faults","Architecture reviews of a proposed crew, before you build it","Labelling production traces with a judge to find the largest failure bucket","Interview answers about why multi-agent systems underperform expectations"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You run two or more agents that pass work or messages","Runs fail in ways a single prompt fix does not explain","You need a shared vocabulary across engineers and reviewers"],"avoid":["Treating the percentages as universal failure rates","Fixing symptoms in prompts before you have labelled traces","Applying it to a single agent without handoffs"],"flips":["One bucket dominates your labelled traces: invest there first","Verification failures dominate: add an independent verifier with external checks","Inter-agent faults dominate: reduce agents, share full traces or merge roles"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to find multi-agent faults","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["MAST-style trace labelling","You have traces and need a failure vocabulary","No tracing exists yet","Annotation effort, judge calibration [[r5]]"],["Outcome-only evals","A final answer has a clear score","Failures hide behind lucky outputs","Misses process faults"],["Unit tests per agent","Each agent has a clear contract","Faults arise between agents","Misses coordination faults"],["Human spot review","Volume is low and stakes are high","Volume is high","Slow, inconsistent"],["Reduce to one agent","Failures cluster in coordination","Breadth truly needs parallel agents","Loses parallel reads [[r2]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The run reports success and a spot check fails","cause":"No or incomplete verification, a task verification failure [[r5]]","fix":"Add an independent verifier with a clean context and an external check such as tests or a schema."},{"symptom":"Agents repeat steps or never stop","cause":"Unaware of termination conditions, a system design failure [[r5]]","fix":"Put termination rules, hop caps and budgets in the harness and make them visible to agents."},{"symptom":"Agent B acts without a fact agent A already found","cause":"Information withholding or loss of history between agents","fix":"Pass structured state or artifact references, and share full traces instead of summaries [[r3]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t16-s1","title":"Success reported, results wrong","situation":"A multi-agent pipeline reports completion on most tasks, but spot checks find that a sizeable share of outputs do not meet the spec. A reviewer agent exists, and it nearly always approves.","constraints":["Traces exist for every run","A reviewer agent shares the generator's context","Spec can be partly checked by code"],"unknowns":["What share of failures does the reviewer miss on a labelled sample?","Which spec items can be tested in code?"],"question":"What do you fix first?","options":[{"name":"Replace the shared-context reviewer with an independent verifier that runs external checks, and calibrate it on labelled failures","verdict":"best","why":"This targets the verification category directly, and independence plus external evidence catches what a same-context reviewer approves.","tradeoff":"More cost per run and effort to build checks and a labelled set."},{"name":"Add more agents to the pipeline","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds coordination faults without fixing the missing verification.","tradeoff":"Right only when a specific capability is missing and measured."},{"name":"Tighten the generator prompts","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and may reduce some spec violations.","tradeoff":"It does not detect failures, so wrong outputs still pass."}],"flips":[{"if":"Failures cluster in handoffs","then":"Fix message structure and shared state before verification."},{"if":"A single agent passes the same evals","then":"Collapse the pipeline."}],"takeaway":"Verification needs independence and evidence."},{"id":"w10-t16-s2","title":"Where do we start with a failing crew?","situation":"A team's agent crew fails often and the causes look scattered. They have 500 traces, a judge model they trust somewhat, and one engineer-week.","constraints":["500 traces available","Limited review time","Judge is not yet calibrated"],"unknowns":["How well does the judge agree with human labels on a small sample?","Which failures cost the most?"],"question":"How do you spend the week?","options":[{"name":"Hand-label about fifty traces with the taxonomy, calibrate the judge on them, label the rest and fix the biggest bucket","verdict":"best","why":"It builds a vocabulary and a checked labeller, then aims effort at the largest bucket.","tradeoff":"Up-front annotation effort, and rates will reflect your system only."},{"name":"Trust the judge on all 500 traces without calibration","verdict":"poor","why":"Uncalibrated judges can mislabel systematically, so you may fix the wrong thing.","tradeoff":"Acceptable once agreement with human labels is measured."},{"name":"Rewrite every agent prompt","verdict":"good","why":"May lift quality somewhat.","tradeoff":"No diagnosis, so you cannot tell which change helped."}],"flips":[{"if":"Judge agreement with humans is high","then":"Label all traces with it and sample for audit."},{"if":"One failure is clearly costly","then":"Fix it first regardless of frequency."}],"takeaway":"Label first, then fix the biggest bucket."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t16-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which MAST category contains the failure mode no or incomplete verification?","options":["System design issues","Task verification","Inter-agent misalignment","Tool failure"],"answer":1,"explain":"MAST has three categories: system design issues, inter-agent misalignment and task verification. No or incomplete verification, incorrect verification and premature termination sit under task verification. Tool failure is not a MAST category. [[r5]]","ref":"r5","difficulty":1},{"id":"w10-t16-q2","type":"match","tag":"diagnose","q":"Match each trace symptom to the MAST category it most likely belongs to.","pairs":[{"l":"The agent repeats the same step many times and never stops","r":"System design issues"},{"l":"An agent ignores another agent's input and drifts off the task","r":"Inter-agent misalignment"},{"l":"The run ends with a success message and no check that could fail","r":"Task verification"}],"explain":"Step repetition and unawareness of termination are system design issues. Ignored input and task derailment are inter-agent misalignment. Declaring success without a check, or with a wrong one, is task verification. [[r5]]","ref":"r5","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are MAST's three categories?","back":"System design issues, inter-agent misalignment and task verification, covering 14 failure modes [[r5]]."},{"front":"What data underlies MAST?","back":"A taxonomy built from 150 traces (kappa 0.88), and MAST-Data with 1,600+ annotated traces across 7 frameworks, plus an LLM-as-a-judge annotator [[r5]]."},{"front":"Which failure is most actionable to fix first in many crews?","back":"Verification. Use an independent verifier with a clean context and external checks, since a same-context reviewer tends to approve [[r5]][[r4]]."}],"related":["w10-l3","w10-t06","w10-t11","w10-t13","w10-t14"],"refs":["r5","r4","r2","r3"]},{"id":"w10-t17","order":17,"group":"Context and memory","title":"Context engineering","minutes":10,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Context engineering is choosing what enters the model's window at every step: instructions, tool definitions, memory, retrieved data, history and tool results. The aim is the smallest set of high-signal tokens that makes the next step succeed.","useIf":"An agent runs many steps, uses many tools or large tool results, or the bill and latency are dominated by input tokens.","avoidIf":"Never skip it for long agent loops. For one short chat turn with a small prompt there is little to engineer.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Anthropic frames context as a finite resource with diminishing returns: the model has an **attention budget**, and every token competes with the others. The goal is the smallest set of high-signal tokens that makes the next step succeed [[r6]]. Cognition calls context engineering effectively the number one job of an agent builder [[r3]]. Five levers matter: what is in the prompt, in which order, how large tool results are, what history you keep and what you load only on demand."},{"t":"formula","title":"The window budget","expr":"used = system + tools + memory + history + retrieval + tool_results + reserved_output     (stay under the window)","vars":[{"k":"tools","v":"tool definitions, resent on every request"},{"k":"reserved_output","v":"room kept for the answer and any reasoning tokens"}],"example":"System 2,000 + 60 tools x 350 + memory 1,500 + 30 turns x 800 + 5 chunks x 900 + tool results 28,000 + output 6,000 = **87,000** tokens in a 100,000 window, leaving 13,000."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t17-d1","title":"A cache-friendly prompt layout","alt":"From top to bottom the prompt holds stable system instructions, tool definitions and memory, then append-only history, then volatile retrieval and tool results, then reserved output. The stable prefix can be cached.","src":"flowchart TB\n  A[System instructions: stable] --> B[Tool definitions: stable, fixed order]\n  B --> C[Memory: stable per session]\n  C --> D[History: append-only]\n  D --> E[Volatile retrieval and tool results]\n  E --> F[Reserved output]"},{"t":"p","md":"Order is a cost lever. Manus calls the KV-cache hit rate the single most important production metric, reports an average input to output ratio near 100 to 1, and quotes cached input at $0.30 per million tokens against $3 uncached for one Claude model in 2025 (check current pricing). Its rules: keep the prefix stable, so a timestamp at the start of the system prompt kills hits, make context append-only, serialise deterministically, and mask tools instead of removing them mid-run [[r7]]. Placement is also a quality lever: accuracy is highest when relevant text sits at the start or end of the input and lowest in the middle [[r39]], so keep critical constraints near the edges and recite the current plan near the end, as Manus does with a todo file [[r7]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Load data on demand. Keep lightweight identifiers such as paths, queries and links, and fetch content with tools when needed [[r6]]. Trim tool results to the fields the model needs and keep a pointer to the rest."},{"t":"lab","id":"context-budget","intro":"Illustrative planner, not a tokenizer: sizes, windows and the cache discount are your inputs, not vendor figures. Load the Tool-heavy agent preset and apply tool search, then compare summarising with dropping on the research preset, and switch the prompt to the cache-friendly order."}],"whereToUse":["Any agent that loops over tools for more than a handful of steps","Assistants with large tool catalogs from several teams","RAG assistants where retrieved chunks compete with history for space","Cost reviews where input tokens dominate the invoice","Latency work: shorter, cache-friendly prompts shorten time to first token"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The agent takes many steps and history grows","Tool definitions or tool results are large","Input tokens, not output, drive cost and latency"],"avoid":["Over-compressing early, which drops details that later steps need","Rewriting history every turn, which breaks cache reuse","Chasing a larger window instead of trimming what you send"],"flips":["Tool results are the bulk: trim or clear them first","Tool definitions are the bulk: load tools on demand with tool search","History is the bulk and stays relevant: summarise with pinned facts and offload detail to files"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Levers compared","head":["Lever","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Stable prefix and append-only context","Many steps share a long prefix","Content must be rewritten each step","Needs discipline in prompt assembly [[r7]]"],["Trim or clear tool results","Large results go stale","A later step needs the raw output","Keep a pointer to restore [[r6]]"],["On-demand retrieval","Data is large and mostly unused","Latency per fetch matters more","Extra tool calls [[r6]]"],["Bigger context window","A single task truly needs all of it","Quality drops with length","Context rot, cost, latency [[r8]]"],["Sub-agents with fresh windows","Branches are independent","Coupled work needs shared state","Tokens multiply [[r2]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Cached-input share collapsed after a prompt change","cause":"A volatile value such as a timestamp or retrieval block sits before stable content","fix":"Move volatile segments to the end, serialise tools and JSON deterministically and keep history append-only."},{"symptom":"The request overflows or quality drops before the user speaks","cause":"Dozens of tool definitions are loaded on every call","fix":"Load a small set of tools plus a search tool, and de-duplicate overlapping tools."},{"symptom":"The agent forgets an early instruction in a long run","cause":"It sits in the middle of a long context where recall is weakest [[r39]]","fix":"Pin instructions near the start, recite the current goal near the end and compact history."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t17-s1","title":"Cache hits fell after a release","situation":"After a prompt change, input cost per request rose sharply and time to first token worsened. Nothing else changed. The release added the current date and a retrieved snippet near the top of the system prompt.","constraints":["Provider supports prefix caching","Long shared prefix across requests","Cost and latency regression is measured"],"unknowns":["What does the cached-token field show per request?","Is tool order stable between requests?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Move the date and retrieval to the end, keep the prefix stable and serialise tools in a fixed order","verdict":"best","why":"Caches reuse the longest identical prefix, so a volatile value early in the prompt defeats them.","tradeoff":"Instructions that depend on the date must read it from later in the prompt."},{"name":"Switch to a larger model","verdict":"poor","why":"It does not touch the cause and raises per-token cost.","tradeoff":"Right only if quality, not cost, were the problem."},{"name":"Disable caching to avoid inconsistency","verdict":"good","why":"Removes the fragility.","tradeoff":"You pay full price for every token and give up latency savings."}],"flips":[{"if":"Prompts are shorter than the provider's minimum cacheable length","then":"Caching will not apply, so focus on trimming instead."},{"if":"Retrieval must precede instructions for quality","then":"Test the quality gain against the lost cache share."}],"takeaway":"Stable first, volatile last."},{"id":"w10-t17-s2","title":"A tool-heavy agent overflows","situation":"An agent exposes eighty tools from several teams and long tool outputs. Requests approach the window limit, and tool choice has become unreliable.","constraints":["Many tool definitions","Large tool outputs","Runs of dozens of steps","No budget to move to a bigger window"],"unknowns":["How much of the window do tool definitions take?","Can large outputs be reduced to key fields?"],"question":"What do you do first?","options":[{"name":"Load a few tools plus a tool search, trim tool results to key fields and clear stale results with pointers","verdict":"best","why":"It attacks the two largest segments with the least risk, and tool search also reduces overlap errors.","tradeoff":"An extra search round trip and the chance of missing a similar tool."},{"name":"Summarise the whole history every five steps","verdict":"good","why":"Frees space as the run grows.","tradeoff":"Loses exact details, costs an extra call and rewrites history, which breaks cache reuse."},{"name":"Raise the window by moving to a larger-context model","verdict":"poor","why":"Quality degrades with length and cost rises, while the overlap problem remains.","tradeoff":"Reasonable for one-off tasks that truly need the whole input."}],"flips":[{"if":"History is the largest segment","then":"Offload to files and summarise, after clearing results."},{"if":"Tool sets differ by trust boundary","then":"Split into sub-agents per boundary."}],"takeaway":"Trim the biggest, safest segment first."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t17-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which prompt order is most friendly to prefix caching?","options":["Retrieved chunks, system prompt, tools, history","Current date, system prompt, tools, history","System prompt, tools, memory, history, retrieval and tool results","History, system prompt, retrieval, tools"],"answer":2,"explain":"Stable segments go first, history is append-only and volatile segments go last, so the longest identical prefix is reused. The other orders put changing content ahead of stable content. [[r7]]","ref":"r7","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t17-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"System 2,000 + 60 tools at 350 tokens + memory 1,500 + 30 turns at 800 + 5 chunks at 900 + tool results 28,000 + reserved output 6,000. How many tokens of headroom remain in a 100,000 token window?","answer":13000,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"tokens","hint":"Sum all segments, then subtract from the window.","explain":"2,000 + 21,000 + 1,500 + 24,000 + 4,500 + 28,000 + 6,000 = 87,000, so headroom is 13,000. Remember the soft quality limit sits below the hard window."},{"id":"w10-t17-q3","type":"multi","tag":"bug","q":"Which practices in an agent's prompt assembly are bugs for cache hit rate?","options":["A timestamp at the start of the system prompt","Appending new turns to the end of history","Serialising tools in a different order on each request","Keeping the system prompt unchanged across turns"],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"A leading timestamp and unstable tool or JSON ordering change the prefix and defeat caching. Append-only history and an unchanged system prompt preserve the prefix. [[r7]]","ref":"r7","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the attention budget idea?","back":"Context is finite with diminishing returns. Aim for the smallest set of high-signal tokens for the next step [[r6]]."},{"front":"Manus's cache rules?","back":"Stable prefix (no leading timestamp), append-only context, deterministic serialisation, and mask tools instead of removing them [[r7]]."},{"front":"Where does the model recall best in long input?","back":"At the start and end of the input, with the lowest recall in the middle (Lost in the Middle) [[r39]]."}],"related":["w10-l4","w10-t18","w10-t19","w10-t11"],"refs":["r6","r3","r7","r39","r8","r2"]},{"id":"w10-t18","order":18,"group":"Context and memory","title":"Context rot and compaction","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Model quality degrades as input grows, often well below the advertised window. Control it by clearing stale tool results, offloading to files, summarising with care and isolating work in sub-agents.","useIf":"A run has many tool calls or turns, quality drops late in the run, or you approach the context limit.","avoidIf":"Do not compact short tasks, and do not use lossy summaries where exact wording, numbers or constraints must survive.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Context rot** is the loss of quality as input length grows. Chroma tested 18 models and found non-uniform degradation with longer inputs even on simple controlled tasks, and noted that needle-in-a-haystack tests only measure lexical retrieval [[r8]]. Other evidence points the same way: accuracy is highest when relevant text sits at the start or end and lowest in the middle [[r39]], and on RULER only about half of the 17 models tested kept satisfactory performance at 32K tokens despite claiming that length or more [[r33]]. On NoLiMa, 11 of 13 models claiming 128K or more fell below 50 percent of their short-context baseline at 32K, and GPT-4o dropped from 99.3 to 69.7 percent [[r22]]. Measure at your own lengths."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t18-d1","title":"Escalation ladder for a growing context","alt":"Five steps from cheapest to most invasive: clear stale tool results, offload to files with pointers, keep structured notes, compact history with a summary, isolate branches in sub-agents.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[1 Clear stale tool results] --> B[2 Offload to files with pointers]\n  B --> C[3 Structured notes]\n  C --> D[4 Compact history]\n  D --> E[5 Sub-agents with fresh windows]"},{"t":"p","md":"Anthropic calls tool result clearing one of the safest, lightest forms of compaction, and lists compaction, structured note-taking and multi-agent architectures as the long-horizon toolkit [[r6]]. Manus uses the file system as restorable external memory: drop bulky content but keep the URL or path so it can be fetched again [[r7]]. The Claude API offers context editing in beta (header `context-management-2025-06-27`, strategies `clear_tool_uses_20250919` and `clear_thinking_20251015`), and server-side compaction in beta, which replaces older turns with a summary Claude writes [[r30]][[tr15]]. Agent Framework's compaction framework is experimental and applies only to agents that keep history in memory, because service-managed agents such as Foundry agents or the Responses API with `store` enabled already manage context server-side [[tr14]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Compaction is lossy and cache-hostile","md":"A summary drops exact wording, numbers and rare constraints, costs an extra model call and rewrites history, which breaks prefix cache reuse for that part. Anthropic found compaction alone was not enough for long coding runs and added progress files and incremental sessions [[r14]]. Keep tool-call and result pairs together when trimming: Agent Framework treats them as one atomic group because dropping one half causes API errors [[tr14]]."}],"whereToUse":["Coding and research agents with dozens of tool calls per task","Long chat sessions where early turns lose relevance","Agents that browse or read large files and pages","Multi-hour runs that span several context windows","Cost and latency work on input-heavy workloads"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Quality or cost degrades as a run grows","Old tool results no longer matter but could be restored","Work branches are independent enough to run in fresh windows"],"avoid":["Summarising exact figures, identifiers or legal text","Compacting so often that cache reuse disappears","Using the largest window as a substitute for trimming"],"flips":["A later step may need old output: offload to a file and keep a pointer instead of dropping it","Facts must survive exactly: store them as structured notes and pin them","One window cannot hold independent branches: use sub-agents and return condensed results"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Techniques compared","head":["Technique","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Clear stale tool results","Results are bulky and rarely reused","A later step needs the raw data","Safe and cheap if a pointer remains [[r6]]"],["Offload to files","Detail must stay restorable","Reading the file costs more than keeping it","Needs a file tool and conventions [[r7]]"],["Structured notes","Decisions and facts must persist across windows","Notes drift from reality","Discipline to update them [[r6]]"],["Summarise or compact history","Narrative history is long and low-value","Exact details matter","Lossy, extra call, cache miss [[tr15]]"],["Sub-agents with fresh windows","Branches are independent","Work is coupled","More tokens, more coordination [[r2]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The agent contradicts a decision from earlier in the run","cause":"A summary dropped the decision or its reason","fix":"Write decisions to structured notes, pin them in context and summarise only narrative."},{"symptom":"API errors after trimming history","cause":"A tool call was removed without its result, or the reverse [[tr14]]","fix":"Trim whole call-and-result groups and let the framework's grouping handle it."},{"symptom":"Costs rise after adding summarisation","cause":"History rewrites break the cached prefix and each summary adds a model call","fix":"Compact at thresholds, not every turn, and keep the stable prefix untouched."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t18-s1","title":"Long coding run near the limit","situation":"A coding agent has made about a hundred tool calls. Most results are old file listings and test logs. Quality is slipping and the context nears its limit. The next steps only need the latest test output and the plan.","constraints":["Many stale tool results","Detail is restorable by re-running tools","A plan and progress file exist"],"unknowns":["Which results are still referenced?","Is the provider's context editing available?"],"question":"What do you apply first?","options":[{"name":"Clear stale tool results and keep pointers, update the progress notes, then compact only if still needed","verdict":"best","why":"Clearing is among the safest, lightest forms of compaction, and nothing is lost because tools can be re-run.","tradeoff":"A later step may need to re-fetch cleared data."},{"name":"Summarise the entire history into one paragraph","verdict":"poor","why":"It is lossy and can drop decisions and constraints, and it breaks cache reuse for the history.","tradeoff":"Right when history is mostly chat with little structure and a lossy summary is acceptable."},{"name":"Start a fresh session with only the original task","verdict":"good","why":"Cleanest context.","tradeoff":"Loses progress unless notes and files carry state, which is the harness job."}],"flips":[{"if":"Notes and files hold state reliably","then":"Fresh sessions per phase become safe and cheap."},{"if":"Results are small but numerous","then":"Compact in batches at a token threshold."}],"takeaway":"Clear first, summarise last."},{"id":"w10-t18-s2","title":"Exact figures must survive","situation":"A support assistant holds a thirty-turn conversation where order numbers, amounts and promised actions must stay exact. The context is growing and answer quality dips on later turns.","constraints":["Exact identifiers matter","Interactive latency","History is mostly chat"],"unknowns":["Which fields must be exact?","Do users revisit early turns?"],"question":"How do you manage the history?","options":[{"name":"Extract exact fields into a structured state object that is always included, and summarise only the narrative","verdict":"best","why":"Exact data stays lossless and the narrative shrinks safely.","tradeoff":"Needs schema design and a reliable extraction step."},{"name":"Free-text summary of the whole conversation","verdict":"poor","why":"Summaries paraphrase numbers and drop rare constraints.","tradeoff":"Fine for casual chat with no exact data."},{"name":"Drop the oldest turns after a fixed count","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and cheap.","tradeoff":"Forgets early commitments unless they are pinned elsewhere."}],"flips":[{"if":"Conversations stay under ten turns","then":"Keep full history and skip compaction."},{"if":"Compliance needs the full record","then":"Store the transcript separately and keep a compact working context."}],"takeaway":"Compact the prose, pin the facts."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t18-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What did the NoLiMa study find about models claiming 128K or more of context?","options":["All kept at least 90 percent of baseline at 32K","Context length had no effect on literal matching","Only open models degraded","At 32K, 11 of 13 fell below 50 percent of their short-context baseline"],"answer":3,"explain":"NoLiMa tested 13 models claiming 128K or more, and at 32K 11 fell below half of their short-context baseline, for example GPT-4o from 99.3 to 69.7 percent. Degradation was not limited to open models. [[r22]]","ref":"r22","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t18-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the context-management techniques from least to most invasive.","items":["Clear stale tool results","Offload bulky content to files with pointers","Compact history into a summary","Split independent branches into sub-agents"],"explain":"Clearing results is the lightest and safest. Offloading keeps content restorable. Summaries are lossy and rewrite history. Sub-agents change the architecture and multiply tokens. [[r6]]","ref":"r6","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t18-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"An agent using the Responses API with store enabled shows no effect from a client-side compaction strategy. Why?","options":["The service manages the conversation state, so client compaction has nothing to compact","Compaction only works with Python","The strategy needs a larger window","Compaction requires temperature zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"Agent Framework's compaction applies to agents that keep history in memory. Service-managed state, such as the Responses API with store enabled or Foundry agents, is handled server-side. [[tr14]]","ref":"tr14","difficulty":3}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What did RULER find about 32K context?","back":"Of 17 models tested, only about half kept satisfactory performance at 32K despite claiming 32K or more [[r33]]."},{"front":"Which Claude API settings control tool result clearing?","back":"Context editing (beta, header context-management-2025-06-27) with strategy clear_tool_uses_20250919, and clear_thinking_20251015 for thinking blocks [[r30]]."},{"front":"Why keep tool call and result together when trimming?","back":"Dropping one half causes API errors. Agent Framework treats them as one atomic message group [[tr14]]."}],"related":["w10-l4","w10-t17","w10-t05","w10-t19"],"refs":["r8","r39","r33","r22","r6","r7","r30","r14","r2","tr14","tr15"]},{"id":"w10-t19","order":19,"group":"Context and memory","title":"Memory types","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Agent memory has a short-lived working part (the context window) and long-term parts that persist: episodic (what happened), semantic (what is true) and procedural (how to do things). Name the type before you choose a store.","useIf":"An assistant should remember things across turns or sessions, and you must decide what to store, where and how it is read back.","avoidIf":"Do not add long-term memory when thread state suffices, or when you cannot meet privacy, deletion or isolation rules for what it stores.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The CoALA framework splits agent memory into **working memory** and long-term memory, and divides long-term memory into **procedural**, **semantic** and **episodic** [[r28]]. Working memory is what sits in the context window at the current step. Generative Agents keep a memory stream of natural-language experiences, synthesise reflections from them and retrieve dynamically [[tr16]]. MemGPT borrows the operating-system idea of tiers: virtual context management that moves information between memory tiers, using interrupts for control flow [[r29]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Memory types, stores and reads","head":["Type","Holds","Typical store","Read policy","Example"],"rows":[["Working","The current prompt, scratchpad, plan","Context window, thread state","Always present","Current task notes"],["Episodic","What happened in past interactions","Event log, summaries, vector index","Retrieve by similarity and recency","Last week's failed deploy"],["Semantic","Facts and preferences that stay true","Profile store, key-value, vector index","Load at session start or by relevance","User prefers metric units"],["Procedural","How to do recurring tasks","Instruction or notes files, prompts, skills","Load for matching workflows","Steps for the release checklist"]]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t19-d1","title":"Write path and read path","alt":"Conversation events pass through extraction and consolidation into long-term stores for episodic, semantic and procedural memory. At run time, relevant items are retrieved into the working memory in the context window.","src":"flowchart LR\n  E[Events] --> X[Extract]\n  X --> K[Consolidate]\n  K --> L[(Long-term memory store)]\n  L -->|retrieve| W[Working memory: context window]\n  W --> E"},{"t":"p","md":"Managed services follow this shape. Foundry Agent Service memory (preview, checked 2026-10-13) offers three types enabled by default: user profile, retrieved at conversation start; chat summary, retrieved per turn; and procedural memory, retrieved for recurring workflows. It runs phases of extraction, consolidation and retrieval, and is reached through the memory search tool on a prompt agent or the Memory Store APIs [[r13]]. In LangGraph, checkpointers store thread-scoped state, while stores hold cross-thread memory [[r43]]. Claude's memory tool is client-side: Claude asks for file operations under `/memories` and your application executes them [[r41]]. Mem0 is a vendor library whose paper reports gains on the LOCOMO benchmark; the results are vendor-authored [[r40]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Memory is more than a vector store","md":"A vector index suits episodic recall. Semantic facts often fit a profile record you can edit and delete, and procedural memory often fits plain text instructions that people can review."}],"whereToUse":["Personal assistants that should recall preferences across sessions","Support agents that need a short history of the customer's earlier cases","Coding agents that learn repository conventions and recurring procedures","Long-running agents that carry notes across context windows","Multi-agent systems that share facts through a common store"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Value comes from recall across sessions","You can name what type of fact each memory is","You can scope, expire and delete each memory"],"avoid":["Thread state or a profile form already covers the need","You cannot isolate memory per user or tenant","The deployment needs network isolation your memory service does not support"],"flips":["Strict network isolation: Foundry memory has no VNet support in preview, so use a store you control [[r13]]","Memory must be human-reviewable: prefer text notes over opaque vectors","Recall quality is poor: improve extraction and consolidation before adding more storage"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Memory approaches compared","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Thread state only","Continuity within one session is enough","Users expect recall next week","Cheapest, no persistence"],["Managed memory service (Foundry memory, preview)","You want extraction and consolidation as a service","Isolated networks, strict limits, preview risk","Limits and no VNet support [[r13]]"],["Library such as Mem0","You want a memory layer you host","You need vendor-neutral evidence","Vendor-reported results [[r40]]"],["Client-side memory files","You need full control and reviewable text","Large-scale semantic recall","You build path safety and storage [[r41]]"],["Bespoke database","Tight schema, audit and deletion needs","Speed to first version matters","You own extraction and retrieval"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The assistant recalls irrelevant or outdated facts","cause":"Everything is stored and retrieved by similarity alone","fix":"Consolidate and deduplicate, add recency and scope, and filter by type at retrieval."},{"symptom":"Memory from one user appears for another","cause":"A shared store with no scope key on reads","fix":"Require a scope such as user or tenant on every read and write, and test isolation."},{"symptom":"The agent repeats mistakes it already learned from","cause":"Lessons were never written as procedural memory","fix":"Write short, reviewable procedures after failures and load them for matching tasks."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t19-s1","title":"Personalisation under network isolation","situation":"An assistant should remember each user's preferences and ongoing projects. The deployment runs inside a private network, and the security team requires that memory data never leave it.","constraints":["Private network required","Per-user memory with deletion on request","Moderate volume"],"unknowns":["Does the managed memory service support your network setup?","Who reviews what gets stored?"],"question":"Which memory approach fits?","options":[{"name":"A store you control inside the network, with scoped profile records and extracted notes","verdict":"best","why":"It satisfies isolation and deletion, and a profile record plus notes covers semantic and episodic needs.","tradeoff":"You build extraction, consolidation and retrieval yourself."},{"name":"Foundry memory in preview","verdict":"poor","why":"As of 2026-10-13 it does not support VNet integration, and it is a preview service.","tradeoff":"A strong fit where network isolation is not required and preview limits are acceptable."},{"name":"No long-term memory, only thread state","verdict":"good","why":"Lowest risk and effort.","tradeoff":"Users must restate preferences each session."}],"flips":[{"if":"The managed service adds the needed network support and leaves preview","then":"Reconsider it to avoid building extraction yourself."},{"if":"No regulatory limits apply","then":"A managed or library-based layer saves build time."}],"takeaway":"Check isolation support before choosing a managed memory."},{"id":"w10-t19-s2","title":"Coding agent that should learn","situation":"A coding agent keeps asking the same questions about build commands and code conventions in each session. Engineers want the lessons stored where they can read and correct them.","constraints":["Lessons are procedures and conventions","Humans must review and edit","Single repository scope"],"unknowns":["How large are the notes after a month?","Which lessons are repository-wide versus personal?"],"question":"What do you store, and where?","options":[{"name":"Procedural notes as plain text files in the repository that the agent loads at session start and updates after failures","verdict":"best","why":"Procedures are small and reviewable, and files live with the code and can be corrected in review.","tradeoff":"Notes need pruning, and the agent needs rules for what is worth writing."},{"name":"Embed every past chat into a vector index","verdict":"poor","why":"Noisy retrieval returns stale or conflicting snippets, and nobody can audit it.","tradeoff":"Right for recalling specific past episodes by similarity at scale."},{"name":"No memory, a longer system prompt","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and reviewable.","tradeoff":"Prompt grows by hand, and cannot capture lessons from new failures automatically."}],"flips":[{"if":"Notes become too long to load","then":"Split by topic and load only matching files."},{"if":"Many repositories share lessons","then":"Add a shared semantic store with review."}],"takeaway":"Match the store to the memory type: procedures like text."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t19-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each memory type to the best example.","pairs":[{"l":"Working memory","r":"The plan and scratchpad in the current context window"},{"l":"Episodic memory","r":"Notes on what happened in last week's failed deployment"},{"l":"Semantic memory","r":"A user's preferred units and language"},{"l":"Procedural memory","r":"Steps for the team's release checklist"}],"explain":"Working memory is the current context. Episodic memory records past events, semantic memory holds lasting facts and preferences and procedural memory holds how-to knowledge. [[r28]]","ref":"r28","difficulty":1},{"id":"w10-t19-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which statement about Foundry Agent Service memory is accurate as of 2026-10-13?","options":["It is generally available with VNet support","It is a preview feature with no VNet support and per-store limits","It stores only chat summaries","It requires no embedding or chat model deployments"],"answer":1,"explain":"The docs mark it as preview, list user profile, chat summary and procedural memory, state a VNet limitation and need compatible chat and embedding deployments. [[r13]]","ref":"r13","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"CoALA's long-term memory types?","back":"Procedural, semantic and episodic, alongside working memory [[r28]]."},{"front":"Foundry memory types, status and one limit?","back":"Preview as of 2026-10-13: user profile, chat summary and procedural. Limits include 100 scopes per store and no VNet support [[r13]]."},{"front":"LangGraph checkpointer versus store?","back":"A checkpointer saves thread-scoped state. A store holds cross-thread memory [[r43]]."}],"related":["w10-l5","w10-t20","w10-t17","w10-t21"],"refs":["r28","tr16","r29","r13","r43","r41","r40"]},{"id":"w10-t20","order":20,"group":"Context and memory","title":"Memory policies, poisoning and privacy","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A memory layer needs written policies for what gets stored, how it is read, when it expires and how it is deleted. Every write is untrusted input, because attackers can plant memories through ordinary conversation.","useIf":"An agent writes to long-term memory, or reads memory that other users, tools or web content could influence.","avoidIf":"Do not rely on a memory store that you cannot scope per user, expire, audit and delete from.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Treat memory like any other data store that takes input from users and tools. A **write policy** says what is worth keeping: durable facts and preferences, not secrets, transient chatter or instructions found in tool output. A **read policy** says which memories may enter context: scoped to the user or tenant, relevant enough, recent enough, shown with provenance and treated as data, not commands. **Expiry** limits staleness and exposure. **Deletion** must reach every copy, including summaries and embeddings derived from the original."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t20-d1","title":"A memory write path with gates","alt":"A candidate memory is screened for injection and personal data, consolidated against existing items, tagged with scope, source and expiry, and stored. Reads filter by scope and show provenance.","src":"flowchart LR\n  C[Candidate memory] --> S[Screen: injection, PII]\n  S --> K[Consolidate: dedupe, resolve conflicts]\n  K --> T[Tag: scope, source, expiry]\n  T --> D[(Memory store)]\n  D -->|scope filter| R[Read with provenance]"},{"t":"p","md":"The threat is real. MINJA shows an attacker can plant malicious memory records through ordinary queries and outputs, with no direct write access to the memory bank [[r23]]. AgentPoison poisons long-term memory or a RAG store to install a backdoor, reporting average attack success above 80 percent at a poison rate below 0.1 percent with under 1 percent impact on benign use [[r24]]. Foundry's memory documentation advises Azure AI Content Safety prompt-injection detection plus adversarial testing [[r13]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Managed controls exist but are bounded. Foundry memory (preview, checked 2026-10-13) added item-level create, read, update and delete, a default TTL per store and direct remember-or-forget commands. Its limits are 100 scopes per store, 10,000 memories per scope and 1,000 search plus 1,000 update requests per minute, and low-level APIs need an explicit `scope` [[r13]]. On privacy, GDPR Article 17 gives data subjects a right to erasure without undue delay where listed grounds apply, with exceptions such as legal obligations and legal claims [[tr11]]. A deletion path that cannot remove a person's memories, derived summaries and index entries is a design gap, not a detail."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Memory is a persistence layer for injection","md":"A one-time injected instruction that lands in memory returns in every later session. Screen writes, record the source of each memory, and prefer user-confirmed writes for anything that changes behaviour."}],"whereToUse":["Any assistant with cross-session memory of user preferences or history","Agents that browse the web or read email and might store what they read","Multi-tenant systems where memory must not cross tenants","Compliance reviews of what an agent stores about people","Red-team plans for agents with write access to memory"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Every memory has a scope, source, timestamp and expiry","Writes from untrusted content are screened or require confirmation","A tested path deletes by user and scope across derived data"],"avoid":["Letting the model write any text it likes into shared memory","Storing sensitive data because it might be useful later","Keeping memories indefinitely with no review"],"flips":["Memory content can steer actions: require user confirmation or review before it becomes active","Regulated data is involved: store less, expire sooner and log access","Web or email content feeds the agent: quarantine memories derived from it with lower trust"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Write policies compared","head":["Policy","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Model writes freely","Low-stakes, single-user, private","Untrusted content or shared stores","Poisoning, privacy leaks [[r23]]"],["Screened writes (injection and PII checks)","General assistants","Attackers craft benign-looking text","Extra latency, false positives [[r13]]"],["User-confirmed writes","Memory changes behaviour or stores sensitive data","High-volume, low-value facts","Friction, but strong control"],["Rules-based extraction (schema fields only)","Fixed profile attributes","Open-ended episodic recall","Misses useful context"],["No long-term memory","Risk outweighs value","Personalisation is the product","Simplest to secure"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The agent changes behaviour across sessions after reading a web page","cause":"An instruction from untrusted content was stored as memory [[r23]]","fix":"Screen writes, tag the source, quarantine untrusted-source memories and require confirmation before they affect actions."},{"symptom":"A deletion request leaves traces in answers","cause":"Derived summaries or embeddings were not removed with the source item","fix":"Track lineage from source to derivatives and delete by user and scope across all of them."},{"symptom":"Stale facts keep winning over newer ones","cause":"No expiry, recency or conflict rule","fix":"Set a TTL, prefer newer items on conflict and show timestamps in retrieval."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t20-s1","title":"Erasure request in a multi-tenant assistant","situation":"A user asks the operator to erase everything stored about them. The assistant keeps profile facts, per-session summaries and embeddings, across several tenants.","constraints":["Per-user erasure obligation","Multiple derived artifacts","Tenant isolation required"],"unknowns":["Are there legal grounds that limit erasure?","Where else is the data copied: logs, backups, caches?"],"question":"What do you need in place?","options":[{"name":"Scoped memory with lineage from source to summaries and embeddings, and a tested delete-by-user path","verdict":"best","why":"It lets you remove every derived copy and prove it, and scoping prevents cross-tenant effects.","tradeoff":"More design work up front, plus a plan for logs and backups."},{"name":"Delete the raw chat only","verdict":"poor","why":"Summaries and embeddings still encode the personal data.","tradeoff":"Enough only if nothing derived is ever stored."},{"name":"Disable memory for that user and keep the data","verdict":"good","why":"Stops future use quickly.","tradeoff":"It does not erase the data, so the obligation is unmet."}],"flips":[{"if":"A legal obligation requires retention","then":"Restrict processing and document the exception."},{"if":"Memory is only thread-scoped and short-lived","then":"A TTL may satisfy deletion without extra machinery."}],"takeaway":"Design deletion before you ship the first memory."},{"id":"w10-t20-s2","title":"Browsing agent that remembers","situation":"A research agent reads web pages and stores notes in long-term memory that later runs trust. Some pages are written by people who benefit from steering the agent.","constraints":["Untrusted content feeds memory","Memory shapes future actions","Runs are unattended"],"unknowns":["Which memories can affect tool use?","Can sources be rated by trust level?"],"question":"How do you protect memory?","options":[{"name":"Tag every memory with its source, quarantine web-derived items as data only, screen for injection and require review before they influence actions","verdict":"best","why":"It limits persistence of injected instructions and keeps a trail for investigation.","tradeoff":"Slower learning and some review work."},{"name":"Store whatever the agent summarises and trust it later","verdict":"poor","why":"A poisoned page can plant a memory that returns in every run, as the research on memory poisoning shows.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only in a sandbox with no consequential tools."},{"name":"Disable memory for this agent","verdict":"good","why":"Removes the persistence risk.","tradeoff":"Loses cross-run learning, so each run restarts from scratch."}],"flips":[{"if":"Sources are all internal and trusted","then":"Lighter screening may be enough."},{"if":"Memories can trigger write tools","then":"Add approval gates on those tools."}],"takeaway":"Persisted text is persisted risk, so trace and gate it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t20-q1","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which controls reduce the risk of memory poisoning?","options":["Tag each memory with its source and trust level","Screen candidate memories for injection before storing them","Let any retrieved memory act as a system instruction","Require confirmation before untrusted-source memories affect actions"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Provenance, screening and confirmation limit persistence and use of planted instructions. Letting memory act as a system instruction gives attackers authority. [[r23]][[r24]]","ref":"r24","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t20-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does MINJA demonstrate?","options":["Memory can only be attacked with direct write access to the store","Attackers can plant malicious memory records through ordinary queries and outputs","Vector stores cannot be poisoned","Poisoning needs more than half of the memory bank"],"answer":1,"explain":"MINJA is a query-only attack: the attacker needs no direct write access to the memory bank. AgentPoison further shows success above 80 percent at a poison rate below 0.1 percent. [[r23]][[r24]]","ref":"r23","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What do AgentPoison's numbers show?","back":"Average attack success above 80 percent at a poison rate below 0.1 percent, with under 1 percent impact on benign use [[r24]]."},{"front":"Four parts of a memory policy?","back":"Write policy, read policy (scope, relevance, provenance), expiry and deletion that reaches derived summaries and embeddings."},{"front":"Foundry memory limits, preview as of 2026-10-13?","back":"100 scopes per store, 10,000 memories per scope, 1,000 search and 1,000 update requests per minute, no VNet support [[r13]]."}],"related":["w10-l5","w10-t19","w10-t22","w10-t17"],"refs":["r23","r24","r13","tr11"]},{"id":"w10-t21","order":21,"group":"Long-running agents","title":"Durable execution and checkpoints","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Long agent runs must keep their state outside the process, so a crash, deploy or long wait resumes from the last checkpoint instead of restarting. Checkpoints help you resume. Durable execution adds retries, timers and deterministic replay.","useIf":"A run lasts hours, waits for humans, calls flaky tools or must survive restarts and deployments.","avoidIf":"A run lasts seconds to minutes and restarting from scratch is cheap and safe. A plain in-process loop is simpler.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A checkpoint is a saved snapshot of run state. In Agent Framework workflows, checkpoints are written at the end of each superstep and capture executor state, pending messages, pending requests and responses and shared state. C# uses `CheckpointManager`, and from Python 1.13.0 an entry checkpoint is also written before the first superstep and when responses to request events arrive [[r21]]. LangGraph checkpointers store thread-scoped state for human-in-the-loop, time travel and fault tolerance. Its `interrupt()` needs a checkpointer and a `thread_id`, and waits indefinitely until resumed with `Command`; `InMemorySaver` loses state on restart, and old checkpoints should be pruned [[r43]][[r44]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t21-d1","title":"Resume instead of restart","alt":"Each step persists a checkpoint before the next step. After a crash or restart the run loads the latest checkpoint and continues from the next step.","src":"flowchart LR\n  S[Run step] --> P[(Persist checkpoint)]\n  P --> N[Next step]\n  N --> S\n  X[Crash or deploy] --> L[Load latest checkpoint]\n  L --> N"},{"t":"p","md":"**Durable execution** goes further. Temporal runs code as durable execution: workflow code must be deterministic because it is replayed, side effects live in Activities with retry policies, timeouts and heartbeats, and timers can last months [[r42]]. The Durable Extension for Agent Framework offers durable agents, multi-agent orchestrations and checkpointed workflows on Azure Functions or your own compute, in C# and Python (Go listed as coming soon), using Durable Task infrastructure such as the Durable Task Scheduler. Human-in-the-loop waits consume no compute and no model tokens, so a 24-hour approval costs only seconds of execution on Flex Consumption. Its install uses a prerelease flag (`--prerelease` for .NET packages, `--pre` for pip), and the page states no GA status, so check before promising production use [[r12]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Anthropic's production lessons are the same shape: agents are stateful and errors compound, so resume from where the agent was, combine model-level error handling with retries and regular checkpoints, and deploy with rainbow deployments so old and new versions run together while traffic shifts [[r2]]. Its Managed Agents design separates an append-only session log, the harness and the sandbox, so a new harness can resume with `wake(sessionId)` (beta, header `managed-agents-2026-04-01`) [[r15]]. For API-level asynchrony, OpenAI background mode runs a response asynchronously that you poll or cancel, and without `store=true` the data is kept only about 10 minutes [[r25]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"A checkpoint is not exactly-once","md":"Resuming from a checkpoint can repeat a step whose side effect already happened. Pair checkpoints with idempotency keys or compensating steps, and keep orchestration code deterministic when replay is used."}],"whereToUse":["Multi-hour research or data-processing agents","Workflows that wait for human approval for hours or days","Agents that call flaky or rate-limited tools and need retries with backoff","Systems deployed continuously while runs are in flight","Pipelines where partial progress is expensive to redo"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Runs last longer than a typical deploy or timeout","A human or external system may take hours to respond","Tool failures are common and restarts are expensive"],"avoid":["Short, cheap runs where restarting is safe","Teams without capacity to operate a durable runtime, unless a managed option fits","Treating in-memory checkpoint savers as durable"],"flips":["Waits grow from minutes to days: move to a durable wait that costs no compute","Side effects cannot repeat: add idempotency keys and compensations","Preview or unverified GA status blocks adoption: use framework checkpoints with your own store and test recovery"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Where a long run can live","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["In-process loop","Minutes-long runs, cheap restarts","Crashes, deploys or approvals interrupt it","Lost work"],["Framework checkpoints (Agent Framework, LangGraph)","You want resume and human-in-the-loop in your own runtime","You need timers, retries and replay guarantees","You operate storage, savers can be non-durable [[r43]]"],["Durable orchestration (Durable Task, Temporal)","Hours to days, retries, long timers, replay","Team cannot run it, or status is unverified","Determinism rules, ops effort [[r42]][[r12]]"],["Managed agent hosting","You prefer a hosted session and harness","You need full control of runtime","Beta status and vendor coupling [[r15]]"],["Provider background mode","One long model call, async polling","Multi-step tool orchestration","Data retention window of about 10 minutes [[r25]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"All progress is lost after a restart","cause":"State lived in memory, or the checkpoint saver was in-memory","fix":"Persist state to a durable store and test a kill-and-resume drill."},{"symptom":"A resumed run sends the same message twice","cause":"The step completed before the crash but its checkpoint was not written","fix":"Use idempotency keys on side effects and record completion before acting where possible."},{"symptom":"Replay produces a different path than the original run","cause":"Non-deterministic code, such as time or random values, runs inside replayed orchestration [[r42]]","fix":"Move side effects and non-determinism into activities and keep orchestration code deterministic."},{"symptom":"A deploy breaks runs in flight","cause":"Old runs resume on incompatible new code","fix":"Version workflows, or use rainbow deployments so old and new run side by side [[r2]]."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t21-s1","title":"Six-hour run with flaky tools","situation":"A data-processing agent runs for about six hours and calls external tools that fail roughly one call in twenty. The service redeploys several times a day, and restarting from scratch costs a lot.","constraints":["Six-hour runs","Frequent tool failures","Daily deploys","Costly restarts"],"unknowns":["Are the tool calls idempotent?","Can the team operate a durable runtime?"],"question":"Where does the run live?","options":[{"name":"Durable orchestration or checkpointed workflow with retries, versioned or rainbow deploys and idempotent activities","verdict":"best","why":"It resumes after crashes and deploys, retries failing calls with policy and keeps side effects safe.","tradeoff":"Determinism rules, operations effort and a status check on the runtime you choose."},{"name":"In-process loop with a retry wrapper","verdict":"poor","why":"Every deploy or crash discards hours of work.","tradeoff":"Right for short runs where a restart is cheap."},{"name":"Framework checkpoints in a local in-memory saver","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap to add and handles some failures.","tradeoff":"An in-memory saver loses state on process restart, so it fails the deploy case."}],"flips":[{"if":"Runs shrink to five minutes","then":"An in-process loop with a simple retry is enough."},{"if":"The durable runtime is preview and policy forbids it","then":"Use framework checkpoints with a durable store you operate."}],"takeaway":"Longer than a deploy means state must live outside the process."},{"id":"w10-t21-s2","title":"Short runs, cheap restart","situation":"A support agent finishes in under a minute. Tools are idempotent reads, and an interrupted run can be retried by the user. The team is small.","constraints":["Runs under a minute","Read-only tools","Small team","Retry by the user is acceptable"],"unknowns":["How often do interruptions occur?","Do any tools write?"],"question":"Do you add durable execution?","options":[{"name":"Keep an in-process loop with step cap, timeout and a user-visible retry","verdict":"best","why":"The cost of failure is tiny, so durable infrastructure would cost more than it saves.","tradeoff":"An interrupted run restarts from scratch."},{"name":"Adopt a durable orchestration platform","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds operational weight and determinism rules for no benefit here.","tradeoff":"Right once runs grow to hours or approvals appear."},{"name":"Persist checkpoints to a database after every step","verdict":"good","why":"Enables resume if needs grow.","tradeoff":"Added storage, schema and complexity before it is needed."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tools start writing to external systems","then":"Add idempotency keys first, then consider checkpoints."},{"if":"Approvals enter the flow","then":"Move to a durable wait."}],"takeaway":"Do not buy durability that the failure cost does not justify."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t21-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"When are Agent Framework workflow checkpoints written?","options":["Only when the process exits","After every individual token","At the end of each superstep","Only on human approval"],"answer":2,"explain":"Checkpoints are written at the end of each superstep and capture executor state, pending messages, pending requests and responses and shared state. Python 1.13.0 added entry checkpoints as well. [[r21]]","ref":"r21","difficulty":1},{"id":"w10-t21-q2","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"After a restart, a resumed run repeats a paid API call. Which changes address it?","options":["Send an idempotency key with the call","Switch the checkpoint saver to in-memory","Record completion before acting, or use a compensating step","Increase the step cap"],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"Idempotency keys and completion records or compensations make repeats safe. An in-memory saver loses state and a larger step cap does nothing for duplicates. [[r26]][[r27]]","ref":"r26","difficulty":2}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Checkpoint versus durable execution?","back":"A checkpoint lets a run resume. Durable execution adds retries, timers and deterministic replay, with side effects in activities [[r42]]."},{"front":"What does LangGraph's interrupt() require?","back":"A checkpointer and a thread_id. It waits indefinitely until resumed with Command. InMemorySaver loses state on restart [[r44]][[r43]]."},{"front":"Why do durable human-in-the-loop waits cost little?","back":"They consume no compute and no model tokens while waiting. A 24-hour approval costs seconds of execution on Flex Consumption [[r12]]."}],"related":["w10-l6","w10-t22","w10-t10","w10-t18"],"refs":["r21","r43","r44","r42","r12","r2","r15","r25","r26","r27"]},{"id":"w10-t22","order":22,"group":"Long-running agents","title":"Human-in-the-loop patterns and idempotency","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Put a person in the loop where an action is costly or irreversible, and make every side effect safe to retry with idempotency keys or compensating steps. Exactly-once is a design goal built from at-least-once delivery plus idempotent handlers.","useIf":"An agent can spend money, change data, message people or call tools whose effects cannot be undone, and runs can be retried or resumed.","avoidIf":"Do not gate every harmless read behind approval. Approval fatigue turns the gate into a rubber stamp.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"OWASP's Excessive Agency entry names three root causes: excessive functionality, excessive permissions and excessive autonomy. One concrete weakness is an extension that fails to independently verify and approve high-impact actions, such as a deletion that needs no confirmation from the user [[tr10]]. A human approval gate is the standard control. In Agent Framework, a function wrapped in `ApprovalRequiredAIFunction` makes the agent run complete with a response that says what input is needed, and the caller returns the decision in a new run [[tr13]]. Workflows use a `RequestPort` and `RequestInfoEvent` in C#, or `ctx.request_info()` in Python, and route the response back to the requesting executor [[tr12]]. LangGraph uses `interrupt()` [[r44]], and Magentic offers plan sign-off [[r10]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w10-t22-d1","title":"Approval gate and idempotent execution","alt":"The agent proposes an action. The gate asks a human to approve the exact arguments. After approval the tool runs once with an idempotency key, and a retry returns the saved result.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant A as Agent\n  participant G as Gate\n  participant H as Human\n  participant T as Tool\n  A->>G: Propose action and args\n  G->>H: Ask approval for exact args\n  H-->>G: Approve\n  G->>T: Execute with idempotency key\n  T-->>A: Result\n  A->>T: Retry, same key\n  T-->>A: Saved result, no second effect"},{"t":"p","md":"Idempotency makes retries safe. Stripe's design: the client generates the key (up to 255 characters), a retry with the same key returns the saved response, and v1 keys can be pruned after at least 24 hours [[r26]]. The Compensating Transaction pattern covers what retries cannot: when a later step of an eventually consistent operation fails, undo the completed steps [[r27]]. Use both for agent tool calls with side effects. The key must be stable across retries, for example derived from run id and step id, not a fresh random value per attempt."},{"t":"table","caption":"Where to put the human","head":["Pattern","Gate placed","Best for"],"rows":[["Approve each call","Before each risky tool call","Rare, high-impact actions"],["Risk-tiered approval","Only above a risk threshold","Mixed traffic with many safe actions"],["Plan approval","Once, before any step runs","Multi-step tasks with reviewable plans"],["Audit and sample review","After the fact, on a sample","Reversible, high-volume actions"],["Escalate when stuck","When brakes fire or confidence is low","Unattended runs"]],"compact":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Bind approval to the exact arguments","md":"If the agent can change the arguments after the human approved, the approval covers nothing. Show the final arguments, approve a hash of them, set an expiry, and reject execution if they differ."}],"whereToUse":["Payments, refunds, deletions, deployments and outbound messages","Long-running workflows that wait hours or days for a reviewer","Tools that call third-party APIs that support idempotency keys","Multi-step operations that need compensation if a later step fails","Regulated flows that need a record of who approved what"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Actions are costly, irreversible or externally visible","Runs can retry, resume or replay","A reviewer can judge the action from what the gate shows"],"avoid":["Approving every read-only call, which causes fatigue","Using approval as a substitute for least-privilege tool permissions","Random idempotency keys that change on every retry"],"flips":["Approval volume overwhelms reviewers: tier by risk and batch low-risk approvals","Actions are reversible and high volume: sample audits and compensations instead of gates","Reviewers lack context: show intent, arguments, evidence and consequences"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Controls compared","head":["Control","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Human approval gate","High-impact, irreversible actions","High volume, low risk","Latency, fatigue [[tr10]]"],["Idempotency keys","Retries and resumes may repeat a call","The target system ignores keys","Key storage and scope rules [[r26]]"],["Compensating steps","Multi-step operation can partly fail","Effects cannot be undone","Design of undo logic [[r27]]"],["Least-privilege tools","Always","Never fully replaced by approval","Design effort up front [[tr10]]"],["Post-hoc audit","Reversible, high volume","Damage is immediate","Detects, does not prevent"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Reviewers approve everything without reading","cause":"Too many low-risk prompts cause approval fatigue","fix":"Gate only above a risk threshold, show a clear summary and measure the rejection rate."},{"symptom":"A retry creates a second charge or message","cause":"No idempotency key, or a new key was generated for each attempt [[r26]]","fix":"Derive the key from run and step ids, and have the tool or its API store results by key."},{"symptom":"A partly finished operation leaves inconsistent state","cause":"A later step failed and nothing undid earlier steps [[r27]]","fix":"Define a compensating action per step and run them in reverse order on failure."},{"symptom":"The approved action differs from the executed one","cause":"Arguments changed between approval and execution","fix":"Approve a hash of the final arguments, set an expiry and verify before execution."}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w10-t22-s1","title":"Irreversible payment action","situation":"An agent can issue refunds through a payment API. A refund cannot be recalled, runs can be retried after crashes, and finance wants to know who approved each one.","constraints":["Irreversible side effect","Retries and resumes possible","Approver identity must be recorded","Low volume"],"unknowns":["Does the payment API support idempotency keys?","How long may an approval wait?"],"question":"What do you implement?","options":[{"name":"Approval on the exact arguments plus a stable idempotency key per run and step, stored with the approver identity","verdict":"best","why":"The gate stops unwanted refunds, the key stops duplicates on retry and the record meets the audit need.","tradeoff":"Latency while waiting for a person, and key handling per API."},{"name":"Autonomous refunds with logging only","verdict":"poor","why":"Logs cannot undo a refund, and a retry could pay twice.","tradeoff":"Right for small reversible credits under a hard cap."},{"name":"Approval only, no idempotency key","verdict":"good","why":"Blocks unwanted refunds.","tradeoff":"A crash after approval can lead to a duplicate on resume."}],"flips":[{"if":"Refunds under a small threshold are routine","then":"Auto-approve under the threshold, gate above it and audit samples."},{"if":"The API has no idempotency support","then":"Keep your own ledger of executed step ids and check it before calling."}],"takeaway":"Gate the decision, and make the execution safe to repeat."},{"id":"w10-t22-s2","title":"High volume, mostly harmless","situation":"A workflow agent performs thousands of actions per day: most are reversible tagging and labelling, a few delete records. Reviewers already complain about approval prompts.","constraints":["High volume","Most actions reversible","A few destructive actions","Reviewer capacity is limited"],"unknowns":["What is the cost of a wrong delete?","Can deletes be soft?"],"question":"How do you place the human?","options":[{"name":"Risk-tiered gates: approve deletes, auto-approve reversible actions with logging and sample audits, and make deletes soft where possible","verdict":"best","why":"It focuses human attention where damage is real and keeps throughput for the rest.","tradeoff":"Tiering rules need tuning, and sample audits detect rather than prevent."},{"name":"Approve every action","verdict":"poor","why":"Fatigue turns approvals into rubber stamps while throughput collapses.","tradeoff":"Right while the agent is new and volume is low."},{"name":"No approvals, rely on compensating undo","verdict":"good","why":"Fast and fits reversible actions.","tradeoff":"Hard deletes cannot be undone, so they still need a gate or a soft-delete design."}],"flips":[{"if":"Deletes can be soft with a retention window","then":"Compensate by restoring and reduce gating."},{"if":"Rejection rate is near zero","then":"Raise the threshold and sample more."}],"takeaway":"Spend human attention on irreversibility, not volume."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w10-t22-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A tool wrapper creates a new random idempotency key for every attempt of the same logical charge. What is the result?","options":["A retry after a timeout can create a second charge","Retries are always safe","Keys are pruned too early","The first request is rejected"],"answer":0,"explain":"Idempotency works only when retries reuse the same key, so the service returns the saved response. A fresh key each attempt makes every retry look like a new request. Derive the key from run and step ids. [[r26]]","ref":"r26","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t22-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which statement about exactly-once execution is the most accurate?","options":["A checkpoint on its own guarantees exactly-once","You can get the effect through at-least-once delivery plus idempotent handlers, or compensations","Approval gates make duplicates impossible","Exactly-once is automatic in all durable runtimes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Resuming or retrying can repeat a step, so safety comes from idempotency keys and compensations on top of checkpoints. Approval gates control whether an action runs, not how many times. [[r26]][[r27]]","ref":"r27","difficulty":2},{"id":"w10-t22-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which are root causes of Excessive Agency in OWASP's description?","options":["Excessive functionality","Excessive permissions","Excessive autonomy","Excessive logging"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"OWASP lists excessive functionality, permissions and autonomy as the typical root causes. Logging is not among them. Approval gates address autonomy and high-impact actions. [[tr10]]","ref":"tr10","difficulty":1}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does an Agent Framework tool approval work?","back":"Wrap the function in ApprovalRequiredAIFunction. The run completes with a response saying what input is needed, and the caller returns the decision in a new run [[tr13]]."},{"front":"Stripe idempotency key rules?","back":"Client-generated, up to 255 characters, a retry returns the saved response, and v1 keys can be pruned after at least 24 hours [[r26]]."},{"front":"What does the Compensating Transaction pattern do?","back":"When a later step of an eventually consistent operation fails, it undoes the steps already completed [[r27]]."}],"related":["w10-l6","w10-t21","w10-t20","w10-t08"],"refs":["tr10","tr13","tr12","r44","r10","r26","r27"]}]});
